window.SPCOBOL_INDEX=[{"t":"SP COBOL Language Reference","u":"index.html","s":"Every statement and data clause SP COBOL supports, explained for people learning COBOL, each with an example you can run.","k":"home overview contents start","c":"","x":"SP COBOL Language Reference Every statement and data clause SP COBOL supports, in plain English, each with a short program you can compile and run. Find a statement, clause or topic SP COBOL turns standard COBOL into an ordinary .NET program. Its front end is GnuCOBOL 3.2\u0027s, so the language is GnuCOBOL\u0027s default dialect (close to COBOL 2014), and a test suite checks that its programs print the same output and write the same data files as GnuCOBOL 3.2 does. A few things work differently on purpose, to help people learning COBOL; the pages mark them, and Differences from GnuCOBOL lists them all. What isn\u0027t supported at all, mostly because GnuCOBOL 3.2 doesn\u0027t support it either, is on Not supported . New to COBOL? Start with Reading this reference , then Source format and Program structure . After that, the pages for DISPLAY , MOVE , PICTURE , IF and PERFORM cover most of what a first program needs. Every example on these pages was compiled with SP COBOL and run, and the output shown under it is what it printed. Click an example\u0027s file name to download it."},{"t":"Reading this reference","u":"reading-this-reference.html","s":"How the syntax boxes, the examples and the common COBOL terms on these pages work.","k":"notation syntax brackets braces ellipsis identifier literal imperative statement data item group item elementary item how to read conventions","c":"Getting started","x":"The syntax boxes Each statement and clause page starts with a box that shows every way you can write it. The box is a pattern, not a program you can copy as it is. Read it like this: In the box Means ADD , TO , GIVING UPPERCASE words are COBOL words. Type them as shown (COBOL doesn\u0027t care about upper or lower case, but most programs use upper case). identifier-1 , literal-2 Lowercase words in italics are the parts you supply: the name of one of your data items, a number, a quoted string. The digit only tells several of them apart. [ ROUNDED ] Square brackets: optional. You may leave it out. { CORRESPONDING | CORR } Braces with bars: choose exactly one of the choices. ... The part just before it may be repeated, as many times as you need. Format 1: ... A label. When a statement has several forms, each one gets its own pattern under its own label. For example, the first form of ADD is shown as ADD { identifier-1 | literal-1 } ... TO { identifier-2 [ ROUNDED ] } ... which allows ADD 1 TO COUNTER , ADD PRICE TAX TO TOTAL and ADD 5 TO A B ROUNDED . Words that are optional \"noise\", such as IS in IF A IS NUMERIC or ON in ON SIZE ERROR , are shown in brackets too. They don\u0027t change what the program does; put them in when they make it read better. Words you will meet Term Meaning data item A named piece of storage that your program describes in the DATA DIVISION, such as 01 TOTAL PIC 9(5) . Other languages call it a variable. elementary item A data item that is not divided any further. It has a PICTURE clause (or a USAGE that implies a size). group item A data item made of smaller items under it, with higher level numbers. A group has no PICTURE of its own; it is simply all of its parts side by side, and COBOL treats it as one alphanumeric field. See DATA DIVISION and level numbers . identifier A way of naming data in a statement: a data name, possibly with qualification, subscripts or reference modification, such as CITY OF HOME-ADDRESS or MONTH-NAME (3) . See referring to data . literal A value written straight into the program: 42 , -3.5 , \"HELLO\" . See literals . figurative constant A reserved word that stands for a value, like ZERO or SPACES . See figurative constants . imperative statement A statement that just does something, with no condition of its own: MOVE , DISPLAY , ADD without SIZE ERROR, and so on, or a statement closed by its scope terminator ( END-IF , END-ADD , ...). Phrases like ON SIZE ERROR and AT END take imperative statements. condition Something that is true or false, such as A \u003e B or CUST-NAME = SPACES . See conditions . arithmetic expression A formula such as PRICE * 1.08 + 2 . See arithmetic expressions . paragraph, section Named blocks of code in the PROCEDURE DIVISION. See program structure . The examples Every example is a complete program that you can type in and run. Under each one is exactly what it printed when SP COBOL ran it, and stock GnuCOBOL 3.2 prints the same thing (where it doesn\u0027t, the page says so). Examples are written in fixed format, with sequence numbers in columns 1-6, as described in source format . Here is a small one. It describes a group item made of three elementary items, shows the whole group, then adds to a counter. Example GREETING is a group item, so DISPLAY shows its three parts side by side: \"HELLO\", the one space in the FILLER, and \"WORLD\". TIMES-SHOWN is a one-digit number that starts at 0; after the ADD it holds 1. Callouts A note adds something useful to know. A warning marks a trap for learners, or a place where SP COBOL behaves differently from GnuCOBOL 3.2. This box marks something SP COBOL does not support (yet), and says what to do instead. See also Source format for where things go on a line, program structure for how a program is put together, differences from GnuCOBOL , not supported ."},{"t":"Source format","u":"source-format.html","s":"Where things go on a line of fixed-format COBOL: sequence numbers, the indicator column, areas A and B.","k":"fixed format columns column 7 indicator comment asterisk slash page continuation hyphen debugging line D area A area B column 72 73-80 sequence number 000010 floating comment *\u003e free format CobolSourceFormat","c":"Getting started","x":"Syntax Columns 1-6 7 8-11 12-72 73-80 000010 indicator area A area B ignored Indicator (column 7): space an ordinary line * comment line / comment line that starts a new page in a printed listing - continuation of the line above D debugging line (compiled only WITH DEBUGGING MODE) Floating comment, anywhere from column 7 on: *\u003e comment-text What it does COBOL was written on 80-column punched cards, and fixed-format source still keeps their layout. Each column range of a line has a job: Columns Name What goes there 1-6 sequence number area A line number: 000010, 000020, ... 7 indicator area Blank, or one of * / - D (below). 8-11 area A Things that begin a new part of the program: division, section and paragraph names, FD / SD , level numbers 01 and 77, END PROGRAM , DECLARATIVES . 12-72 area B Everything else: statements, the level numbers 02-49, 66 and 88, clauses that continue a line. 73-80 identification area Ignored by the compiler. On cards it held the program\u0027s name. In practice: start division, section and paragraph names and 01 levels in column 8, and start statements in column 12. Anything you type after column 72 is not part of your program, which can cause confusing errors when a long line gets cut off. Keep lines short and continue statements on the next line instead; a statement can be split between any two words. Sequence numbers Columns 1-6 hold a line number. SP COBOL\u0027s convention is to number from 000010 in steps of 10 (000010, 000020, 000030, ...), the way COBOL is usually written, and every example on this site follows it. The compiler ignores these columns completely: a gap, a duplicate or a line out of order is never an error. The COBOL editor for Visual Studio keeps the numbers for you: pressing Enter numbers the new line and moves every line below it up by 10, and Edit \u003e Renumber COBOL Lines fixes a whole file. Outside the editor, tools\\renumber.ps1 FILE.cbl does the same. Comments A * in column 7 makes the whole line a comment. A / in column 7 is also a comment, and asks for a new page when the compiler prints a listing. The pair *\u003e starts a floating comment : it can begin in any column from 7 on, including after code on the same line, and runs to the end of the line. Continuing a long literal A statement can be split between words anywhere, but a quoted literal that is too long for one line needs a continuation line . Let the literal run up to column 72 without a closing quote. On the next line put - in column 7, then in area B a quote followed by the rest of the literal. Every character up to column 72 of the first line is part of the literal, spaces included, so count carefully. (An easier way to build a long value is to join shorter literals with \u0026 ; see literals .) Debugging lines A D in column 7 marks a debugging line. It is treated as a comment unless the SOURCE-COMPUTER paragraph says WITH DEBUGGING MODE ; then it is compiled like any other line. This lets you leave trace DISPLAYs in a program and switch them all on or off in one place. Example The comment lines and the *\u003e comments are skipped. The MOVE\u0027s literal runs to column 72 (\"...CARRIES ON\"); the continuation line picks it up at the quote, so the whole sentence lands in MESSAGE-TEXT. The D line is not compiled, and the word IGNORED! in columns 73-80 of the ADD line is not seen at all, so TOTAL is 5. Example: switching debugging lines on With WITH DEBUGGING MODE in SOURCE-COMPUTER, the D line is compiled and its DISPLAY runs. SP COBOL notes Fixed format is the default, and this reference uses it throughout. GnuCOBOL\u0027s free format (no column rules; comments start with *\u003e ) is also available: set the project property \u003cCobolSourceFormat\u003efree\u003c/CobolSourceFormat\u003e in your .csproj (see BUILD.md). When the compiler complains about a wrong character in column 7 or text past column 72, the Visual Studio build adds a plain-English hint about the column rules to the error. See also Program structure , literals , debugging , COPY (copybooks follow the same format)."},{"t":"Program structure","u":"program-structure.html","s":"How a COBOL program is built from divisions, sections, paragraphs, sentences and statements.","k":"divisions sections paragraphs sentences statements period full stop scope terminator END-IF END-PERFORM END PROGRAM nested program several programs one file structure outline","c":"Getting started","x":"Syntax IDENTIFICATION DIVISION. PROGRAM-ID. program-name. [ ENVIRONMENT DIVISION. environment-entries ] [ DATA DIVISION. data-entries ] [ PROCEDURE DIVISION [ USING data-name-1 ... ]. [ section-name SECTION. ] paragraph-name. statement-1 [ statement-2 ] ... . ... ] [ nested-program ... ] [ END PROGRAM program-name. ] What it does A COBOL program is a nest of named parts, from large to small: Part What it is division The four main parts, always in this order: IDENTIFICATION (the program\u0027s name), ENVIRONMENT (the computer and the files), DATA (every data item) and PROCEDURE (the code). Only the IDENTIFICATION DIVISION is required; leave out any other division you don\u0027t need. section A named group inside a division. The ENVIRONMENT and DATA divisions have fixed sections (WORKING-STORAGE SECTION, ...). In the PROCEDURE DIVISION you name your own sections, or use none. paragraph In the PROCEDURE DIVISION, a name followed by a period, starting in area A, and the code after it up to the next paragraph or section name. Paragraphs are what PERFORM and GO TO refer to. sentence One or more statements ended by a period. statement One action, starting with a COBOL verb: MOVE , ADD , IF , ... The period In the PROCEDURE DIVISION, a period ends a sentence, and it also ends every statement that is still open, however deeply nested. That is the classic COBOL trap: a period inside an IF ends the IF there, and the lines after it run whether the condition was true or not. The safe modern style is to write one period at the end of each paragraph and none inside it, closing each statement with its scope terminator instead. Outside the PROCEDURE DIVISION, periods end each header and each entry ( 01 TOTAL PIC 9(5). ) and are required. Scope terminators Statements that contain other statements, or that have phrases like ON SIZE ERROR or AT END , can be closed with a matching END- word: END-IF , END-PERFORM , END-EVALUATE , END-READ , END-ADD , END-COMPUTE , END-STRING , END-CALL and so on. The terminator shows exactly where the statement stops, so you can nest one IF inside another without periods. Several programs in one file A source file may hold more than one program. Each one starts with its own IDENTIFICATION DIVISION and ends with END PROGRAM name. A program placed before its outer program\u0027s END PROGRAM is a nested program: it belongs to the outer program and is called with CALL . A program placed after another\u0027s END PROGRAM is a separate program that happens to share the file. When a file has only one program, END PROGRAM is optional. Example: the parts of a program The PROCEDURE DIVISION has two sections. In MAIN-LOGIC, the first IF is closed by END-IF, so \"AFTER END-IF\" is shown no matter what. The second IF is ended by the period after DISPLAY \"NOT SHOWN\" , so the next sentence runs as usual. PERFORM HELPERS runs the whole HELPERS section: both its paragraphs, so COUNT-UP adds 1 a fourth time and SAY-DONE speaks. STOP RUN ends the program before control could fall into HELPERS on its own. Example: programs in one file PROGSTR2IN sits inside PROGSTR2 (before END PROGRAM PROGSTR2 ), so it is a nested program. PROGSTR2NEXT comes after PROGSTR2\u0027s END PROGRAM, so it is a separate program in the same file. Both are reached with CALL and come back with GOBACK. See also Source format , PROCEDURE DIVISION for how control moves between paragraphs, CALL , PERFORM , IF , IDENTIFICATION DIVISION for COMMON and INITIAL programs."},{"t":"IDENTIFICATION DIVISION","u":"identification-division.html","s":"Name the program, and optionally mark it COMMON, INITIAL or RECURSIVE.","k":"PROGRAM-ID AUTHOR INSTALLATION DATE-WRITTEN DATE-COMPILED SECURITY COMMON INITIAL RECURSIVE program name","c":"Divisions","x":"Syntax IDENTIFICATION DIVISION. PROGRAM-ID. program-name [ IS { COMMON | INITIAL | RECURSIVE } ]. [ AUTHOR. comment-entry ] [ INSTALLATION. comment-entry ] [ DATE-WRITTEN. comment-entry ] [ DATE-COMPILED. comment-entry ] [ SECURITY. comment-entry ] What it does The IDENTIFICATION DIVISION comes first in every program. Its one required entry is PROGRAM-ID , which gives the program its name. The name is what other programs use in CALL , and what END PROGRAM repeats. Use letters, digits and hyphens. Comment paragraphs AUTHOR , INSTALLATION , DATE-WRITTEN , DATE-COMPILED and SECURITY are left over from early COBOL. The compiler reads the text after each one as a comment, up to the next paragraph, so they change nothing about the program. Many shops still fill them in as a standard header. Today a block of * comment lines does the same job. COMMON Normally a nested program can be called only by the program that directly contains it. IS COMMON lets its sibling nested programs (the others inside the same outer program) call it too. That makes it a good place for a helper every part of the outer program uses. INITIAL A called program normally keeps its WORKING-STORAGE from one CALL to the next: a counter that was 2 when the program last returned is still 2 next time. IS INITIAL makes the program start fresh each time it is called, with every item back at its VALUE. RECURSIVE IS RECURSIVE allows a program to CALL itself, directly or through another program. Without it, a program that is already running may not be called again until it returns. Each active call of a recursive program needs its own copy of its working items, so put them in the LOCAL-STORAGE SECTION (see DATA DIVISION ), which is set up fresh on every call; WORKING-STORAGE is shared by all the calls. A program with LOCAL-STORAGE can\u0027t be nested inside another, so a recursive program is usually a separate program, as in the third example. Example: the comment paragraphs The five paragraphs after PROGRAM-ID are only comments; the program just runs its one DISPLAY. Example: COMMON and INITIAL IDDIV2KEEP is an ordinary program, so CALLS-SEEN keeps counting: 1, 2, 3. IDDIV2INIT is INITIAL, so its CALLS-SEEN goes back to 0 on every CALL and always shows 1. IDDIV2SHOW is COMMON, which is why IDDIV2KEEP, its sibling, may call it. Example: a RECURSIVE program IDDIV3FACT works out a factorial: N! is N times (N - 1)!, and 1! is 1. For any N above 1 it CALLs itself with N - 1, then multiplies the answer it gets back by N. SMALLER and SUB-ANSWER are in LOCAL-STORAGE, so each level of the call has its own pair, and a deeper call can\u0027t overwrite the values of the call waiting for it. IDDIV3FACT follows END PROGRAM IDDIV3 in the same file, so it is a separate program, not a nested one. See also Program structure , CALL , CANCEL (which also resets a called program), ENVIRONMENT DIVISION ."},{"t":"ENVIRONMENT DIVISION","u":"environment-division.html","s":"Describe the computer, special names, and the files a program uses.","k":"CONFIGURATION SECTION SOURCE-COMPUTER OBJECT-COMPUTER SPECIAL-NAMES CRT STATUS CURSOR CURRENCY SIGN DECIMAL-POINT IS COMMA CLASS ALPHABET SYMBOLIC CHARACTERS PROGRAM COLLATING SEQUENCE INPUT-OUTPUT SECTION FILE-CONTROL SELECT ASSIGN WITH DEBUGGING MODE","c":"Divisions","x":"Syntax ENVIRONMENT DIVISION. [ CONFIGURATION SECTION. [ SOURCE-COMPUTER. computer-name [ WITH DEBUGGING MODE ]. ] [ OBJECT-COMPUTER. computer-name [ PROGRAM COLLATING SEQUENCE IS alphabet-name ]. ] [ SPECIAL-NAMES. [ CRT STATUS IS data-name-1 ] [ CURSOR IS data-name-2 ] [ CURRENCY SIGN IS literal-1 ] [ DECIMAL-POINT IS COMMA ] [ CLASS class-name IS { literal-2 [ THRU literal-3 ] } ... ] [ ALPHABET alphabet-name IS { NATIVE | STANDARD-1 | EBCDIC | { literal-4 [ THRU literal-5 ] } ... } ] [ SYMBOLIC CHARACTERS { symbol-name ... { IS | ARE } integer ... } ... ] . ] ] [ INPUT-OUTPUT SECTION. FILE-CONTROL. SELECT file-name ASSIGN TO { literal-6 | data-name-3 } [ ORGANIZATION IS { LINE SEQUENTIAL | SEQUENTIAL | RELATIVE | INDEXED } ] [ other-file-clauses ] . ... ] What it does The ENVIRONMENT DIVISION holds the parts of a program that depend on the machine it runs on and the files it uses. A program that uses no files and no special names can leave the whole division out. CONFIGURATION SECTION SOURCE-COMPUTER and OBJECT-COMPUTER name the computer the program is compiled on and runs on. The names are documentation only. One phrase matters: SOURCE-COMPUTER. name WITH DEBUGGING MODE. turns on the debugging lines ( D in column 7, see source format ). PROGRAM COLLATING SEQUENCE in OBJECT-COMPUTER names an ALPHABET that sets the order used when the program compares text. SPECIAL-NAMES gives names to things the program needs: Clause What it does CRT STATUS IS item After an ACCEPT of a screen, item (a PIC 9(4) field) holds the key that ended the input: 0 for Enter, 1001 for F1, 2005 for Esc, and so on. See SCREEN SECTION . CURSOR IS item Names a field holding a screen position (line and column, as LLCC in a PIC 9(4) ). Before an ACCEPT of a screen, it says which field the input starts in; afterwards it holds where the cursor was. See the last example. CURRENCY SIGN IS \"c\" Uses a different character instead of $ as the currency symbol in PICTURE strings. It can\u0027t be a letter that is already a PICTURE symbol (such as E or L ). DECIMAL-POINT IS COMMA Swaps the roles of period and comma, the European way: 1234,50 is a number with two decimals, and PICTURE Z.ZZ9,99 prints 1.234,50 . CLASS name IS ... Defines your own class condition: a list of the characters allowed, such as \"0\" THRU \"9\" \"A\" THRU \"F\" . Then IF field IS name is true when every character of the field is in the list. ALPHABET name IS ... Names a character order: NATIVE (the machine\u0027s own), STANDARD-1 (ASCII), EBCDIC (the IBM mainframe order) or a list of literals in the order you want. It is used by PROGRAM COLLATING SEQUENCE and the COLLATING SEQUENCE phrase of SORT . In a list, the characters you name come first, in your order, and every other character follows in the usual order. SYMBOLIC CHARACTERS name IS n Gives a name to a single character by its position in the character set. Positions count from 1, so the character with code 42 ( * ) is position 43. The name can then be used like a one-character literal. The clauses of SPECIAL-NAMES are written one after another, and a single period ends the whole paragraph. INPUT-OUTPUT SECTION The FILE-CONTROL paragraph has one SELECT entry for each file the program uses. SELECT gives the file the name the program uses for it, ASSIGN TO says which file on disk it is, and ORGANIZATION says how its records are laid out. Each file also needs an FD entry in the FILE SECTION of the DATA DIVISION . The details (ACCESS MODE, RECORD KEY, FILE STATUS, ...) are on the files page. Example: SYMBOLIC CHARACTERS Position 43 is * (code 42) and position 46 is - (code 45). STAR and DASH can be used anywhere a one-character literal could. Example: FILE-CONTROL The SELECT connects the name NAME-FILE to the disk file NAMES.TXT, a line sequential (plain text) file. The program writes two lines, then reads the first one back. Example: CRT STATUS The keystrokes typed \"ADA\", Tab, \"LOVELACE\" and then F1. F1 ended the ACCEPT, so KEY-CODE holds 1001. Example: CLASS and ALPHABET HEX-DIGIT is a class of the characters 0 to 9 and A to F : 1F3A is all hex digits, 1G3A isn\u0027t. The alphabet VOWELS-FIRST puts A, E, I, O and U ahead of every other character, and PROGRAM COLLATING SEQUENCE makes the program compare text in that order. So EGG comes before BOX, and even the literal \"U\" is less than \"B\" . Example: DECIMAL-POINT IS COMMA and CURRENCY SIGN With DECIMAL-POINT IS COMMA, the VALUE 1234,5 means 1234.50 and the edited pictures use . to group thousands and , for the decimals. CURRENCY SIGN makes # the currency symbol: ###.##9,99 floats it next to the first digit, and #**.**9,99 keeps it in the first column with check-protection stars after it. The doubled amount shows the arithmetic is unchanged. Example: CURSOR IS CURSOR-POS starts as 0310, line 3 column 10, which is where the LAST field begins. So the ACCEPT starts in LAST instead of FIRST, and the keystrokes \"HOPPER\" and Enter fill LAST-NAME. Compare the CRT STATUS example, where input starts in the first field. SP COBOL notes After the ACCEPT, SP COBOL puts the start of the field the cursor was in into the CURSOR item; GnuCOBOL gives the exact column within that field. See also Files , SCREEN SECTION , conditions (class conditions), SORT , DATA DIVISION ."},{"t":"DATA DIVISION","u":"data-division.html","s":"Describe every piece of data the program uses, section by section.","k":"FILE SECTION WORKING-STORAGE SECTION LOCAL-STORAGE SECTION LINKAGE SECTION REPORT SECTION SCREEN SECTION FD SD group item elementary item level number 01 77 FILLER variables declare","c":"Divisions","x":"Syntax DATA DIVISION. [ FILE SECTION. { FD | SD } file-name [ file-clauses ] . record-description ... ] [ WORKING-STORAGE SECTION. data-description ... ] [ LOCAL-STORAGE SECTION. data-description ... ] [ LINKAGE SECTION. data-description ... ] [ REPORT SECTION. report-description ... ] [ SCREEN SECTION. screen-description ... ] data-description: level-number { data-name | FILLER } [ clauses ] . What it does Every data item a program uses is described in the DATA DIVISION before the code runs: its name, its size and what kind of data it holds. COBOL has no \"declare as you go\"; if it isn\u0027t here, the PROCEDURE DIVISION can\u0027t use it. The sections must appear in the order above, and you include only the ones you need. Section What it holds FILE SECTION One FD (file description) for each file named in a SELECT, followed by its record layout: the area a READ fills and a WRITE takes from. SD describes a sort work file. See files and SORT . WORKING-STORAGE SECTION The program\u0027s own data. It is set up once, when the program first runs, and keeps its values for as long as the program is loaded, including between CALLs of a subprogram. LOCAL-STORAGE SECTION Like WORKING-STORAGE, but set up fresh (with its VALUE clauses) every time the program is called, and gone when it returns. See the second example. LINKAGE SECTION Describes data that belongs to the calling program, passed in through PROCEDURE DIVISION USING . No storage is set aside here; the names are laid over the caller\u0027s items. See CALL . REPORT SECTION Report layouts for the Report Writer. See Report Writer . SCREEN SECTION Screen layouts for ACCEPT and DISPLAY of whole screens. See SCREEN SECTION . Data descriptions and level numbers Each entry starts with a level number, then the item\u0027s name (or FILLER for a part you never refer to by name), then clauses such as PICTURE , VALUE , USAGE and OCCURS , and ends with a period. 01 starts a record: a new item at the top level, in area A. 02 to 49 are parts of the item above them with a smaller number. Most programs use 05, 10, 15, ... so there is room to add levels later. 77 is a stand-alone elementary item that can\u0027t have parts. 88 names a condition on the item above it; 66 renames part of a record. See condition names and RENAMES . The full rules are on the level numbers page. Group and elementary items An item with parts under it is a group item ; an item with no parts is an elementary item . Only elementary items have a PICTURE. A group is simply its parts laid end to end, and when you use the group name you get all of them as one alphanumeric field, whatever their own pictures say. MOVE to a group fills the whole group as text. Example CUSTOMER is a group of two parts: CUST-NAME (itself a group of FIRST-NAME and LAST-NAME) and CUST-BALANCE. Displaying CUSTOMER shows all 20 bytes in a row, with the balance as its six digits and no decimal point, since the group is just text. MOVE SPACES to CUST-NAME clears both names but leaves the balance alone. VISITS is a level 77 item. Example: LOCAL-STORAGE and WORKING-STORAGE The main program CALLs the subprogram COUNTER (in DATADIV2.sub\\COUNTER.cbl ) twice. COUNTER adds 1 to a WORKING-STORAGE count and to a LOCAL-STORAGE count. The WORKING-STORAGE count keeps its value between the calls, so it reaches 2. The LOCAL-STORAGE items are set up again from their VALUE clauses on each call: the count is back to 0 before the ADD, and LS-NOTE says FRESH again even though the first call changed it to USED. LOCAL-STORAGE is what you want for a RECURSIVE program, where each active call needs its own copy. See also Level numbers , PICTURE , VALUE , USAGE , referring to data , PROCEDURE DIVISION ."},{"t":"PROCEDURE DIVISION","u":"procedure-division.html","s":"The division that holds the program\u0027s code, and how control moves through it.","k":"USING RETURNING DECLARATIVES END DECLARATIVES sections paragraphs fall through control flow STOP RUN GOBACK LINKAGE parameters","c":"Divisions","x":"Syntax PROCEDURE DIVISION [ USING { [ BY REFERENCE | BY VALUE ] data-name-1 } ... ] . [ DECLARATIVES. { section-name SECTION. USE-statement . [ paragraph-name. [ sentence ] ... ] ... } ... END DECLARATIVES. ] { [ section-name SECTION. ] { paragraph-name. [ sentence ] ... } ... } ... What it does The PROCEDURE DIVISION is where the program does its work. It is made of paragraphs, which may be grouped into sections (see program structure ). Statements before the first paragraph name are allowed too, as in many short examples on this site. How control flows The program starts at the first statement of the PROCEDURE DIVISION (after any DECLARATIVES) and runs downward. When it reaches the end of a paragraph it simply carries on into the next paragraph: a paragraph name is a label, not a wall. This is called falling through . The order changes only when a statement changes it: PERFORM runs a paragraph or section (or a range with THRU ) and comes back to the statement after the PERFORM. GO TO jumps to a paragraph and doesn\u0027t come back. STOP RUN ends the run; GOBACK returns to the caller (or ends the run in the main program). Running off the end of the last paragraph ends the program as GOBACK would. The usual pattern is a main paragraph at the top that PERFORMs the others and ends with STOP RUN or GOBACK, so control never falls into the paragraphs below by accident. USING: receiving parameters A subprogram lists the items it receives from its caller after USING . Each one must be described in the LINKAGE SECTION. They are matched to the caller\u0027s CALL ... USING list by position, not by name. BY REFERENCE (the default) means the subprogram works directly on the caller\u0027s item, so changes are seen by the caller. The details, including BY CONTENT and BY VALUE, are on the CALL page. RETURNING PROCEDURE DIVISION RETURNING is not supported, the same as in GnuCOBOL 3.2: the compiler warns \"program RETURNING is not implemented\". To pass a result back, set RETURN-CODE in the subprogram (the caller reads it after the CALL, or receives it with CALL ... RETURNING ), or change an item the caller passed BY REFERENCE . The second example below does both. DECLARATIVES If present, DECLARATIVES come first, between DECLARATIVES. and END DECLARATIVES. Each section in them starts with a USE statement saying when it runs, for example after an error on a file. These sections are not reached by falling through; the run time calls them when their event happens. Example: falling through FIRST-PARA performs THIRD-PARA, which runs and comes back. Then FIRST-PARA ends and control falls into SECOND-PARA, then into THIRD-PARA again. After the last paragraph there is nothing more, so the program ends. There is no STOP RUN anywhere. Example: USING and RETURN-CODE PROCDIV2SQ receives NUM and SQUARE by reference. It stores the square straight into the caller\u0027s SQUARE and reports success in RETURN-CODE. 200 squared (40000) doesn\u0027t fit in PIC 9(4) , so the SIZE ERROR phrase sets RETURN-CODE to 8. The main program clears RETURN-CODE before STOP RUN, since its value becomes the program\u0027s exit code. See also Program structure , PERFORM , CALL , USE , special registers (RETURN-CODE), DATA DIVISION (LINKAGE SECTION)."},{"t":"Level numbers","u":"level-numbers.html","s":"How the numbers in front of data names build records out of groups and elementary items.","k":"01 05 10 49 66 77 78 88 group item elementary item FILLER record structure hierarchy level number","c":"Data description","x":"Syntax level-number { data-name | FILLER } [ clauses ] . level-number is one of: 01-49 an item in a record (01 starts a new record) 66 another name for a range of items (RENAMES) 77 a stand-alone elementary item 78 a named constant 88 a condition-name (a name for some values) What it does Every line in the DATA DIVISION that describes data starts with a level number . The number tells COBOL how the item fits into the items around it. Groups and elementary items An elementary item is a single field: it has a PICTURE (or a USAGE such as COMP-1 that implies its size) and nothing below it. A group item has no PICTURE; it is made up of the items with higher level numbers that follow it. The group ends at the next item whose level number is the same as the group\u0027s or lower. 01 starts a record: the top of a structure, beginning in column 8. Items inside it use any numbers from 02 to 49 . The numbers don\u0027t have to go up by one; 05 , 10 , 15 is the usual style, because it leaves room to add a level later. What matters is that items at the same depth use the same number. 01 CUSTOMER. group (the whole record) 05 CUST-ID PIC 9(4). elementary 05 CUST-NAME. group inside the group 10 FIRST-NAME PIC X(6). 10 LAST-NAME PIC X(8). A group\u0027s size and type A group takes up exactly the bytes of the items inside it, one after another, with no gaps. Its size is the sum of their sizes. A group is always treated as alphanumeric ( PIC X ), whatever is inside it: when you MOVE to a group or DISPLAY it, COBOL copies the bytes as they are, without any number conversion. That is why MOVE \"GRACE HOPPER\" TO CUST-NAME in the example splits the text across FIRST-NAME and LAST-NAME by position. FILLER Write FILLER (or leave the name out) for an item you never need to refer to, such as a separator or spacing in a print line. It still takes up room and can have a VALUE . The special levels 77 : an elementary item that is not part of any record. It works like an 01 with a PICTURE; many programmers just use 01 . 66 : gives another name to a run of items in the record just above it. See RENAMES . 78 : a named constant. See Constants . 88 : a condition-name attached to the item above it, such as 88 PASSED VALUE 60 THRU 100 . See Condition-names . Example CUSTOMER is 4 + 1 + 14 + 5 = 24 bytes, and displaying it shows all of its items side by side, including the FILLER dash and BALANCE\u0027s digits without a decimal point (the point is only implied). CUST-NAME is 6 + 8 = 14 bytes. Moving 12 characters into CUST-NAME fills FIRST-NAME with the first 6 and LAST-NAME with the rest, padded with spaces. See also PICTURE , USAGE , VALUE , OCCURS , REDEFINES , DATA DIVISION , Referring to data ."},{"t":"PICTURE","u":"picture.html","s":"Describe what an elementary item holds and how big it is, symbol by symbol.","k":"PIC PICTURE 9 X A V S P Z * $ CR DB B 0 / edited numeric-edited floating insertion check protect currency sign digits size repetition 38 digits","c":"Data description","x":"Syntax { PICTURE | PIC } [ IS ] character-string What it does The PICTURE clause describes an elementary item: what kind of characters it holds and how many. Each symbol in the character string stands for one position (one character of the field), except V , S and P , which take no room in a normal DISPLAY field. A number in parentheses repeats the symbol before it, so 9(5) is the same as 99999 and X(30) is thirty X s. A PICTURE puts an item in one of these categories: numeric (only 9 V S P ): a number you can calculate with; alphanumeric ( X , or a mix of X 9 A ): any characters; alphabetic (only A ): letters and spaces; numeric-edited ( 9 plus editing symbols such as Z , . $ - CR ): a number laid out for people to read, used as the receiving field of a MOVE or a GIVING ; national ( N ): Unicode characters, see National data . Data symbols Symbol Meaning Example 9 One decimal digit. PIC 9(5) holds 0 to 99999. X Any character. PIC X(20) holds 20 characters. A A letter or a space. PIC A(10) V Where the decimal point is. It is only implied : no \".\" is stored and it takes no room. PIC 9(3)V99 holds 123.45 in 5 bytes. S The number has a sign. Must come first. Without it, a negative result loses its minus sign. PIC S9(4) P A scaling position: an assumed zero that is not stored. PIC 9(3)PPP holds 45000 in 3 bytes (see the third example). N One national (UTF-16) character, 2 bytes. PIC N(10) How many digits A numeric item can have up to 38 digits in SP COBOL, as in GnuCOBOL 3.2. The last lines of the first example show a PIC S9(38) item holding a 38-digit number and doing arithmetic on it. Numeric-edited symbols These make a number readable. You don\u0027t calculate with an edited field; you MOVE a number into it (or name it in GIVING ) and then DISPLAY or WRITE it. Symbol What it does Z A digit, but a leading zero becomes a space. * A digit, but a leading zero becomes * . This is check protection : nobody can write extra digits in front of the amount on a check. . The actual decimal point, printed. Lines up with the number\u0027s decimal point. , A comma, printed. It becomes a space when it falls among suppressed leading zeros. $ The currency sign. One $ is printed in a fixed place; several ( $$$ ) float : the sign is printed just left of the first digit shown. + - A sign. + prints + or -; - prints - for negative numbers and a space otherwise. One at the start or end is fixed; several at the start ( ----9 ) float like $$$ . CR DB At the right end: printed when the number is negative, spaces when it isn\u0027t. Accountants\u0027 credit and debit. B Inserts a space. 0 Inserts a zero. / Inserts a slash. A numeric-edited picture may also use 9 for a digit that is always shown. If every digit position is Z (or * ) and the value is zero, the whole field is spaces (or stars), decimal point included. Alphanumeric-edited A picture of X or A mixed with B , 0 or / is alphanumeric-edited : a MOVE into it inserts those characters between the moved characters, for example PIC XXXX/XX/XX turns 20261125 into 2026/11/25 . The moved text fills the X and A positions from the left; the inserted characters take no characters from it. The third example shows it. Example: data symbols A DISPLAY shows a numeric item\u0027s digits with its implied decimal point as a real . , and a signed item with a leading - or + . Moving -12 into the unsigned 9(3) drops the sign. A(5) and X(5) pad short values with spaces on the right. The 38-digit item keeps every digit through the subtraction. Example: edited pictures Each number is moved into ten different edited fields. Look at how the zeros in front are replaced: by spaces with Z , by stars with * . The floating $$$,$$9.99 puts the dollar sign right next to the first digit, while $ZZ,ZZ9.99CR keeps it in the first column and adds CR only for the negative number. ----9.99 shows a minus sign only for -987.65, while +++++9 always shows a sign and, having no decimal places, drops the cents. 99B999B99 and 9(5)00 show the inserted spaces and zeros, and the all- Z picture ZZZZZ.ZZ turns zero into a blank field. Example: alphanumeric-edited and P scaling The first three lines are alphanumeric-edited MOVEs: slashes, spaces ( B ) and zeros are inserted between the moved characters. 9(3)PPP stores only three digits; the three P s stand for zeros after them. A DISPLAY shows the assumed zeros ( 045000 ), ADD and MOVE work with the full value, and moving 123456 into it keeps only the digits it can hold, so the last three become zeros. See also USAGE for how a number is stored, BLANK WHEN ZERO , SIGN , JUSTIFIED , MOVE , Level numbers , National data ."},{"t":"USAGE","u":"usage.html","s":"Choose how an item is stored in memory: as characters, binary, packed decimal or floating point.","k":"USAGE DISPLAY BINARY COMP COMPUTATIONAL COMP-4 COMP-5 COMP-3 PACKED-DECIMAL COMP-1 COMP-2 FLOAT-SHORT FLOAT-LONG INDEX POINTER NATIONAL big-endian little-endian bytes storage","c":"Data description","x":"Syntax [ USAGE [ IS ] ] { DISPLAY | BINARY | COMP | COMPUTATIONAL | COMP-4 | COMP-5 | COMP-3 | PACKED-DECIMAL | COMP-1 | FLOAT-SHORT | COMP-2 | FLOAT-LONG | INDEX | POINTER | NATIONAL } What it does The PICTURE says what values an item can hold; USAGE says how those values are kept in memory. The word USAGE IS is optional, so PIC 9(4) COMP is the usual way to write it. If you write USAGE on a group, every item in the group gets it. Without a USAGE clause, an item is DISPLAY . Usage doesn\u0027t change what your program computes: ADD 1 gives the same answer in every form. It changes the size of the item, how fast arithmetic is, and what the bytes look like when you write a record to a file or pass it to another program. Usage Stores Size DISPLAY One character per digit, like text. A sign is folded into the last digit (see SIGN ). 1 byte per digit BINARY , COMP , COMPUTATIONAL , COMP-4 A binary integer, most significant byte first (big-endian). The PICTURE\u0027s digits still limit the value. 1-2 digits: 1 byte; 3-4: 2; 5-9: 4; 10-18: 8 COMP-5 A binary integer in the computer\u0027s own byte order (least significant byte first on a PC). It can hold any value its bytes can: a PIC 9(4) COMP-5 holds 65000, where a PIC 9(4) COMP keeps only the last 4 digits (5000). as COMP COMP-3 , PACKED-DECIMAL Two digits per byte, with the sign in the last half-byte (C or F for plus, D for minus). digits / 2 + 1, rounded down COMP-1 , FLOAT-SHORT A single-precision floating-point number. No PICTURE. 4 bytes COMP-2 , FLOAT-LONG A double-precision floating-point number. No PICTURE. 8 bytes INDEX A table position, for SET and SEARCH . No PICTURE. 4 bytes POINTER The address of some data, set with SET . No PICTURE. 8 bytes NATIONAL UTF-16 characters, used with PIC N . See National data . 2 bytes per character Which one to use Use DISPLAY (the default) while you learn: you can see the digits in a data file. Use COMP or COMP-5 for counters and subscripts. COMP-3 is common in files that came from IBM mainframes. Floating point ( COMP-1 , COMP-2 ) is for science and engineering values; it can\u0027t hold most decimal fractions exactly, so don\u0027t use it for money. How DISPLAY shows them A DISPLAY of a DISPLAY , COMP or COMP-3 item shows its digits as the PICTURE describes them. A COMP-5 item is shown differently: as its raw value with no decimal point, padded with zeros to the most digits its bytes can hold (5 digits for 2 bytes, 10 for 4, 20 for 8). COMP-1 and COMP-2 are shown with up to 8 and 16 significant digits, with an exponent such as E+20 for very large or small values. SP COBOL matches GnuCOBOL 3.2 in all of these. Example: sizes and display The same value, -1234.56, takes 9 bytes as DISPLAY, 4 as COMP or COMP-5, and 5 as COMP-3. The COMP-5 item displays as -0000123456 : the stored integer, without the implied decimal point, padded to 10 digits. COMP-1 shows -1234.5601 because a 4-byte float can\u0027t hold 1234.56 exactly. A PIC 9(4) COMP-5 displays 5 digits ( 01234 ), while PIC 9(4) BINARY displays 4. After adding 1, all the forms agree. Example: the bytes FUNCTION HEX-OF shows the bytes of an item in hexadecimal. 258 is 1 * 256 + 2. As COMP it is stored 01 02 , high byte first; as COMP-5 it is 02 01 , the PC\u0027s own order. A negative COMP value is stored as its two\u0027s complement ( FFFE for -2). The packed items hold the digits 1 2 3 4 5 two to a byte, ending with D for minus or F for an unsigned value. The last line shows a signed DISPLAY item: the bytes are the characters 1 , 2 and s , because the minus sign is folded into the last digit. SP COBOL notes COMP is big-endian, as in GnuCOBOL\u0027s default configuration, so binary data files are the same bytes in both. The COMP-5 display width and the COMP-1/COMP-2 display format follow GnuCOBOL 3.2 exactly; DECISIONS.md has the details. See also PICTURE , SIGN , SYNCHRONIZED , National data , Intrinsic functions (HEX-OF, BYTE-LENGTH)."},{"t":"VALUE","u":"value.html","s":"Give a data item the value it starts with when the program begins.","k":"VALUE VALUES initial value starting value figurative constant SPACES ZERO ALL HIGH-VALUES table OCCURS INITIALIZE","c":"Data description","x":"Syntax Format 1: an item\u0027s starting value VALUE [ IS ] literal-1 Format 2: on a level-88 condition-name { VALUE [ IS ] | VALUES [ ARE ] } { literal-1 [ { THROUGH | THRU } literal-2 ] } ... What it does A VALUE clause in WORKING-STORAGE puts a value in the item before the first statement of the program runs. An item without one starts out with spaces if it is alphanumeric and zero if it is numeric, but writing VALUE makes your intent clear. The literal must suit the item: a number for a numeric item (it must fit in the PICTURE, or the compiler warns), text in quotes for an alphanumeric one. You can also use a figurative constant : SPACES , ZERO , HIGH-VALUES , LOW-VALUES , QUOTES , or ALL \"x\" to fill the whole item with a character or pattern. VALUE on a group A group can have a VALUE too, as long as it is text (or a figurative constant). The text is put into the group\u0027s bytes from the left, as if the group were one PIC X field, and the items inside it then hold their part of it. Leave VALUE off the items inside it. VALUE in tables A VALUE on an item with OCCURS gives every occurrence the same starting value. To start a table with different values, the classic way is to write a group of FILLER items with the values one after another, then REDEFINES that group as a table. The example does this for day names. VALUE on condition-names On a level-88 item, VALUE doesn\u0027t set anything. It lists the values for which the condition is true, and may have several values and ranges with THRU . See Condition-names . VALUE versus INITIALIZE VALUE is applied once, when the program starts. To reset items while the program runs, use INITIALIZE or MOVE . Note that INITIALIZE doesn\u0027t go back to the VALUE: by default it sets numeric items to zero and alphanumeric items to spaces. ( INITIALIZE item ALL TO VALUE is the way to put the VALUE clauses back.) Give starting values in WORKING-STORAGE. In the LINKAGE SECTION and FILE SECTION, use VALUE only on 88 levels: that storage belongs to the calling program or to the file\u0027s records, not to your program. Example Each item starts with its VALUE: the short literal \"HELLO\" is padded with spaces, ALL \"*\" fills STARS, and HIGH-VALUES are bytes of FF . The group HEADER\u0027s value is split between H-NAME and H-CITY by position. The day names come from the FILLER values, seen through the redefining table, and every occurrence of CNT starts at 07. After the MOVEs, INITIALIZE sets GREETING to spaces and the CNT items to zero, not back to their VALUE of 7. See also INITIALIZE , Figurative constants , Literals , OCCURS , Condition-names , Constants ."},{"t":"OCCURS","u":"occurs.html","s":"Repeat an item to make a table (an array), with fixed or variable size.","k":"OCCURS TIMES table array subscript index INDEXED BY DEPENDING ON ODO variable length ASCENDING DESCENDING KEY two-dimensional nested","c":"Data description","x":"Syntax Format 1: a fixed number of occurrences OCCURS integer-1 [ TIMES ] [ { ASCENDING | DESCENDING } KEY IS data-name-1 ... ] ... [ INDEXED BY index-name-1 ... ] Format 2: a variable number (OCCURS DEPENDING ON) OCCURS integer-1 TO integer-2 [ TIMES ] DEPENDING ON data-name-2 [ { ASCENDING | DESCENDING } KEY IS data-name-1 ... ] ... [ INDEXED BY index-name-1 ... ] Referring to an occurrence data-name ( subscript [ [ , ] subscript ] ... ) What it does OCCURS makes an item repeat, side by side in memory. The result is a table (other languages call it an array). 05 PRICE PIC 9(3)V99 OCCURS 5 is five prices, one after another. Usually the table is put inside a group (an 01 with the OCCURS item under it), so that the whole table has a name too. OCCURS can be on an elementary item or on a group. On a group, each occurrence holds the whole group: REGION OCCURS 2 in the example is two copies of a name followed by four sales figures. Subscripts You pick one occurrence with a subscript in parentheses after the name. Numbering starts at 1, not 0. A subscript can be a number, a numeric item, an index name, or an item plus or minus a number ( ITEM(IX + 1) , QTR-SALES(R - 1, Q) ). A space before the parenthesis is allowed; inside, a space or comma separates subscripts. COBOL doesn\u0027t check subscripts for you by default, so check your loop limits. The third example and the SP COBOL notes show what happens when a subscript is out of range. Tables of tables When an item with OCCURS is inside a group that also has OCCURS, you need one subscript for each level, outermost first. QTR-SALES(2, 4) is the fourth quarter of the second region. This is a two-dimensional table; you can nest further in the same way. INDEXED BY INDEXED BY names an index for the table: a special subscript that the compiler keeps in the form that\u0027s fastest for that table. You don\u0027t declare an index in WORKING-STORAGE. You set and change it with SET ( SET IX TO 1 , SET IX UP BY 1 ) or PERFORM VARYING , and SEARCH needs one. OCCURS DEPENDING ON Format 2 makes the table\u0027s current size come from another item (the ODO object ). Room is kept for the maximum, but the group that contains the table is treated as only as long as the current count: a DISPLAY, a MOVE of the whole group, or a WRITE of the record uses just that many occurrences. A variable table must be the last thing in its record. ASCENDING / DESCENDING KEY These phrases tell COBOL that the table\u0027s entries are kept in order by the named items. They don\u0027t sort anything; they are what SEARCH ALL (a binary search) relies on. See SEARCH . VALUE in tables A VALUE on an item with OCCURS gives every occurrence that value. See VALUE for starting a table with different values. Example: a two-dimensional table Each region holds a 5-byte name and four 4-digit amounts, so a region is 21 bytes and the table 42. The nested PERFORMs fill and total the table with two subscripts. A subscript can be an expression: with R at 1, QTR-SALES(R + 1, 2) is the second quarter of region 2. REGION(1) with one subscript is the whole first group. Example: DEPENDING ON and INDEXED BY With ITEM-COUNT at 3, ORDER-REC is 6 + 3 * 4 = 18 bytes long, and a DISPLAY of it stops after the third item. Setting the count to 5 makes it 26 bytes. The loop runs the index IX up to the current count, and SET IX UP BY 1 moves it from 2 to 3. Example: a subscript past the end SCORE has 3 occurrences, but the loop runs N up to 4. SCORE (4) would be past the end of SCORE-TABLE, so SP COBOL stops the program there with an error line and a hint, and the exit code is 1. Stock GnuCOBOL prints SCORE 4: followed by whatever two bytes come after the table in memory (here two spaces), then carries on. See the SP COBOL notes. SP COBOL notes Deliberate difference from GnuCOBOL 3.2 (see Differences from GnuCOBOL ): a subscript or index that reaches past the end of the whole 01 record stops the program with libcob: error: a subscript, index or reference modification is outside its data item and a hint line, exit code 1. Plain GnuCOBOL silently reads or overwrites the bytes after the record; it gives the same error only when the program is compiled with -debug . In SP COBOL each 01 record is its own block of memory, so there are no bytes after it to use. A subscript that stays inside the record (past the table but before the record ends) still reads or changes the items that follow the table, as in GnuCOBOL. Run the program in Visual Studio (F5) to stop on the statement that went out of range. See also SEARCH , SET , PERFORM , VALUE , REDEFINES , Referring to data ."},{"t":"REDEFINES","u":"redefines.html","s":"Describe the same bytes of storage a second way.","k":"REDEFINES overlay union two views same storage date year month day","c":"Data description","x":"Syntax level-number data-name-1 REDEFINES data-name-2 [ other clauses ] . What it does REDEFINES doesn\u0027t create new storage. It gives the bytes of data-name-2 a second description, data-name-1 . Both names refer to the same memory, so a change through one name is seen through the other at once. The most common uses are: splitting a value into parts, such as a date PIC 9(8) seen as year, month and day; looking at the same bytes as text or as a number, for example to check input before using it in arithmetic; starting a table with different values: FILLER items with VALUEs, redefined as an OCCURS table (see VALUE ); records that come in several layouts, where a type code says which layout applies. Rules The REDEFINES entry has the same level number as the item it redefines, and comes right after it (after that item\u0027s own subordinate items, if it is a group). You can redefine an item more than once: several entries can all name the same data-name-2 . At level 01 the new description may be longer than the original, as in the example. At lower levels keep it the same size or shorter. Don\u0027t put a VALUE on the redefining item; give the starting value on the original. COBOL doesn\u0027t convert anything between the two views. If the bytes don\u0027t make sense for the new description, such as letters seen through a PIC 9 item, you get nonsense rather than an error. Example TODAY-PARTS covers the same 8 bytes as TODAY, so YYYY, MM and DD show the parts of the date. Moving 12 to MM changes the month inside TODAY. AMOUNT-9 sees the six characters 001250 as the number 12.50; adding 1 to it changes the text to 001350 . LONG-ONE is a 6-byte view of a 4-byte item, which is allowed at level 01. See also RENAMES , Level numbers , OCCURS , VALUE , Referring to data (reference modification, another way to look at part of an item)."},{"t":"RENAMES","u":"renames.html","s":"Level 66: give one name to a run of neighboring items in a record.","k":"RENAMES 66 THRU THROUGH regroup alias","c":"Data description","x":"Syntax 66 data-name-1 RENAMES data-name-2 [ { THROUGH | THRU } data-name-3 ] . What it does A level-66 entry gives a new name to part of the record just above it, without changing the record. With THRU , data-name-1 covers everything from the start of data-name-2 to the end of data-name-3 , as if it were a group of those items. Without THRU , it is simply another name for data-name-2 . It is useful when you need a group that the record\u0027s layout doesn\u0027t have, such as \"city, state and ZIP\" out of an address, without reorganizing the record. Rules 66 entries come right after the last item of the 01 record they belong to. The renamed items must be in that record, and data-name-3 must come after data-name-2 . You can\u0027t rename an 01 or 77 item, a level-88 or another 66, or an item with (or inside an item with) OCCURS . A RENAMES of several items acts like a group: it is alphanumeric and its length is the total of the items it covers. Example CITY-STATE-ZIP covers CITY, STATE and ZIP: 10 + 2 + 5 = 17 bytes. POSTAL-CODE is just another name for ZIP. Moving one 17-character string into CITY-STATE-ZIP fills all three items by position. See also REDEFINES , Level numbers ."},{"t":"Condition-names (level 88)","u":"condition-names.html","s":"Give a name to a value or set of values, then test it like a true/false condition.","k":"88 level-88 condition-name VALUE VALUES THRU THROUGH SET TO TRUE SET TO FALSE FALSE IS flag switch boolean","c":"Data description","x":"Syntax 88 condition-name { VALUE [ IS ] | VALUES [ ARE ] } { literal-1 [ { THROUGH | THRU } literal-2 ] } ... [ FALSE [ IS ] literal-3 ] . Using it IF [ NOT ] condition-name ... SET condition-name ... TO TRUE SET condition-name ... TO FALSE What it does A level-88 entry comes right under a data item (its conditional variable ) and names some of the values that item can have. The name then works as a condition: it is true when the item holds one of those values. Instead of IF GRADE \u003e= 60 AND GRADE \u003c= 100 you write IF PASSED , which says what the test means. An 88 takes up no storage. One item may have many 88 entries, and their value lists may overlap: in the example, 100 makes both PASSED and PERFECT true. Values, lists and ranges List as many values as you need after VALUE or VALUES (the two words mean the same). THRU (or THROUGH ) gives a range, both ends included. Values and ranges can be mixed in one entry. Where to use them Anywhere a condition is allowed: IF , EVALUATE TRUE with WHEN condition-name (see EVALUATE ), PERFORM ... UNTIL condition-name (see PERFORM ), and with NOT , AND and OR (see Conditions ). SET TO TRUE and SET TO FALSE SET condition-name TO TRUE moves the condition\u0027s (first) value into the conditional variable, which makes the condition true. It is a clearer way of writing a flag than MOVE \"Y\" TO END-FLAG . SET condition-name TO FALSE needs a FALSE phrase in the 88 entry, and moves that value instead. Example 75 is in the range 60 THRU 100 but isn\u0027t 100, so PASSED is true and PERFECT is not. SET PERFECT TO TRUE moves 100 into GRADE. \"O\" is in WARM\u0027s list, so EVALUATE takes the first WHEN. The loop runs until SET ALL-DONE TO TRUE puts \"Y\" in END-FLAG, and SET ALL-DONE TO FALSE puts back the FALSE value, \"N\". See also SET , IF , EVALUATE , Conditions , VALUE , Level numbers ."},{"t":"Constants (level 78 and CONSTANT)","u":"constants.html","s":"Give a name to a fixed value that the compiler substitutes wherever you use it.","k":"78 level-78 CONSTANT AS named constant LENGTH OF table size fixed value","c":"Data description","x":"Syntax Format 1: level 78 78 constant-name VALUE [ IS ] literal-1 . Format 2: CONSTANT (COBOL 2002 style) 01 constant-name CONSTANT AS { literal-1 | LENGTH OF data-name-1 } . What it does A constant is a name for a value that never changes. It takes no storage: wherever the name appears, the compiler uses the value instead, as if you had typed the literal there. So a constant can go in places where only a literal is allowed, such as the number of occurrences in OCCURS or the length in a PICTURE. Both forms do the same job. 78 is the older form that GnuCOBOL took over from Micro Focus COBOL; CONSTANT AS is the form in the 2002 COBOL standard, and it can also take the length of an item or of another constant with LENGTH OF . What you can use one for a table size: OCCURS MAX-ITEMS , and the same name as the loop limit, so changing one line changes both; a size in a PICTURE: PIC X(TITLE-LEN) ; a value in an expression, a comparison, a MOVE or a DISPLAY, like any literal. Because it isn\u0027t a data item, you can\u0027t MOVE anything into a constant or change it in any other way. Example MAX-ITEMS sets the table size (5 prices of 5 bytes, 25 bytes) and is also the loop limit and a subscript. TITLE-LEN is the length of the text constant SHOP-NAME, 12, and sizes the HEADING-TXT field to fit it exactly. TAX-RATE is used in a COMPUTE like any number: 50.00 times 0.0775 is 3.875, rounded to 3.88. See also Literals , Level numbers , OCCURS , Figurative constants , VALUE ."},{"t":"JUSTIFIED","u":"justified.html","s":"Line text up on the right of an alphanumeric item instead of the left.","k":"JUSTIFIED JUST RIGHT right-align alignment alphanumeric MOVE UNSTRING truncation","c":"Data description","x":"Syntax { JUSTIFIED | JUST } [ RIGHT ] What it does Normally, when you MOVE text into an alphanumeric item, it goes in from the left: short text is padded with spaces on the right, and text that is too long loses characters on the right. JUSTIFIED RIGHT turns this around for the receiving item. Short text is padded with spaces on the left , so it ends at the right edge, and text that is too long loses characters on the left. It is allowed only on elementary alphabetic or alphanumeric items ( PIC A or PIC X ), not on numbers, which are always lined up by their decimal point. RIGHT is optional; JUST is short for JUSTIFIED . JUSTIFIED affects only data moved into the item. A VALUE clause is not moved, so VALUE \"AB\" still starts at the left. UNSTRING into a justified item An UNSTRING piece that is shorter than the item is lined up on the right, as with MOVE. Remember that the piece includes any spaces it contains: when the sending field is a PIC X(12) holding DOG,EMU , the piece after the comma is EMU followed by five spaces, which fills an 8-byte item from the left. A piece that is too long is cut on the right , unlike MOVE (see the notes). Example \"CAT\" goes to the left of the plain item and to the right of the JUSTIFIED one. \"ELEPHANT\" is too long for PIC X(4) JUST , so the MOVE keeps the rightmost four characters, HANT . RIGHT-START shows that a VALUE is not right-justified. The first UNSTRING puts \"EMU\" on the right. In the last one, the second piece is the twelve characters IJKLMNOPQRST , and the justified item keeps the first eight of them. SP COBOL notes When an UNSTRING piece is longer than a JUSTIFIED RIGHT item, GnuCOBOL 3.2 fills the item from the left and drops the extra characters on the right, where a MOVE would drop them on the left. SP COBOL keeps this behavior on purpose so that programs give the same results as under GnuCOBOL (DECISIONS.md, \"Kept as stock\"). If you need the MOVE behavior, UNSTRING into a plain item and then MOVE it to the justified one. See also MOVE , UNSTRING , PICTURE ."},{"t":"BLANK WHEN ZERO","u":"blank-when-zero.html","s":"Show a numeric display field as all spaces when its value is zero.","k":"BLANK WHEN ZERO BLANK ZERO blank zero suppress report edited","c":"Data description","x":"Syntax BLANK [ WHEN ] { ZERO | ZEROS | ZEROES } What it does BLANK WHEN ZERO makes a field become all spaces whenever zero is moved into it. Any other value is stored as the PICTURE says. It is used on reports, where a column of 0.00 entries is harder to read than empty space. It goes on an elementary numeric or numeric-edited item whose usage is DISPLAY. A plain numeric item with BLANK WHEN ZERO is treated as numeric-edited, so you use it as the receiving field of a MOVE and for display, not for arithmetic. An edited picture made only of Z s already turns zero into spaces (see PICTURE ). BLANK WHEN ZERO does the same for pictures that show digits with 9 , such as Z,ZZ9.99 , which would otherwise print 0.00 . Example With 12.50, all three fields show the number. With zero, the plain edited field shows 0.00 , while the two BLANK WHEN ZERO fields are spaces. BLANK ZERO without WHEN means the same thing. See also PICTURE , MOVE , Report Writer ."},{"t":"SIGN","u":"sign.html","s":"Choose where the sign of a signed DISPLAY number is kept, and whether it gets its own byte.","k":"SIGN LEADING TRAILING SEPARATE CHARACTER overpunch overpunched zoned sign negative minus","c":"Data description","x":"Syntax [ SIGN [ IS ] ] { LEADING | TRAILING } [ SEPARATE [ CHARACTER ] ] What it does A signed number with usage DISPLAY ( PIC S9(3) ) keeps one character per digit. The S doesn\u0027t add a byte. Instead, the sign is folded into one of the digits by changing that character: this is called an overpunched sign, after the way punched cards marked it. The SIGN clause says where the sign goes. Clause Where the sign is -123 is stored as Size of S9(3) (none) folded into the last digit 12s 3 SIGN LEADING folded into the first digit q23 3 SIGN TRAILING SEPARATE its own byte after the digits 123- 4 SIGN LEADING SEPARATE its own byte before the digits -123 4 In the overpunched forms, a negative 0-9 digit is stored as one of the letters p to y , which is what you see if you look at the bytes in a data file or through a PIC X view of the field. A positive number is stored as plain digits. A DISPLAY of the number itself always shows a readable sign, whatever the clause. SEPARATE costs one byte but makes data files easier to read and to exchange with other programs; CHARACTER is an optional word. The SIGN clause needs an S in the PICTURE. Example The first line DISPLAYs all four items. Three show -123 ; the TRAILING SEPARATE item is shown as it is stored, 123- , with the sign last. The next lines look at their bytes with reference modification, which treats them as plain characters: the default puts the sign in the last digit ( 3 becomes s ), LEADING puts it in the first ( 1 becomes q ), and the SEPARATE forms keep an ordinary - in a byte of its own, which is why they are 4 bytes long. +123 is stored as plain 123 . See also PICTURE (the S symbol and edited signs + - CR DB ), USAGE , Referring to data (reference modification)."},{"t":"SYNCHRONIZED","u":"synchronized.html","s":"Line binary items up on memory boundaries, adding unused bytes in front of them.","k":"SYNCHRONIZED SYNC LEFT RIGHT alignment slack bytes boundary binary COMP","c":"Data description","x":"Syntax { SYNCHRONIZED | SYNC } [ LEFT | RIGHT ] What it does Processors read a 4-byte or 8-byte binary number fastest when it starts at an address that is a multiple of its size. SYNCHRONIZED asks the compiler to place a binary item ( COMP , BINARY , COMP-5 , POINTER , ...) on such a boundary. To do that inside a record, it inserts unused bytes, called slack bytes , in front of the item, so the record gets longer. In SP COBOL, as in GnuCOBOL 3.2, this really changes the layout: the example\u0027s record grows from 14 to 24 bytes. On a DISPLAY item SYNC has no effect. Values and arithmetic are the same either way. When to use it Rarely. On a modern PC the speed difference is too small to notice, and the slack bytes change the record\u0027s length and the position of every item after them. Use SYNC only when a record layout must match one that was defined with it, such as a file written by another program or a structure passed to a C library. LEFT and RIGHT The words LEFT and RIGHT are accepted and give the same alignment as plain SYNC. The compiler warns that RIGHT \"is not implemented\", but the layout is the same as with LEFT. Example Without SYNC the four items are packed: 1 + 4 + 1 + 8 = 14 bytes. With SYNC, 3 slack bytes put S-COUNT at offset 4, S-CODE follows at offset 8, and 7 slack bytes put S-TOTAL at offset 16, for 24 bytes in all. The arithmetic works as usual. See also USAGE , Level numbers ."},{"t":"GLOBAL and EXTERNAL","u":"global-external.html","s":"Share data with nested programs (GLOBAL) or between separately compiled programs (EXTERNAL).","k":"GLOBAL EXTERNAL shared data nested program contained program common storage scope","c":"Data description","x":"Syntax 01 data-name [ other clauses ] [ IS ] GLOBAL . 01 data-name [ other clauses ] [ IS ] EXTERNAL . FD file-name [ IS ] GLOBAL ... What it does Normally each program\u0027s WORKING-STORAGE is private: no other program can see it, and the only way to share data is to pass it on a CALL with USING . GLOBAL and EXTERNAL are two other ways to share. Both go on an 01 item (and apply to everything inside it). GLOBAL A program can contain other programs, written before its END PROGRAM line. These are nested (or contained ) programs; see Program structure . An item marked GLOBAL in the outer program can be used by name in every program nested inside it, without being declared again or passed on the CALL. All of them work on the same storage. Items without GLOBAL stay private to the program that declares them. If a nested program declares an item with the same name, its own item is the one it sees. A file\u0027s FD can be GLOBAL too, so nested programs can READ and WRITE a file the outer program opened, and a USE GLOBAL declarative can handle their I/O errors (see USE ). EXTERNAL EXTERNAL is meant for programs compiled separately, which can\u0027t see each other\u0027s names. Each program declares the same 01 item with EXTERNAL , and they all share one copy of it, found by its name when the program starts. Declare it with the same name and the same layout in every program; the name is what links them, so a different layout would just read the same bytes a different way. Give the shared data its starting values with MOVE in the program that runs first, as the example does. Example: GLOBAL ADD-SALE is nested inside GLOBAL1. It adds to GRAND-TOTAL by name, although GRAND-TOTAL is declared only in the outer program and is not passed on the CALL. The sale amount itself is passed with USING, the usual way. After two calls, the outer program sees the total that the nested program built up. Example: EXTERNAL TAKEORDER is a separate program (in GLOBAL2.sub\\TAKEORDER.cbl ), not nested in GLOBAL2, so GLOBAL could not reach it. Both programs declare SHARED-TOTALS as EXTERNAL, and nothing is passed on the CALL. The main program sets the starting values, TAKEORDER counts the orders and records the customer, and afterwards the main program sees what TAKEORDER stored, because there is only one SHARED-TOTALS. SP COBOL notes GLOBAL data, GLOBAL files, USE GLOBAL and EXTERNAL items work as in GnuCOBOL 3.2. The debugger\u0027s Watch window view of COBOL data leaves out EXTERNAL items. See also CALL , Program structure , USE , Files , Debugging ."},{"t":"National data (PIC N)","u":"national.html","s":"Hold Unicode text as UTF-16 characters with PIC N, N\"...\" literals and USAGE NATIONAL.","k":"PIC N national NATIONAL USAGE NATIONAL N\"\" national literal Unicode UTF-16 BYTE-LENGTH","c":"Data description","x":"Syntax level-number data-name PIC N(n) [ USAGE [ IS ] NATIONAL ] [ VALUE N\"text\" ] . A national literal N\"text\" What it does National data holds Unicode characters, so it can store text in any language. Each N in the PICTURE is one character, kept as UTF-16: two bytes per character, high byte first. PIC N(5) holds five characters in 10 bytes. FUNCTION LENGTH counts characters; FUNCTION BYTE-LENGTH counts bytes. A literal written N\"...\" is a national literal. Use it in VALUE clauses, MOVEs and comparisons with national items. MOVE and comparison work as with PIC X : a longer value is cut on the right, and two national items compare character by character. USAGE NATIONAL PIC N is national by itself, so you don\u0027t need to write USAGE NATIONAL . In GnuCOBOL 3.2, adding USAGE NATIONAL to a PIC N item doubles its size again: PIC N(5) USAGE NATIONAL is 20 bytes, not 10, and a 5-character value moved into it is followed by 5 characters of padding. SP COBOL lays out storage exactly as GnuCOBOL does, so it does the same. Leave USAGE NATIONAL out. Padding When a shorter national value is moved into a national item, GnuCOBOL fills the rest with bytes of hexadecimal 20 (the single-byte space), two per character, rather than with the Unicode space. A DISPLAY in SP COBOL shows each such pair as ? (see the next section). Keep national values the full length of the item, or pad them yourself, if you plan to DISPLAY them. DISPLAY of national data A DISPLAY of a PIC N item in SP COBOL prints its characters. Characters above U+00FF can\u0027t be shown on the console, which uses the Latin-1 character set, so each prints as ? . Example GREETING holds 5 characters in 10 bytes. MOVE copies N\"WORLD\" into NAME-N, and moving the 5-character GREETING into the 3-character SHORT-N keeps HEL . The comparison with a national literal is true. N\"HI\" in the 4-character PADDED-N is followed by the 20 20 padding described above, which DISPLAY shows as ?? . SP COBOL notes SP COBOL deliberately differs here. GnuCOBOL 3.2\u0027s DISPLAY writes a national item\u0027s raw UTF-16 bytes, so N\"HELLO\" prints as NUL H NUL E NUL L NUL L NUL O, which is no use to a reader. SP COBOL decodes the field and prints the characters (above U+00FF as ? ), on the console and on SCREEN SECTION screens. The storage is unchanged, so MOVEs, comparisons and data files are the same as in GnuCOBOL. Only what DISPLAY prints is different; that is why this example\u0027s output is not compared with GnuCOBOL\u0027s. The compiler warns that national support is \"unfinished\" in GnuCOBOL. That warning is about GnuCOBOL\u0027s own runtime; SP COBOL\u0027s project builds turn it off. The intrinsic functions NATIONAL-OF and DISPLAY-OF are not implemented in GnuCOBOL 3.2\u0027s runtime, and stop the program in SP COBOL too. See also PICTURE , USAGE , Literals , DISPLAY , Differences from GnuCOBOL ."},{"t":"Literals","u":"literals.html","s":"Values written directly in the program: numbers, quoted text, hexadecimal and other forms.","k":"numeric literal alphanumeric literal string quotes apostrophe doubled quote hexadecimal X\"\" national N\"\" null-terminated Z\"\" boolean B\"\" floating-point E exponent continuation concatenation ampersand \u0026","c":"Using data","x":"Syntax Numeric literal: [ + | - ] digits [ . digits ] [ + | - ] digits [ . digits ] E [ + | - ] digits (floating-point) Alphanumeric literal: \"characters\" or \u0027characters\u0027 Other forms: X\"hex-digits\" alphanumeric, given as hexadecimal bytes Z\"characters\" alphanumeric, with a null byte added at the end N\"characters\" national (Unicode) B\"bits\" boolean Joining literals: literal-1 \u0026 literal-2 ... What it does A literal is a value written straight into the source, such as 100 or \"TOTAL:\" , as opposed to a data item whose value can change. Numeric literals Digits, with an optional sign in front and an optional decimal point: 42 , -0.5 , +7 , 12345.67 . A numeric literal may have up to 38 digits. Don\u0027t put a comma or a space inside one, and don\u0027t end it with a decimal point ( 5. reads as 5 followed by the end of the sentence). A literal with E and an exponent, such as 1.5E3 (1500), is a floating-point literal. Alphanumeric literals Characters between quotation marks: \"HELLO\" . Apostrophes work too: \u0027HELLO\u0027 . To put the quote character itself inside a literal, write it twice: \"SHE SAID \"\"HI\"\"\" holds SHE SAID \"HI\" . Inside apostrophes a quotation mark is ordinary, and the other way round. When a literal is moved to a longer field, it is padded with spaces on the right; when it is compared with a longer field, it is treated as if padded with spaces. A literal that won\u0027t fit on one line can be continued (see source format ), but joining shorter literals with \u0026 is easier to read and to edit: \"FIRST PART \" \u0026 \"SECOND PART\" is one literal. Hexadecimal literals X\"...\" gives the bytes in hexadecimal, two digits per byte. X\"41\" is the letter A; X\"0D0A\" is a carriage return and line feed. Use it for bytes you can\u0027t type. Null-terminated literals Z\"ABC\" is the three letters followed by a byte of zero (LOW-VALUE), so it is four bytes long. This is the form C functions expect for a string. National literals N\"...\" is a national literal, for PIC N items: each character takes two bytes (UTF-16). MOVE N\"ABC\" TO NAT-NAME fills a PIC N(3) item, which is 3 characters and 6 bytes long. See national data . Boolean literals B\"1\" and B\"0\" are boolean literals, for items with PIC 1 . Example: numbers and text Each numeric literal is moved to AMOUNT and shown through an edited picture. The doubled quotes in the first DISPLAY print as single ones. X\"434F424F4C\" is the five bytes C, O, B, O, L. The last DISPLAY shows one literal built from three with \u0026 . Example: the special forms Z\"ABC\" is 4 bytes, and moving it to C-TEXT leaves a LOW-VALUE byte in the fourth position. FLAG starts as B\"1\" . 1.5E3 is 1500. SP COBOL notes The compiler marks national literals and PIC N as \"unfinished\" in GnuCOBOL 3.2 (warning -Wunfinished ). SP COBOL implements them, and its build turns that warning off. DISPLAY of a PIC N item differs from stock GnuCOBOL: SP COBOL shows the characters. See national data . See also Figurative constants (ZERO, SPACES, ALL \"x\"), source format (continuation lines), PICTURE , national data , constants ."},{"t":"Figurative constants","u":"figurative-constants.html","s":"Reserved words such as ZERO, SPACES and HIGH-VALUES that stand for a value and fill a whole field.","k":"ZERO ZEROS ZEROES SPACE SPACES HIGH-VALUE HIGH-VALUES LOW-VALUE LOW-VALUES QUOTE QUOTES ALL literal NULL NULLS fill blank clear","c":"Using data","x":"Syntax { ZERO | ZEROS | ZEROES } { SPACE | SPACES } { HIGH-VALUE | HIGH-VALUES } { LOW-VALUE | LOW-VALUES } { QUOTE | QUOTES } ALL literal { NULL | NULLS } What it does A figurative constant is a word that stands for a value. Unlike a literal it has no fixed length: it repeats as often as needed to fill whatever it is moved to or compared with. The singular and plural spellings mean exactly the same thing. Word Value Typical use ZERO , ZEROS , ZEROES The number 0, or the character \"0\" repeated in a text field. Clearing totals and counters. SPACE , SPACES Blank characters. Clearing text fields and records; testing for an empty field. HIGH-VALUE , HIGH-VALUES The highest byte value, X\"FF\" (255). A key that sorts after every real key, such as an end-of-file marker when matching two files. LOW-VALUE , LOW-VALUES The lowest byte value, X\"00\". A key that sorts before everything; filling a field with binary zeros. QUOTE , QUOTES The quotation mark character \" . Building text that contains quotes. ALL literal The literal repeated: ALL \"AB\" fills a field with ABABAB... Lines of dashes or stars; filling with a pattern. NULL , NULLS The null address. Only with USAGE POINTER items: SET PTR TO NULL , IF PTR = NULL (see SET ). Where you can use them As the source of a MOVE : MOVE SPACES TO CUST-NAME fills every byte of CUST-NAME. Moved to a numeric field, ZERO sets it to 0. In a VALUE clause: 01 TOTAL PIC 9(5) VALUE ZERO. In a comparison: IF CUST-NAME = SPACES is true when the field is all blanks, however long it is. The same goes for HIGH-VALUES , LOW-VALUES and ALL \"x\" : IF END-KEY = HIGH-VALUES is true only when every byte is a high value. Only ZERO makes sense for a numeric field. SPACE, QUOTE and ALL \"x\" are for alphanumeric fields; HIGH-VALUES and LOW-VALUES are bytes, not numbers. Examples TEXT-6 starts as SPACES and NUM-4 as ZERO, from their VALUE clauses. Moving ZEROS to a text field fills it with the character 0. ALL \"AB\" repeats the pattern across all six bytes. HIGH-VALUES and LOW-VALUES are bytes you can\u0027t see on the screen, so the program shows the code of the first byte instead: 255 and 0. The last test compares part of the field with SPACES; the figurative constant takes the length of whatever it is compared with. Comparing with figurative constants Each constant is stretched to the six bytes of the field it is compared with. END-KEY and START-KEY were filled from their VALUE clauses, so they match. Any ordinary text such as SMITH lies between LOW-VALUES and HIGH-VALUES, which is why they make good \"before everything\" and \"after everything\" keys. ALL \"AB\" is compared as ABABAB. Changing one byte of STARS is enough to make it unequal to ALL \"*\" . See also Literals , MOVE , INITIALIZE , VALUE , conditions ."},{"t":"Referring to data","u":"referring-to-data.html","s":"Name the exact data you mean: qualification with OF/IN, subscripts, indexes and reference modification.","k":"qualification OF IN qualified name subscript index relative subscript reference modification substring (start:length) table element two-dimensional identifier","c":"Using data","x":"Syntax Qualification: data-name-1 [ { OF | IN } data-name-2 ] ... Subscripts (items in a table): data-name ( subscript [ , subscript ] ... ) subscript: integer | data-name-3 [ { + | - } integer ] | index-name [ { + | - } integer ] Reference modification (part of an item): identifier ( start : [ length ] ) What it does Most of the time a data item\u0027s name is all you need. This page covers the three ways to be more exact. Together they make up an identifier , the thing the syntax boxes on other pages ask for, and they can be combined: SEAT OF SEATS (2, 3) , MONTH-NAME (3) (2:2) . Qualification: OF and IN Two items may have the same name if they are in different groups, like the CITY of a home address and the CITY of a work address. To say which one you mean, add OF (or IN , which means the same) and the name of a group that contains it: CITY OF HOME-ADDRESS . You only need enough qualifiers to make the name unique; A OF B OF C is allowed when one level isn\u0027t enough. Subscripts An item with an OCCURS clause is a table, and each use of it needs a subscript in parentheses saying which element: MONTH-NAME (2) is the second one. Elements are numbered from 1. A subscript can be: a literal: MONTH-NAME (2) ; a numeric data item: MONTH-NAME (N) ; a data item plus or minus a literal (a relative subscript ): MONTH-NAME (N + 1) . A table inside a table needs one subscript for each level, outer first, separated by commas or spaces: SEAT (ROW-NO, SEAT-NO) . Indexes OCCURS ... INDEXED BY name declares an index for the table. An index is used like a subscript, MONTH-NAME (M-IDX) , but you change it with SET ( SET M-IDX TO 6 , SET M-IDX UP BY 1 ) or PERFORM VARYING , not with MOVE or ADD. Indexes are what SEARCH uses. Relative indexing ( M-IDX + 1 ) is allowed too. Reference modification To use part of an item, write the starting position and the length in parentheses with a colon between: CITY (1:3) is the first three characters of CITY. Leave out the length to mean \"to the end\": CITY (5:) . Positions count from 1, and either part can be a data item or an arithmetic expression. The result is always treated as alphanumeric text, even when the item is a number. On a table element, the subscript comes first: MONTH-NAME (3) (2:2) . Keep subscripts between 1 and the OCCURS count. COBOL doesn\u0027t check them for you. In stock GnuCOBOL, MONTH-NAME (7) in a six-element table quietly reads whatever bytes lie in memory after the table. SP COBOL keeps each 01 record in its own block of memory, so a subscript, index or reference modification that runs off the end of the record stops the program with libcob: error: a subscript, index or reference modification is outside its data item and a hint, as GnuCOBOL does only when compiled with -debug . This is a deliberate difference (see Differences from GnuCOBOL ); the OCCURS page has an example. Example: one of each The two CITY items are told apart by OF and IN. With N = 4, MONTH-NAME (N) is APR, (N + 1) is MAY and (N - 3) is JAN. The index M-IDX is SET to 6, then down by one. MONTH-TABLE (N:5) takes five characters of the whole table starting at position 4, which crosses from FEB into MAR. Example: a two-level table SEATS holds two rows of three seats. SEAT (1, 2) is row 1, seat 2. The second MOVE adds a qualifier as well; it comes before the subscripts. SEAT-ROW (R) is a whole row, shown as three characters. See also OCCURS , SET , SEARCH , DATA DIVISION , intrinsic functions (FUNCTION LENGTH, for example)."},{"t":"Conditions","u":"conditions.html","s":"The tests that IF, EVALUATE, PERFORM UNTIL and SEARCH WHEN use: relations, classes, signs, condition names, AND/OR/NOT.","k":"relation condition class condition sign condition NUMERIC ALPHABETIC ALPHABETIC-UPPER ALPHABETIC-LOWER POSITIVE NEGATIVE ZERO AND OR NOT abbreviated combined condition equal greater less GREATER THAN LESS THAN EQUAL TO comparison padding spaces 88 condition-name","c":"Using data","x":"Syntax Relation condition: operand-1 [ IS ] [ NOT ] { = | EQUAL TO | \u003e | GREATER THAN | \u003c | LESS THAN | \u003e= | GREATER THAN OR EQUAL TO | \u003c= | LESS THAN OR EQUAL TO | \u003c\u003e } operand-2 Class condition: identifier [ IS ] [ NOT ] { NUMERIC | ALPHABETIC | ALPHABETIC-UPPER | ALPHABETIC-LOWER | class-name } Sign condition: identifier [ IS ] [ NOT ] { POSITIVE | NEGATIVE | ZERO } Condition name: condition-name Combined and negated: condition-1 { AND | OR } condition-2 ... NOT condition ( condition ) What it does A condition is a test that is either true or false. Conditions appear in IF , EVALUATE , PERFORM ... UNTIL and SEARCH ... WHEN . Relation conditions Compare two values. The symbols and the words mean the same: A \u003c B is A IS LESS THAN B . NOT reverses the test, so A NOT = B is true when they differ ( \u003c\u003e says the same thing). Numbers are compared by value, whatever their pictures: PIC 9(3) holding 005 equals PIC 9V99 holding 5.00. Text is compared character by character from the left, by the characters\u0027 codes. If the two sides are different lengths, the shorter one is treated as if padded with spaces on the right. So a PIC X(8) field holding \"ADA\" equals the literal \"ADA\" , and the same rule makes IF NAME = SPACES true for an all-blank field of any length. Either side may be an arithmetic expression : IF A + 1 \u003e B * 2 . Both sides may even be literals ( IF 1 \u003c 2 ), though such a test always gives the same answer. A figurative constant such as HIGH-VALUES , SPACES or ALL \"*\" is repeated to the length of the other side, so IF KEY-FIELD = HIGH-VALUES is true only when every byte of KEY-FIELD is a high value. Class conditions Test what kind of characters an item holds: Test True when NUMERIC Every character is a digit (a numeric item may also have a valid sign). Use it to check input before doing arithmetic with it. ALPHABETIC Every character is a letter (upper or lower case) or a space. ALPHABETIC-UPPER Every character is A-Z or a space. ALPHABETIC-LOWER Every character is a-z or a space. class-name Every character is in a CLASS you defined in SPECIAL-NAMES, such as CLASS HEX-DIGIT IS \"0\" THRU \"9\" \"A\" THRU \"F\" . Spaces count as alphabetic, so a blank field passes ALPHABETIC. Sign conditions POSITIVE is true for a value greater than zero, NEGATIVE for less than zero, and ZERO for exactly zero. Zero is neither positive nor negative. The item tested may be an arithmetic expression: IF BAL + 12 IS ZERO . Condition names A level 88 entry gives a name to one or more values of an item; the name itself is a condition: IF IS-MEMBER . See condition names . AND, OR, NOT and parentheses Join conditions with AND (both must be true) and OR (at least one must be true). NOT in front of a condition reverses it. Without parentheses, NOT is applied first, then AND , then OR , so A AND B OR C means (A AND B) OR C . Use parentheses whenever you mix AND and OR; they cost nothing and say what you mean. Abbreviated combined conditions When several relations test the same item, you can leave the item out after the first: IF CODE-X = 1 OR 3 OR 5 means IF CODE-X = 1 OR CODE-X = 3 OR CODE-X = 5 , and IF AGE \u003e 12 AND \u003c 20 means IF AGE \u003e 12 AND AGE \u003c 20 . The left side and the last operator written carry forward to each value that follows. IF A NOT = 1 OR 2 means A NOT = 1 OR A NOT = 2 , which is always true. To test \"A is neither 1 nor 2\" write IF NOT (A = 1 OR A = 2) , or use an 88-level name. Example: relation, class and sign conditions FIRST-NAME is eight characters holding \"ADA\" and five spaces; compared with the three-character literal \"ADA\", the literal is padded with spaces, so they are equal. \"ADA\" is less than \"BOB\" because A comes before B, and greater than \"AD\" because the padded \"AD \" has a space where \"ADA\" has an A. \"12A\" is not NUMERIC, but its first two characters are. FIRST-NAME is ALPHABETIC and ALPHABETIC-UPPER because its trailing spaces count as alphabetic. Example: combined conditions AGE \u003e 12 AND \u003c 20 and CODE-X = 1 OR 3 OR 5 are the abbreviated forms. In the last two IFs, CODE-X is 3, so CODE-X = 9 is false. Without parentheses the test is (CODE-X = 9 AND AGE = 99) OR IS-MEMBER , which is true because of IS-MEMBER. With the parentheses, the AND needs CODE-X = 9, so the whole test is false. Example: numbers by value, and the NOT trap WHOLE and WITH-CENTS have different pictures but the same value, so they are equal. A NOT = 1 OR 2 expands to A NOT = 1 OR A NOT = 2 ; A is 1, so the second half is true and the whole test passes. The version with parentheses says what was meant. Example: expressions, literals, a CLASS and HIGH-VALUES 11 is more than 8, and -12 + 12 is zero, so the first two tests are true. 1 \u003c 2 compares two literals and is always true. HEX-DIGIT is a class defined in SPECIAL-NAMES: every character of \"1F3A\" is in it, but the G in \"1G3A\" is not. LAST-KEY was filled with HIGH-VALUES, so it equals them, and any ordinary text compares less than HIGH-VALUES. See also IF , EVALUATE , condition names , arithmetic expressions , ENVIRONMENT DIVISION (CLASS), figurative constants ."},{"t":"Arithmetic expressions","u":"arithmetic-expressions.html","s":"Formulas built from numbers, data items and the operators + - * / **, as used in COMPUTE.","k":"expression formula operators plus minus times divide power exponent ** precedence parentheses unary minus intermediate results precision COMPUTE order of operations","c":"Using data","x":"Syntax operand [ { + | - | * | / | ** } operand ] ... operand: identifier | numeric-literal | FUNCTION function-name [ ( arguments ) ] | ( arithmetic-expression ) | { + | - } operand What it does An arithmetic expression is a formula: numbers and numeric data items joined by operators. Its main home is COMPUTE : COMPUTE PAY = HOURS * RATE . Expressions are also allowed in reference modification ( TEXT-1 (N - 2 : N + 1) ) and in conditions , so you can write IF A * 2 \u003e B + 10 or PERFORM UNTIL N * N \u003e 30 without first COMPUTEing the value into a field. Operator Meaning Example + add A + B - subtract (or, in front of one operand, negate) A - B , - A * multiply A * B / divide A / B ** raise to a power 2 ** 10 , 9 ** 0.5 (square root) Put a space on both sides of every operator. A-B is not \"A minus B\": a hyphen between letters makes it one name, A-B . Write A - B . Order of operations As in ordinary arithmetic: parentheses, innermost first; a sign in front of an operand (unary + or - ); ** ; * and / , left to right; + and - , left to right. So A + B * 2 multiplies first, and A - B - 1 is (A - B) - 1 . Several ** in a row are worked from the right in GnuCOBOL: 2 ** 3 ** 2 is 2 ** 9 , 512. When in doubt, add parentheses. Precision of the working values While an expression is worked out, COBOL keeps far more digits than any field holds, so A / B is not cut to the size of A or B. The answer is only fitted to the receiving field at the end: extra decimal places are dropped, or rounded if you write ROUNDED . Because of that, the order of operations still matters for division. 1 / 3 * 3 works out 1 / 3 as 0.3333... with many digits but not infinitely many, so the product is a little under 1 and a field with two decimals gets 0.99; with ROUNDED it gets 1.00. Multiplying first ( 1 * 3 / 3 ) avoids the problem. If the answer is too big for the receiving field, or you divide by zero, that is a size error; see COMPUTE for ON SIZE ERROR . Examples Each result is stored in R ( PIC S9(5)V99 ) and shown through an edited picture. A / B is 10 / 4 = 2.50. - A + 3 negates A first, giving -7. 9 ** 0.5 takes a square root. The last two lines show the truncated and the ROUNDED result of 1 / 3 * 3 . Expressions in conditions 20 is more than 14, and (10 - 4) × 3 is 18, so both IFs display. The loop adds 1 to N until N × N passes 30: 5 × 5 is 25, 6 × 6 is 36, so it stops at 6. A / B is 2.5 here, worked out exactly, not cut to a whole number, so the second WHEN is chosen. See also COMPUTE , ADD , SUBTRACT , MULTIPLY , DIVIDE , conditions , intrinsic functions ."},{"t":"ACCEPT","u":"accept.html","s":"Read a line of input, or get the date, the time, a command-line argument or an environment variable.","k":"input keyboard stdin FROM DATE YYYYMMDD DAY YYYYDDD DAY-OF-WEEK TIME COMMAND-LINE ARGUMENT-NUMBER ARGUMENT-VALUE ENVIRONMENT ENVIRONMENT-VALUE ON EXCEPTION END-ACCEPT read user input","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax Format 1: read a line of input ACCEPT identifier-1 [ END-ACCEPT ] Format 2: the date and time ACCEPT identifier-1 FROM { DATE [ YYYYMMDD ] | DAY [ YYYYDDD ] | DAY-OF-WEEK | TIME } [ END-ACCEPT ] Format 3: the command line and the environment ACCEPT identifier-1 FROM { COMMAND-LINE | ARGUMENT-NUMBER | ARGUMENT-VALUE | ENVIRONMENT literal-1 | ENVIRONMENT-VALUE } [ ON EXCEPTION imperative-statement-1 ] [ NOT ON EXCEPTION imperative-statement-2 ] [ END-ACCEPT ] Format 4: a field on the screen (see SCREEN SECTION) ACCEPT { identifier-1 AT LINE line-number COL column-number | screen-name } [ END-ACCEPT ] What it does ACCEPT puts a value into a data item from outside the program. Where the value comes from depends on the format. Reading input (Format 1) Plain ACCEPT identifier reads one line from standard input : what the user types at the keyboard, or a file fed to the program with \u003c on the command line. The line is then moved into the field the way MOVE would: Into an alphanumeric field ( PIC X ), text is placed from the left. A short line is padded with spaces; a long one is cut off on the right. Into a numeric field, the text is read as a number, so you can type 12.5 or -42 and the decimal point and sign go where the PICTURE says. When there is no more input (end of file), an alphanumeric field is filled with spaces. Nothing you type is shown by the program itself; the console echoes it as you type. To print a prompt on the same line as the answer, use DISPLAY ... WITH NO ADVANCING first. Date and time (Format 2) Phrase Gives Example DATE YYMMDD, 6 digits 261125 DATE YYYYMMDD YYYYMMDD, 8 digits 20261125 DAY YYDDD: year and day of the year 26329 DAY YYYYDDD YYYYDDD, 7 digits 2026329 DAY-OF-WEEK 1 digit, 1 = Monday ... 7 = Sunday 3 TIME HHMMSSCC: hours, minutes, seconds, hundredths 14302517 Use the four-digit-year forms in new programs. For more control (time zone, formatting) use the intrinsic functions such as FUNCTION CURRENT-DATE . Command line and environment (Format 3) COMMAND-LINE : everything typed after the program\u0027s name, as one string. ARGUMENT-NUMBER : how many arguments there are. ARGUMENT-VALUE : the next argument, one per ACCEPT. ON EXCEPTION runs when there are no more. ENVIRONMENT \"NAME\" : the value of environment variable NAME. ON EXCEPTION runs when it isn\u0027t set, and the field is filled with spaces. ENVIRONMENT-VALUE : the value of the variable named earlier by DISPLAY \"NAME\" UPON ENVIRONMENT-NAME . A program can set a variable for itself (and the programs it starts) with SET ENVIRONMENT \"NAME\" TO value ; see SET . Screens (Format 4) With AT LINE ... COL ... , or with the name of a screen from the SCREEN SECTION, ACCEPT lets the user type into fields on a full screen instead of reading a line. That is covered on the SCREEN SECTION page. Examples Reading lines of input The prompt and the name appear on one line because of WITH NO ADVANCING (the typed text isn\u0027t part of the output here, because it came from a file). 7 and 12.5 become numbers with leading zeros, -42 keeps its sign, and TOOLONGTEXT is cut to the five characters SHORT-CODE holds. The last ACCEPT finds no more input, so LEFT-OVER becomes spaces. Date and time The program checks each value instead of printing it, so its output is the same every day. In your own programs you would simply DISPLAY TODAY-8 . NOW-TIME is a group, so its parts can be used one by one after a single ACCEPT. Arguments and environment variables The program was run with no arguments, so ARGUMENT-NUMBER is 0 and the command line is blank. ACCEPT3_MODE was set by the program itself just before it was read. ACCEPT3_NOT_SET doesn\u0027t exist, so the exception branch runs and the field is cleared to spaces. Look at the END-DISPLAY after the first DISPLAY. Without it, the NOT ON EXCEPTION line would belong to that DISPLAY, not to the ACCEPT, because DISPLAY has exception phrases of its own; both messages would print. Close any statement that could take the next phrase with its END- word. SP COBOL notes Plain ACCEPT, the date and time forms, and the command-line and environment forms behave as in GnuCOBOL 3.2. The screen form follows SP COBOL\u0027s screen rules, which differ from GnuCOBOL in a few ways meant for beginners (the first key typed replaces a field\u0027s contents, for example); see SCREEN SECTION . See also DISPLAY , SCREEN SECTION , intrinsic functions (CURRENT-DATE and friends), SET ENVIRONMENT , MOVE for how values are placed in fields."},{"t":"ADD","u":"add.html","s":"Add numbers together and store the sum.","k":"TO GIVING ROUNDED SIZE ERROR END-ADD CORRESPONDING CORR sum plus addition","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax Format 1: add to each receiving field ADD { identifier-1 | literal-1 } ... TO { identifier-2 [ ROUNDED ] } ... [ ON SIZE ERROR imperative-statement-1 ] [ NOT ON SIZE ERROR imperative-statement-2 ] [ END-ADD ] Format 2: store the sum somewhere else ADD { identifier-1 | literal-1 } ... [ TO { identifier-2 | literal-2 } ] GIVING { identifier-3 [ ROUNDED ] } ... [ ON SIZE ERROR imperative-statement-1 ] [ NOT ON SIZE ERROR imperative-statement-2 ] [ END-ADD ] Format 3: add matching fields of two groups ADD { CORRESPONDING | CORR } group-1 TO group-2 [ ROUNDED ] [ ON SIZE ERROR imperative-statement-1 ] [ NOT ON SIZE ERROR imperative-statement-2 ] [ END-ADD ] What it does Format 1 adds every value before TO together, then adds that total to each field after TO . The fields after TO change; the ones before it don\u0027t. ADD A B TO C means \"C = C + A + B\". Format 2 adds up all the values (the ones after TO too) and puts the sum in the field after GIVING , replacing what was there. Nothing else changes. ADD A TO B GIVING C means \"C = A + B\". Because the GIVING field only receives the answer, it may be a numeric-edited field such as PIC $$,$$9.99 . Format 3 adds each numeric item of the first group to the item with the same name in the second group. Items whose names have no match are skipped. Decimals and rounding COBOL lines up the decimal points for you. If the answer has more decimal places than the receiving field can hold, the extra digits are dropped (truncated), unless you write ROUNDED after that field. SIZE ERROR If the answer has more digits in front of the decimal point than the receiving field can hold, that\u0027s a size error . Without ON SIZE ERROR , the high-order digits are lost silently: adding 5 to a PIC 9 holding 7 leaves 2. With ON SIZE ERROR , the field keeps its old value and your statements run instead. NOT ON SIZE ERROR runs when the answer fit. Write END-ADD when the ADD has a SIZE ERROR phrase and sits inside an IF or another statement, so it\u0027s clear where the phrase ends. Example The first ADD adds 19.95 and 4.50 to TOTAL, which started at zero. The second replaces TOTAL with 19.95 + 4.50. ROUGH has one decimal place, so 24.45 is rounded to 24.5. The fourth would make TINY 12, which doesn\u0027t fit in PIC 9 , so TINY keeps its value and the SIZE ERROR message is shown. The last ADD has no SIZE ERROR phrase, so the leading 1 of 12 is simply lost and TINY becomes 2. See also COMPUTE for whole formulas, SUBTRACT , MULTIPLY , DIVIDE , PICTURE for how many digits a field holds."},{"t":"ALTER","u":"alter.html","s":"Change where a GO TO jumps, while the program runs. Obsolete: use a flag instead.","k":"ALTER TO PROCEED TO GO TO obsolete self-modifying switch paragraph","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax ALTER procedure-name-1 TO [ PROCEED TO ] procedure-name-2 [ procedure-name-3 TO [ PROCEED TO ] procedure-name-4 ] ... What it does ALTER changes the target of a GO TO while the program is running. procedure-name-1 must be a paragraph that holds nothing but one GO TO sentence. After the ALTER, that GO TO jumps to procedure-name-2 instead of where its source code says. It was used in early COBOL as a switch: \"the first time through, go here; after that, go there\". The example below shows the classic pattern. Why not to use it ALTER was declared obsolete in the 1985 standard and removed from the 2002 standard. The problem is that the source code no longer tells the truth: GO TO FIRST-TIME may really go somewhere else, and the only way to know is to find every ALTER that names the paragraph and work out which ones have run. Programs that use it are very hard to read, test and change. You will meet it only in old code. Use a flag (a data item, usually with an 88-level condition name ) and an IF or EVALUATE instead. The decision is then written where it happens. Examples The classic ALTER switch GREET through GREET-EXIT is performed three times. The first time, GREET\u0027s GO TO FIRST-TIME goes where it says: the welcome prints, and the ALTER changes GREET so that from then on it goes to LATER-TIMES. The second and third times print \"WELCOME BACK\", although the source line still reads GO TO FIRST-TIME . The better way: a flag The same \"first time only\" greeting, written with a flag instead of ALTER: GREET is performed three times. The first time the flag is still \"Y\" , so the welcome prints and the flag is turned off; after that the ELSE branch runs. The output is the same as the ALTER version, and the decision can be read straight from the code. See also GO TO , IF , condition names , PERFORM ."},{"t":"CALL","u":"call.html","s":"Run another COBOL program as a subprogram, passing it data, and come back.","k":"subprogram USING BY REFERENCE BY CONTENT BY VALUE RETURNING ON EXCEPTION ON OVERFLOW NOT ON EXCEPTION END-CALL LINKAGE SECTION nested program COMMON GLOBAL module not found COB_LIBRARY_PATH CobolMainProgram","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax CALL { literal-1 | identifier-1 } [ USING { [ BY REFERENCE ] identifier-2 | BY CONTENT { identifier-3 | literal-2 } | BY VALUE { identifier-4 | literal-3 } } ... ] [ RETURNING identifier-5 ] [ ON EXCEPTION imperative-statement-1 ] [ NOT ON EXCEPTION imperative-statement-2 ] [ END-CALL ] ON OVERFLOW may be written in place of ON EXCEPTION. In the called program: PROCEDURE DIVISION [ USING { [ BY REFERENCE ] data-name-1 | BY VALUE data-name-2 } ... ] What it does CALL runs another program, the called program or subprogram, and continues with the next statement when it finishes (with GOBACK or EXIT PROGRAM ). The name is the called program\u0027s PROGRAM-ID , written as a literal ( CALL \"PAYROLL\" ) or held in an alphanumeric data item ( CALL PROG-NAME ), which lets the program decide at run time what to call. Passing data: USING The items after USING are the arguments . The called program describes them in its LINKAGE SECTION and lists them, in the same order, in its PROCEDURE DIVISION USING header. They are matched by position, not by name, and should have the same size and PICTURE on both sides. Phrase What the called program gets Can it change the caller\u0027s item? BY REFERENCE (the default) the caller\u0027s own item yes BY CONTENT a copy of the item or literal no: changes go to the copy BY VALUE the number itself, as a binary integer; the called program declares it BY VALUE too, usually as PIC S9(9) COMP-5 no A BY phrase applies to the items after it until the next BY . Getting a result back: RETURN-CODE and RETURNING Every program has a special register, RETURN-CODE . Whatever the called program leaves in it is available to the caller in RETURN-CODE after the CALL. CALL ... RETURNING identifier also stores that value in identifier . To pass back anything else (text, several values), change a BY REFERENCE argument. When the program can\u0027t be found: ON EXCEPTION If no program with that name exists, ON EXCEPTION (or its older spelling ON OVERFLOW ) runs instead. NOT ON EXCEPTION runs after a successful call. Without ON EXCEPTION, a missing program stops the run with an error message. Inside ON EXCEPTION , end a DISPLAY with END-DISPLAY : ON EXCEPTION DISPLAY \"missing\" NOT ON EXCEPTION ... attaches the NOT ON EXCEPTION to the DISPLAY, which has exception phrases of its own, not to the CALL. This is standard COBOL, and GnuCOBOL reads it the same way. Nested programs and COMMON A program can contain other programs, written before its END PROGRAM line. A nested program can be CALLed only by the program that directly contains it, unless it says IS COMMON in its PROGRAM-ID; a COMMON program can also be called by the other programs nested beside it. Items the outer program declares GLOBAL can be used by name in all the programs nested inside it. How SP COBOL finds the program It looks for a program with that PROGRAM-ID among everything already loaded: the main program, the other programs in its source file, and the other .cbl files of the project. An exact match comes first, then a match that ignores upper and lower case. Then it looks for NAME.dll beside the program, or in a folder listed in the COB_LIBRARY_PATH environment variable. In a Visual Studio project, every .cbl file is compiled. One becomes the main program: the one named by the CobolMainProgram property if you set it, else Program.cbl , else the only file, else the first file whose PROCEDURE DIVISION has no USING. The others become subprograms the main program can CALL. Examples Passing arguments The called program, CALLSUB1, is in a source file of its own, shown after the main program. The first CALL passes COUNTER by reference, COPY-ME by content and 5 by value. The subprogram adds 20 and 5 to COUNTER, so the caller sees 35. It also sets its copy of COPY-ME to 9999, which the caller never sees. It puts 35 in RETURN-CODE, and RETURNING RESULT picks that up. The second CALL names the program through PROG-NAME and adds 20 + 1 to COUNTER. NOSUCHPROG doesn\u0027t exist, so the ON EXCEPTION branch runs. The program sets RETURN-CODE back to 0 at the end, because a main program\u0027s RETURN-CODE becomes its exit code. Nested programs, COMMON and GLOBAL ADD-TEN and LOG-IT are nested inside CALL2. ADD-TEN can call LOG-IT only because LOG-IT is COMMON. LOG-IT reads and changes MESSAGE-TEXT without any USING, because CALL2 declared it GLOBAL. A program that isn\u0027t there Without ON EXCEPTION, the run stops at the CALL with exit code 1. The first message line is the same as GnuCOBOL\u0027s; the hint: line is SP COBOL\u0027s addition. SP COBOL notes After the \"module not found\" message, SP COBOL prints a hint: line saying what to check. GnuCOBOL doesn\u0027t. Set the environment variable COB_NO_HINTS=1 to turn hints off. Not supported: PROCEDURE DIVISION RETURNING in the called program. GnuCOBOL 3.2 doesn\u0027t implement it either. Pass results back through RETURN-CODE or a BY REFERENCE argument. CALL ... RETURNING ADDRESS OF , program pointers ( SET pointer TO ENTRY \"NAME\" and calling through them), and CALL STATIC . See also CANCEL , GOBACK , EXIT PROGRAM , program structure , GLOBAL and EXTERNAL , special registers (RETURN-CODE), DATA DIVISION (LINKAGE SECTION)."},{"t":"CANCEL","u":"cancel.html","s":"Reset a called program, so that the next CALL starts it with fresh WORKING-STORAGE.","k":"CANCEL CALL subprogram WORKING-STORAGE reset IS INITIAL INITIAL program fresh state","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax CANCEL { literal-1 | identifier-1 } ... What it does A called program keeps its WORKING-STORAGE between calls. If a subprogram counts how often it has been called, the count goes on growing from one CALL to the next, because its data stays in memory after GOBACK . CANCEL throws that state away. The next CALL to the program finds it as if for the first time: every WORKING-STORAGE item is back to its VALUE clause (or to its default if it has none). Name the program the same way as in CALL: a literal or a data item holding the PROGRAM-ID. You can cancel several programs in one statement. Cancelling a program that hasn\u0027t been called yet does nothing. IS INITIAL programs If a program should always start fresh, write IS INITIAL in its PROGRAM-ID paragraph: PROGRAM-ID. TAXCALC IS INITIAL. Its WORKING-STORAGE is reset on every CALL, with no CANCEL needed. Example The first CANCEL names a program that hasn\u0027t run yet, which is allowed and does nothing. CANCELSUB1 counts 1, 2 across the first two calls. After CANCEL \"CANCELSUB1\" its CALL-COUNT is back to its VALUE of 0, so the third call prints 1 again. CANCELSUB2 is an INITIAL program, so it prints 1 on every call without being cancelled. See also CALL , GOBACK , IDENTIFICATION DIVISION (PROGRAM-ID ... IS INITIAL), DATA DIVISION (WORKING-STORAGE and LOCAL-STORAGE), VALUE ."},{"t":"CLOSE","u":"close.html","s":"Finish with one or more files, writing out anything still waiting.","k":"CLOSE WITH LOCK REEL UNIT NO REWIND implicit CLOSE warning 42 38 07","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax CLOSE { file-name-1 [ { REEL | UNIT } | WITH LOCK | WITH NO REWIND ] } ... What it does CLOSE ends the program\u0027s use of a file. Anything still waiting to be written goes to disk, and the file is released so other programs (and a later OPEN in this one) can use it. One CLOSE can close several files: CLOSE IN-FILE OUT-FILE . After CLOSE the file can be opened again, in any mode. Closing a file that isn\u0027t open gives status 42. WITH LOCK CLOSE file WITH LOCK closes the file and stops this program from opening it again: a later OPEN gives status 38. REEL, UNIT and NO REWIND These phrases come from magnetic tape. On a disk file they don\u0027t apply: CLOSE REEL and CLOSE UNIT give status 07 and leave the file open ; CLOSE WITH NO REWIND closes it with status 07. Just use a plain CLOSE. Files left open Files still open when the program ends with STOP RUN are closed for you, but the runtime prints a warning for each one, to remind you to close them yourself. Example One OPEN opens IN-F for input and OUT-F for output, and one CLOSE closes both. Closing IN-F again gives 42. After CLOSE WITH LOCK , opening OUT-F gives 38. IN-F is opened again and never closed, so the program ends with the \"implicit CLOSE\" warning (it goes to the error output, standard error). SP COBOL notes The implicit CLOSE warning and the 07 status for REEL / UNIT are kept as GnuCOBOL 3.2 has them. See also OPEN , Files , FILE STATUS codes ."},{"t":"COMPUTE","u":"compute.html","s":"Work out an arithmetic expression and store the result.","k":"COMPUTE = EQUAL ROUNDED SIZE ERROR END-COMPUTE formula expression ** power divide by zero ROUNDED MODE","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax COMPUTE { identifier-1 [ ROUNDED [ MODE IS rounding-mode ] ] } ... { = | EQUAL } arithmetic-expression [ ON SIZE ERROR imperative-statement-1 ] [ NOT ON SIZE ERROR imperative-statement-2 ] [ END-COMPUTE ] What it does COMPUTE works out the formula on the right of = and stores the answer in the field (or fields) on the left. The formula can use + - * / , ** for \"to the power of\", parentheses, numeric fields, literals and numeric functions ; the rules are on the arithmetic expressions page. EQUAL is another way to write = . Use COMPUTE whenever a calculation has more than one step. COMPUTE TOTAL = PRICE * QTY * (1 + TAX-RATE) is one line, where ADD and MULTIPLY would need several statements and a work field. Several receiving fields You can list more than one field before the = . The formula is worked out once and the answer is stored in each field, following each field\u0027s own PICTURE and ROUNDED phrase. ROUNDED While working out the formula, COBOL keeps enough decimal places for an exact (or nearly exact) answer. When that answer is stored, extra decimal places that don\u0027t fit the field are dropped, unless you write ROUNDED after the field. ROUNDED rounds a final digit of 5 or more away from zero: 64.46 stored in one decimal place becomes 64.5, and -2.5 stored in a whole number becomes -3. ROUNDED MODE ROUNDED MODE IS picks another way of rounding for that field: Mode What it does 2.5 -2.5 2.1 NEAREST-AWAY-FROM-ZERO Nearest; a half goes away from zero. This is what plain ROUNDED does. 3 -3 2 NEAREST-EVEN Nearest; a half goes to the even digit (\"banker\u0027s rounding\"). 2 -2 2 NEAREST-TOWARD-ZERO Nearest; a half goes toward zero. 2 -2 2 AWAY-FROM-ZERO Any extra digits push the number away from zero. 3 -3 3 TRUNCATION Drop the extra digits (the same as no ROUNDED). 2 -2 2 TOWARD-GREATER Always up, toward the larger number. 3 -2 3 TOWARD-LESSER Always down, toward the smaller number. 2 -3 2 PROHIBITED The answer must fit exactly; if digits would be lost, it is a size error and the field keeps its old value. error error error The same phrase works on ADD , SUBTRACT , MULTIPLY and DIVIDE . SIZE ERROR A size error happens when the answer has more digits before the decimal point than the field can hold, or when the formula divides by zero. With ON SIZE ERROR , the field keeps its old value and your statements run. NOT ON SIZE ERROR runs when the answer was stored. Without the phrase, a too-big answer loses its high-order digits silently, and a division by zero leaves the field unchanged. Write END-COMPUTE when the COMPUTE has a SIZE ERROR phrase and sits inside another statement such as IF. Examples 19.99 × 3 × 1.075 is 64.46775. TOTAL has two decimal places, so it gets 64.46 (the rest is dropped). The second COMPUTE stores the same answer in two fields: CUT drops the extra digits, NEAR is ROUNDED to 64.5. 3 ** 4 is 81, which fits in PIC 99, so NOT ON SIZE ERROR runs. 3 ** 5 is 243, which doesn\u0027t fit, so SMALL keeps 81. Dividing by ZERO-QTY is a size error too, and AVERAGE keeps its value of 5. Rounding modes Each value is stored in five one-digit fields, each rounded its own way. Plain ROUNDED and NEAREST-EVEN differ only on an exact half: 2.5 goes up to 3 with ROUNDED but down to the even 2 with NEAREST-EVEN, while 3.5 goes to 4 both ways. AWAY-FROM-ZERO rounds 2.1 up to 3 because any leftover counts. UP (TOWARD-GREATER) and DOWN (TOWARD-LESSER) ignore the size of the leftover and look only at the direction, so -2.5 goes up to -2 and down to -3. SP COBOL notes Division by zero leaves the receiving field unchanged and raises SIZE ERROR, as in GnuCOBOL. See also arithmetic expressions , ADD , SUBTRACT , MULTIPLY , DIVIDE , intrinsic functions , PICTURE ."},{"t":"CONTINUE","u":"continue.html","s":"A statement that does nothing, for places where COBOL needs a statement but you have nothing to do.","k":"CONTINUE no operation do nothing placeholder empty branch IF ELSE WHEN NEXT SENTENCE","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax CONTINUE What it does CONTINUE does nothing, and the program carries on with whatever comes next. It exists because some places in COBOL must hold at least one statement: the branch of an IF , a WHEN of an EVALUATE , or the ON SIZE ERROR of an arithmetic statement. Writing CONTINUE there says \"nothing to do in this case\" on purpose. A common use is an IF whose interesting case is the ELSE: IF BALANCE \u003e= 0 CONTINUE ELSE ... . You could reverse the condition instead ( IF BALANCE \u003c 0 ), and often that reads better; CONTINUE is handy when the condition is easier to state the positive way. In an EVALUATE, a WHEN with CONTINUE catches a case that needs no action, so it doesn\u0027t fall into WHEN OTHER . CONTINUE is not the same as NEXT SENTENCE . CONTINUE goes on with the statement after the END-IF; NEXT SENTENCE jumps past the next period, which can skip statements you meant to run. The IF page shows the difference. Example BALANCE is negative, so the ELSE branch prints the message; had it been zero or more, CONTINUE would have done nothing. CODE-IN is \"B\" , whose WHEN does nothing, so neither \"CODE A\" nor \"UNKNOWN CODE\" appears and the program goes straight on to DONE. See also IF , EVALUATE , EXIT , PROCEDURE DIVISION (statements and sentences)."},{"t":"DELETE","u":"delete.html","s":"Remove a record from a relative or indexed file, or delete a whole file.","k":"DELETE RECORD INVALID KEY NOT INVALID KEY END-DELETE DELETE FILE remove record erase file I-O 23 43 49","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax Format 1: delete a record DELETE file-name [ RECORD ] [ INVALID KEY imperative-statement-1 ] [ NOT INVALID KEY imperative-statement-2 ] [ END-DELETE ] Format 2: delete files from the disk DELETE FILE file-name-1 ... What it does Format 1 removes one record from a RELATIVE or INDEXED file. (Records can\u0027t be removed from SEQUENTIAL or LINE SEQUENTIAL files; copy the file without them instead.) Like READ, it takes the file name. The file must be open I-O (status 49 otherwise). With sequential access , it deletes the record the last READ returned; a READ must come just before (status 43 otherwise). With random or dynamic access , it deletes the record whose key you set: the slot number in the RELATIVE KEY, or the value in the RECORD KEY. If there is no such record, the status is 23 and INVALID KEY runs. A deleted RELATIVE slot becomes empty: reading in order skips it, and a later WRITE can fill it again. Format 2 , DELETE FILE , erases the file from the disk. The file must be closed. Deleting a file that doesn\u0027t exist gives status 35. Example Task 2 is deleted. There is no task 7, so the second DELETE takes the INVALID KEY branch with status 23. Reading from slot 1 on shows only tasks 1 and 3. Finally DELETE FILE removes tasks.dat , and opening it for input gives 35. SP COBOL notes DELETE of an empty RELATIVE slot that lies inside the file gives 23 in SP COBOL; GnuCOBOL 3.2 reports 00, a bug in its file handler. See REWRITE for the same difference shown in an example. The format of SP COBOL\u0027s indexed files doesn\u0027t reuse the space of deleted records; it stays in the file until the file is copied (see Files ). See also REWRITE , READ , START , Files , FILE STATUS codes ."},{"t":"DISPLAY","u":"display.html","s":"Print literals and data items on the screen (standard output), or send them elsewhere with UPON.","k":"print output show write screen console WITH NO ADVANCING UPON SYSOUT SYSERR CONSOLE stderr END-DISPLAY ENVIRONMENT-NAME ENVIRONMENT-VALUE ARGUMENT-NUMBER COMMAND-LINE","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax Format 1: a line of output DISPLAY { identifier-1 | literal-1 } ... [ UPON { SYSOUT | CONSOLE | SYSERR } ] [ WITH NO ADVANCING ] [ END-DISPLAY ] Format 2: set the environment or the command line DISPLAY { identifier-1 | literal-1 } UPON { ENVIRONMENT-NAME | ENVIRONMENT-VALUE | ARGUMENT-NUMBER | COMMAND-LINE } [ END-DISPLAY ] Format 3: on the screen (see SCREEN SECTION) DISPLAY { identifier-1 AT LINE line-number COL column-number | screen-name } [ END-DISPLAY ] What it does DISPLAY writes the items you list, one after another with nothing in between, and then ends the line. To put a space between two items, list a \" \" literal between them. How items look Item Prints as Example a literal exactly as written (without the quotes) 3.14 PIC X its whole contents, trailing spaces included ADA unsigned PIC 9 every digit, leading zeros too, with a decimal point where the V is 0012.50 signed PIC S9 a + or - in front, then the digits -0042 COMP , COMP-3 as the number, the same way as a DISPLAY item of that PICTURE ( COMP-5 is the exception: it shows as many digits as its size can hold, 5 for 2 bytes, 10 for 4) +012.3 numeric-edited its edited characters, as stored 1,234.50- a group its raw bytes, all fields run together ABC007 To show a number the way people write it (no leading zeros, commas, a trailing minus), move it to a numeric-edited field first and DISPLAY that. WITH NO ADVANCING Leaves the cursor at the end of the output instead of starting a new line, so the next DISPLAY (or what the user types for an ACCEPT ) continues on the same line. Use it for prompts. UPON UPON SYSOUT and UPON CONSOLE mean the normal output, the same as no UPON phrase. UPON SYSERR writes to standard error , the stream meant for error messages. In a console window both appear on the screen; the difference shows when output is redirected to a file, since \u003e file catches only standard output. Format 2 doesn\u0027t print anything. DISPLAY \"NAME\" UPON ENVIRONMENT-NAME picks an environment variable and DISPLAY value UPON ENVIRONMENT-VALUE sets it (or use SET ENVIRONMENT ). UPON ARGUMENT-NUMBER chooses which command-line argument the next ACCEPT ... FROM ARGUMENT-VALUE reads. Screens With AT LINE ... COL ... , or the name of a screen from the SCREEN SECTION, DISPLAY draws on a full screen at a position, with colors. See SCREEN SECTION . Examples Each line shows one kind of item. CUST-NAME is PIC X(8) , so \"ADA\" is followed by five spaces before the \"!\". UNSIGNED-NUM shows its decimal point and leading zeros; the signed and packed items show a sign; the edited item shows exactly the characters it holds. The group prints its two fields run together. The two DISPLAYs with and without NO ADVANCING make one line. Standard error All three lines reach the screen. Had the program been run with DISPLAY2 \u003e out.txt , line 2 would still appear on the screen and only lines 1 and 3 would go into the file. SP COBOL notes DISPLAY of a national item ( PIC N ) prints its characters. GnuCOBOL 3.2 prints the raw UTF-16 bytes instead (a zero byte before each letter). See national data . A line feed inside the displayed data is written as CR LF on Windows, as GnuCOBOL does there. DISPLAY AT LINE/COL and screen DISPLAYs follow SP COBOL\u0027s screen rules (see SCREEN SECTION ); plain DISPLAY output matches GnuCOBOL byte for byte. See also ACCEPT , SCREEN SECTION , PICTURE (editing), USAGE , national data , literals ."},{"t":"DIVIDE","u":"divide.html","s":"Divide one number by another, optionally keeping the remainder.","k":"INTO BY GIVING REMAINDER ROUNDED SIZE ERROR END-DIVIDE division quotient divide by zero","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax Format 1: divide into a field, replacing it DIVIDE { identifier-1 | literal-1 } INTO { identifier-2 [ ROUNDED ] } ... [ ON SIZE ERROR imperative-statement-1 ] [ NOT ON SIZE ERROR imperative-statement-2 ] [ END-DIVIDE ] Format 2: store the quotient somewhere else DIVIDE { identifier-1 | literal-1 } { INTO | BY } { identifier-2 | literal-2 } GIVING { identifier-3 [ ROUNDED ] } ... [ ON SIZE ERROR imperative-statement-1 ] [ NOT ON SIZE ERROR imperative-statement-2 ] [ END-DIVIDE ] Format 3: quotient and remainder DIVIDE { identifier-1 | literal-1 } { INTO | BY } { identifier-2 | literal-2 } GIVING identifier-3 [ ROUNDED ] REMAINDER identifier-4 [ ON SIZE ERROR imperative-statement-1 ] [ NOT ON SIZE ERROR imperative-statement-2 ] [ END-DIVIDE ] What it does The word between the two values says which one is divided: DIVIDE A INTO B means B / A (\"A goes into B\"). DIVIDE A BY B means A / B. BY needs GIVING. Format 1 divides each field after INTO by the first value and stores the answer back in that field. Format 2 leaves both values alone and stores the answer, the quotient , in the field after GIVING , which may be a numeric-edited field. Format 3 also stores the remainder : what is left over, dividend minus quotient times divisor. DIVIDE 60 INTO 125 GIVING HOURS REMAINDER MINS gives 2 hours and 5 minutes. Decimals and ROUNDED The quotient is cut to the decimal places of its field, unless you write ROUNDED , which rounds a last digit of 5 or more away from zero. For the REMAINDER, COBOL uses the quotient cut to its field\u0027s decimal places, before any rounding. With a negative dividend the remainder is negative too: -17 / 5 gives a quotient of -3 and a remainder of -2. SIZE ERROR and dividing by zero A size error happens when the quotient (or remainder) has more digits before the decimal point than its field holds, and whenever the divisor is zero. With ON SIZE ERROR the receiving fields keep their old values and your statements run instead. Dividing by zero never stores anything, with or without the phrase, so test for it. Examples 100 / 4 is 25. DIVIDE 2 INTO QUARTER (Format 1) halves QUARTER in place: 12.5, cut to 12 because QUARTER has no decimal places. 100 / 7 is 14 with 2 left over (14 × 7 = 98). The ROUNDED quotient 14.2857... becomes 14.29. 125 minutes is 2 hours and 5 minutes. 100 / 8 is 12.5, too many digits for PIC 9 , so TINY keeps 5. DIVIDE 0 INTO SHARE is a division by zero, a size error, so SHARE keeps the 14 it had. The remainder with a ROUNDED quotient 20 / 3 is 6.67. The first DIVIDE stores 6 and a remainder of 20 - 6 × 3 = 2. The second rounds the quotient to 7, but the remainder is still 2: it is worked out from the quotient before rounding (6), cut to the quotient field\u0027s decimal places, not from the rounded 7. The last line shows the signs with a negative dividend. SP COBOL notes As in GnuCOBOL, the remainder uses the quotient truncated to the quotient field\u0027s decimal places, even when the quotient is ROUNDED. See also COMPUTE (division with / ), MULTIPLY , ADD , SUBTRACT , FUNCTION MOD and REM ."},{"t":"EVALUATE","u":"evaluate.html","s":"Choose one of several actions by comparing values or testing conditions; COBOL\u0027s case statement.","k":"EVALUATE WHEN OTHER ALSO THRU THROUGH TRUE FALSE ANY NOT END-EVALUATE case switch select multiway decision table","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax EVALUATE subject-1 [ ALSO subject-2 ] ... { { WHEN object-1 [ ALSO object-2 ] ... } ... imperative-statement-1 } ... [ WHEN OTHER imperative-statement-2 ] [ END-EVALUATE ] subject: { identifier | literal | arithmetic-expression | condition | TRUE | FALSE } object: { ANY | condition | TRUE | FALSE | [ NOT ] { identifier-1 | literal-1 | arithmetic-expression-1 } [ { THRU | THROUGH } { identifier-2 | literal-2 | arithmetic-expression-2 } ] } What it does EVALUATE looks at a value (the subject ) and runs the statements under the first WHEN that matches it. Only one WHEN runs; then the program goes on after END-EVALUATE . If no WHEN matches, the statements under WHEN OTHER run, or nothing at all if there is no WHEN OTHER. It does the job of a long chain of IF ... ELSE IF ... , and is much easier to read. What a WHEN can say A value: WHEN \"A\" , WHEN 100 , WHEN LIMIT-AMT . Matches when the subject equals it. A range: WHEN 90 THRU 100 matches anything from 90 to 100, both ends included. NOT before a value or range: matches when the subject is anything else. ANY : matches every value. It is most useful with ALSO, to say \"this subject doesn\u0027t matter here\" ( WHEN 0 THRU 12 ALSO ANY ). With EVALUATE TRUE , each WHEN is a condition ( WHEN AGE \u003c 18 , WHEN IS-MEMBER ), and the first one that is true is chosen. With EVALUATE FALSE , the first condition that is false is chosen. Several WHENs sharing one action Write the WHENs one after another with no statement between them. The statements after the last one run when any of them matches. ALSO: more than one subject EVALUATE AGE ALSO MEMBER-FLAG tests two things at once. Each WHEN then has one object per subject, separated by ALSO , and matches only when every pair matches. This is a decision table written as code. Subjects may be of different kinds: EVALUATE TRUE ALSO QTY takes a condition and then a value in each WHEN. Examples Each score is checked against the ranges in order. 72 and 61 both reach the shared action of the two WHENs for 70-79 and 60-69. 40 matches no range, so WHEN OTHER runs. Two subjects, and EVALUATE TRUE The first EVALUATE is a small decision table on age and membership. A 70-year-old non-member matches 65 THRU 999 ALSO \"N\" . A 40-year-old non-member matches nothing and gets the OTHER branch. The second EVALUATE tests conditions in order: the 10-year-old is caught by the first WHEN, so the member test is never reached for that row. EVALUATE FALSE, and NOT in a WHEN In the first EVALUATE each WHEN fires when its condition is false . Both conditions are true here, so WHEN OTHER runs. In the second, QTY 5 is in the range 1 THRU 9, so the first WHEN matches; a QTY of 0 or 10 or more would have matched NOT 1 THRU 9 . WHEN ANY The ticket table again, shorter. A child pays the child price whether or not they are a member, so the first WHEN says ALSO ANY instead of listing \"Y\" and \"N\". Any member older than 12 then matches ANY ALSO \"Y\" , because the children were already caught by the first WHEN. The 70-year-old non-member gets the senior ticket. See also IF , conditions , condition names (88 levels) , CONTINUE (a WHEN that does nothing), PERFORM ."},{"t":"EXIT","u":"exit.html","s":"Leave a paragraph, a section, a PERFORM loop or a called program early; or mark the end of a PERFORM THRU range.","k":"EXIT PARAGRAPH EXIT SECTION EXIT PERFORM CYCLE EXIT PROGRAM exit paragraph break continue loop leave early return","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax EXIT EXIT PARAGRAPH EXIT SECTION EXIT PERFORM [ CYCLE ] EXIT PROGRAM What it does Statement Effect EXIT Nothing. It marks a paragraph that exists only to be the end of a PERFORM ... THRU range or the target of a GO TO. EXIT PARAGRAPH Skip the rest of the current paragraph, as if its end had been reached. EXIT SECTION Skip the rest of the current section, including any paragraphs after this one in it. EXIT PERFORM Leave the inline PERFORM ... END-PERFORM loop it is in; the program goes on after END-PERFORM. Like break in other languages. EXIT PERFORM CYCLE Skip the rest of this pass through the inline loop and start the next one (the VARYING item is stepped and the UNTIL test made as usual). Like continue in other languages. EXIT PROGRAM In a called program, return to the caller. In a main program it does nothing. See GOBACK . EXIT as an exit paragraph Older code performs a range of paragraphs, PERFORM CHECK-IT THRU CHECK-IT-EXIT , and leaves early with GO TO CHECK-IT-EXIT . The last paragraph holds only EXIT. because a paragraph must contain a statement. EXIT PARAGRAPH and EXIT SECTION do the same job without the extra paragraph or the GO TO, and are the better choice in new code. EXIT PERFORM and EXIT PERFORM CYCLE belong inside an inline PERFORM (one with END-PERFORM). To leave an out-of-line PERFORM early, use EXIT PARAGRAPH or EXIT SECTION in the performed code. Example In the loop, EXIT PERFORM CYCLE skips the DISPLAY when I is 2, and EXIT PERFORM ends the loop when I reaches 4, so only 1 and 3 print. AGE is 15, so CHECK-AGE leaves through EXIT PARAGRAPH, CHECK-SECT leaves the whole section (CHECK-SECT-2 never runs) through EXIT SECTION, and OLD-STYLE does the same thing the old way, with GO TO to a paragraph holding EXIT. None of the \"NOT SHOWN\" lines print. See also PERFORM , GO TO , GOBACK , STOP RUN , PROCEDURE DIVISION (paragraphs and sections)."},{"t":"GENERATE","u":"generate.html","s":"Print a Report Writer detail line, with any headings, footings and page breaks it triggers.","k":"report writer detail line summary reporting control break print report-name","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax Format 1: print a detail line GENERATE detail-group-name Format 2: summary reporting (totals only) GENERATE report-name What it does GENERATE is the statement that makes a Report Writer report grow. Before it, move the values for the line into the fields the report\u0027s SOURCE, SUM and CONTROL clauses name. GENERATE then does, in order, whatever is due: On the first GENERATE after INITIATE : the report heading and the first page heading. If a control field changed since the last GENERATE: the control footings (with their totals) for the old value, then the control headings for the new one. If the lines won\u0027t fit on this page: the page footing, a new page, and its page heading. The detail line itself (Format 1), and adding the values into the SUM counters. Detail reporting and summary reporting Format 1 names a TYPE DETAIL group and prints it. Format 2 names the report itself. It does everything in the list above except print a detail line: the totals still add up and the headings and footings still print. That\u0027s called summary reporting : a report of totals only, from the same RD. Example The same data goes through the same report twice. In pass 1, GENERATE BOX-LINE prints a line for every record, and a TOTAL line each time WS-FRUIT changes. In pass 2, GENERATE FRUIT-REPORT prints no detail lines, only the totals, which come out the same. See also Report Writer , INITIATE , TERMINATE , SUPPRESS ."},{"t":"GO TO","u":"go-to.html","s":"Jump to another paragraph or section, without coming back.","k":"GO TO GOTO jump branch DEPENDING ON computed goto PERFORM THRU exit paragraph spaghetti structured programming","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax Format 1 GO TO procedure-name-1 Format 2 GO TO { procedure-name-1 } ... DEPENDING ON identifier-1 What it does GO TO sends the program to the start of another paragraph or section. Unlike PERFORM , it doesn\u0027t come back: the program simply carries on from the new place, and falls into the paragraphs that follow it in the source. Format 2 picks a target by number. GO TO P-ONE P-TWO P-THREE DEPENDING ON K goes to P-ONE when K is 1, P-TWO when K is 2, and P-THREE when K is 3. For any other value of K (zero, negative, or more than the number of names) nothing happens and the next statement runs. K must be an integer. When you will see it GO TO was the main way to control the flow of early COBOL programs. Today it survives mostly in one pattern: a PERFORM PARA THRU PARA-EXIT range where GO TO PARA-EXIT leaves early, and the exit paragraph holds only EXIT . A GO TO that stays inside the range being performed is safe. Why structured code avoids it A program full of GO TOs is hard to follow, because to know how you reached a paragraph you have to search for every GO TO that names it. A GO TO that jumps out of a PERFORMed range can also leave the PERFORM unfinished, which causes confusing bugs later. Modern COBOL has a structured way to do each job: loops: PERFORM ... UNTIL and PERFORM VARYING choices: IF and EVALUATE (which also replaces GO TO DEPENDING ON) leaving early: EXIT PARAGRAPH , EXIT SECTION , EXIT PERFORM Example The loop performs PICK THRU PICK-EXIT four times. For K = 1, 2 and 3, GO TO DEPENDING ON jumps to P-ONE, P-TWO or P-THREE. Each of those jumps on to PICK-EXIT (P-THREE just falls into it, since it comes next). For K = 4 there is no fourth name, so the statement after the GO TO runs. After the loop, a plain GO TO moves to FINISH, which ends the run. See also PERFORM , EXIT , EVALUATE , ALTER (an obsolete way to change a GO TO\u0027s target), PROCEDURE DIVISION ."},{"t":"GOBACK","u":"goback.html","s":"End the current program: return to the caller from a subprogram, or end the run from the main program.","k":"GOBACK STOP RUN EXIT PROGRAM return end program subprogram main program RETURN-CODE exit code","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax GOBACK What it does GOBACK ends the program it is in. Where control goes depends on how that program was started: In a program that was CALL ed, it returns to the caller, which carries on after its CALL. In the main program (the one the run started with), it ends the run, like STOP RUN. That makes GOBACK the safe choice for ending any program: it does the right thing whether the program is run on its own or called by another. GOBACK, STOP RUN and EXIT PROGRAM compared Statement In the main program In a called program GOBACK ends the run returns to the caller STOP RUN ends the run ends the whole run, caller and all EXIT PROGRAM does nothing; the next statement runs returns to the caller When the run ends, the main program\u0027s RETURN-CODE becomes the program\u0027s exit code, which a batch file or script can test. Example The three called programs are in one source file, shown after the main program. GOBACKSUB1 returns with GOBACK and GOBACKSUB2 with EXIT PROGRAM; both times GOBACK1 carries on. The EXIT PROGRAM in GOBACK1 itself is ignored, because GOBACK1 is the main program. GOBACKSUB3 uses STOP RUN, which ends everything, so \"NEVER SHOWN\" never prints. See also STOP RUN , EXIT PROGRAM , CALL , CANCEL , special registers (RETURN-CODE)."},{"t":"IF","u":"if.html","s":"Run statements only when a condition is true, with an optional ELSE for when it is false.","k":"IF ELSE END-IF THEN NEXT SENTENCE CONTINUE nested if condition decision period scope terminator","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax IF condition [ THEN ] { statement-1 ... | NEXT SENTENCE } [ ELSE { statement-2 ... | NEXT SENTENCE } ] [ END-IF ] What it does IF tests a condition . When it is true, the statements after it run; when it is false, the statements after ELSE run (or nothing, if there is no ELSE). Either way, the program then goes on after END-IF . A branch can hold any number of statements, including other IFs. Each IF inside another one is nested , and needs its own END-IF; an ELSE belongs to the nearest IF before it that doesn\u0027t already have one. Indent each level so the structure is easy to see, but remember that COBOL ignores the indentation: only END-IF and periods decide where an IF ends. Conditions can compare values ( BALANCE \u003c 0 ), test a class ( QTY IS NUMERIC ), test a sign ( AMOUNT IS NEGATIVE ), use an 88-level condition name , and be combined with AND , OR and NOT . See conditions . Doing nothing in a branch: CONTINUE and NEXT SENTENCE A branch must hold something. CONTINUE means \"nothing here\", and the program goes on after the END-IF. NEXT SENTENCE is older and different: it jumps to just after the next period in the source, skipping everything up to it, even statements after the END-IF. Use CONTINUE. The period trap A period ends a sentence , and it ends every IF that is still open, however deeply nested. In older code without END-IF, the period was how an IF was closed. If you put a period at the end of a line inside an IF by mistake, the IF stops there and the lines after it run every time, though the indentation says otherwise. The safe habit: close every IF with END-IF, and put periods only at the end of a paragraph. Examples For 750 the outer condition is false, so the ELSE runs and the nested IF finds it over the limit. For 120 the nested IF\u0027s ELSE prints OK. For -30 the first branch runs and the nested IF is never reached. CONTINUE, NEXT SENTENCE and the period trap N is 5. With CONTINUE, the program goes on after END-IF and prints \"AFTER END-IF\". With NEXT SENTENCE, it jumps past the next period, so \"AFTER END-IF\" is skipped and the program resumes at the next sentence. In the last part, N \u003e 7 is false, and \"BIG\" is not shown. But the period after DISPLAY \" BIG\" ended the IF, so the next DISPLAY isn\u0027t part of it and runs anyway. See also conditions , condition names , EVALUATE (for many cases), CONTINUE , PROCEDURE DIVISION (statements and sentences)."},{"t":"INITIALIZE","u":"initialize.html","s":"Reset the fields of a group, record or table: spaces in text fields, zeros in numeric ones.","k":"INITIALIZE REPLACING ALPHANUMERIC NUMERIC NUMERIC-EDITED ALPHANUMERIC-EDITED WITH FILLER ALL TO VALUE THEN TO DEFAULT clear reset blank zero record table","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax INITIALIZE identifier-1 ... [ WITH FILLER ] [ { ALL | category } TO VALUE ] [ REPLACING { category [ DATA ] BY { identifier-2 | literal-1 } } ... ] [ THEN TO DEFAULT ] category: { ALPHABETIC | ALPHANUMERIC | ALPHANUMERIC-EDITED | NUMERIC | NUMERIC-EDITED | NATIONAL | NATIONAL-EDITED } What it does INITIALIZE sets every elementary item inside identifier-1 to a starting value chosen by its category. On its own, with no phrases: Kind of field Becomes alphabetic, alphanumeric, alphanumeric-edited ( PIC A , X ) spaces numeric ( PIC 9 , any USAGE) zero numeric-edited ( PIC ZZ9.99 , $$$9.99 ) zero, edited through its PICTURE FILLER items are left alone unless you write WITH FILLER . Every occurrence of a table (OCCURS) is initialized. You can also name a single elementary field. INITIALIZE is clearer than MOVE SPACES TO record , which also blanks the numeric fields, leaving them invalid for arithmetic. The phrases REPLACING category BY value : fields of that category get value , moved as by MOVE . Fields of the categories you don\u0027t name are not changed (add THEN TO DEFAULT to give them spaces and zeros). A numeric-edited field is not NUMERIC for this purpose; name NUMERIC-EDITED to replace it. WITH FILLER : initialize FILLER items too. ALL TO VALUE : fields that have a VALUE clause get that value back. Fields without one are not changed. THEN TO DEFAULT : after the other phrases, fields they didn\u0027t set get the usual space or zero. The standard says NUMERIC TO VALUE (a category instead of ALL) restores only the VALUE clauses of that category. GnuCOBOL 3.2, and SP COBOL with it, restores the VALUE clauses of every category either way, so write ALL TO VALUE and avoid the surprise. Examples With REPLACING, the alphanumeric field gets \"?\" (padded with spaces, as a MOVE would) and the numeric ones 7. CUST-SHOW is numeric-edited, a category the REPLACING phrase doesn\u0027t name, so it keeps $12.00. The plain INITIALIZE then blanks CUST-NAME, zeros the numeric fields, and gives CUST-SHOW the edited form of zero. INITIALIZE works on a single field as well. In the table, all three occurrences get spaces and zeros. FILLER, VALUE and DEFAULT PART-REC has a FILLER holding \"-*-\" . A plain INITIALIZE leaves the FILLER alone; WITH FILLER blanks it too. ALL TO VALUE puts back the VALUE clauses of PART-CODE and PART-QTY, but not the FILLER\u0027s, since FILLER was not included this time; PART-NOTE has no VALUE and is not touched. The last statement, after the record was filled with X\u0027s, restores every VALUE (FILLER included) and then gives PART-NOTE, which has no VALUE, its default of spaces. See also MOVE , VALUE , OCCURS (tables), PICTURE (categories), REDEFINES ."},{"t":"INITIATE","u":"initiate.html","s":"Start a Report Writer report, resetting its page and line counters and its totals.","k":"report writer start report begin PAGE-COUNTER LINE-COUNTER RD","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax INITIATE report-name-1 ... What it does INITIATE gets a report (an RD in the REPORT SECTION ) ready to print. It sets PAGE-COUNTER to 1 and LINE-COUNTER to 0, sets every SUM counter to zero, and forgets the previous values of the control fields. It doesn\u0027t print anything: the report heading and the first page heading appear at the first GENERATE . The report\u0027s file must already be open for output ( OPEN OUTPUT ). You can name several reports in one INITIATE. Once a report has been ended with TERMINATE , you may INITIATE it again to print it a second time, in the same file or after reopening it. Example Straight after INITIATE, PAGE-COUNTER is 1 and LINE-COUNTER is 0, and nothing has been printed yet. The report is then printed twice. The second INITIATE starts the page number at 1 again and the TOTAL starts from zero, so it shows 22, not 137. Each run is padded to its 6-line page. See also Report Writer , GENERATE , TERMINATE , OPEN ."},{"t":"INSPECT","u":"inspect.html","s":"Count, replace or convert characters inside a field.","k":"TALLYING REPLACING CONVERTING CHARACTERS ALL LEADING FIRST TRAILING BEFORE AFTER INITIAL count characters replace text upper case translate","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax Format 1: count INSPECT identifier-1 TALLYING { identifier-2 FOR { CHARACTERS [ phrase ] ... | { ALL | LEADING | TRAILING } { identifier-3 | literal-1 } [ phrase ] ... } ... } ... Format 2: replace INSPECT identifier-1 REPLACING { CHARACTERS BY { identifier-4 | literal-2 } [ phrase ] ... | { ALL | LEADING | FIRST | TRAILING } { { identifier-3 | literal-1 } BY { identifier-4 | literal-2 } [ phrase ] ... } ... } ... Format 3: count, then replace INSPECT identifier-1 TALLYING ... REPLACING ... Format 4: convert character by character INSPECT identifier-1 CONVERTING { identifier-5 | literal-3 } TO { identifier-6 | literal-4 } [ phrase ] ... where phrase is: { BEFORE | AFTER } INITIAL { identifier-7 | literal-5 } What it does INSPECT looks through the characters of identifier-1 from left to right. It can count what it finds ( TALLYING ), change what it finds ( REPLACING ), or swap characters one for one ( CONVERTING ). TALLYING Each FOR phrase adds to a counter, identifier-2 , which must be a numeric item. INSPECT adds to the counter; it doesn\u0027t clear it first, so set it to zero yourself before the INSPECT. CHARACTERS counts every character (inside the BEFORE/AFTER region, if you give one). ALL \"x\" counts every place the text appears. LEADING \"x\" counts the text only where it repeats right at the start of the field (or the region). TRAILING \"x\" counts it where it repeats at the very end. This is a GnuCOBOL extension, and handy for counting the trailing spaces of a field. REPLACING Each phrase says what to look for and what to put in its place. The replacement must be the same length as the text it replaces, because INSPECT never changes the length of the field. CHARACTERS BY \"x\" replaces every character, ALL every match, LEADING the matches at the start, FIRST only the first match, and TRAILING (an extension) the matches at the end. With both TALLYING and REPLACING in one statement, the counting is done first, on the original text, and then the replacing. CONVERTING CONVERTING \"abc\" TO \"xyz\" changes every a to x , every b to y and every c to z , in one pass. The two strings must be the same length. It is the usual way to change letters to upper case (see also FUNCTION UPPER-CASE ). BEFORE and AFTER INITIAL A phrase can be limited to part of the field. BEFORE INITIAL \",\" means \"only look at the characters in front of the first comma\"; AFTER INITIAL \":\" means \"only look at the characters after the first colon\". If the comma or colon isn\u0027t there at all, BEFORE covers the whole field and AFTER covers nothing. You may give both, one BEFORE and one AFTER, on the same phrase. When phrases compete With several phrases in one INSPECT, it helps to know exactly how the scan works: INSPECT moves along the field one position at a time. At each position it tries the phrases in the order you wrote them. The first phrase that matches there wins: it counts or replaces, and the scan jumps past the characters it matched. Later phrases never see those characters. So the order matters. Counting ALL \"AB\" before ALL \"A\" gives different numbers from the other way round (the second example shows this). Replaced characters are not looked at again: replacing X by B and then B by Y doesn\u0027t turn an X into a Y. BEFORE and AFTER look for their delimiter in the text as it was before the INSPECT began, so replacing can\u0027t move the region\u0027s edge. Examples The field holds \" BANANA, PAPAYA, MANGO\" followed by seven spaces (it is 30 characters long). It has seven A\u0027s and two leading spaces, and eight characters come before the first comma. In AMOUNT, only the zeros at the front are replaced, so the zero in .50 stays. The phone number shows a combined TALLYING and REPLACING, then FIRST , which changes only one 5, then CHARACTERS limited to the part after the first period. Finally CONVERTING turns the lower-case letters into capitals. The text is ABAABA plus four spaces. When \"AB\" is listed first, the A\u0027s that start an AB are counted as AB and skipped, so each counter gets 2. With \"A\" first, every A is taken by that phrase and AB never matches. In the third INSPECT both phrases add to the same counter: 4 A\u0027s plus 6 other characters is 10, every character counted exactly once. The X\u0027s become B\u0027s, but the B\u0027s made that way are not turned into Y\u0027s; only the original B is. In the last one the four A\u0027s in front of the colon become colons, and the region still ends at the original colon. SP COBOL notes SP COBOL\u0027s INSPECT follows GnuCOBOL\u0027s own algorithm step for step, so programs that depend on the finer points (competing phrases, regions, a signed number field, which is inspected without its sign) give the same results as GnuCOBOL 3.2. See also STRING and UNSTRING for joining and splitting text, reference modification for working with part of a field, intrinsic functions such as UPPER-CASE, TRIM and REVERSE."},{"t":"JSON GENERATE","u":"json-generate.html","s":"Turn a group item and its fields into JSON text.","k":"JSON GENERATE FROM COUNT IN NAME OF OMITTED SUPPRESS ON EXCEPTION JSON-CODE END-JSON web API JavaScript object notation","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax JSON GENERATE identifier-1 FROM identifier-2 [ COUNT IN identifier-3 ] [ NAME OF { identifier-4 IS { literal-1 | OMITTED } } ... ] [ SUPPRESS identifier-5 ... ] [ ON EXCEPTION imperative-statement-1 ] [ NOT ON EXCEPTION imperative-statement-2 ] [ END-JSON ] What it does JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is the text format most web services use to exchange data. It looks like {\"name\":\"value\",\"count\":42} . JSON GENERATE takes the data item after FROM , usually a group, and writes it as JSON into the alphanumeric field identifier-1 . How the data becomes JSON A group becomes an object, {\"GROUP-NAME\":{...}} , with one member per item inside it, in order. Groups inside it become objects inside the object. The member names are the COBOL data names, exactly as written (upper case, with hyphens). Numeric items become JSON numbers: leading zeros are dropped, the sign and decimal point are shown ( 00042 becomes 42 , -123.45 stays as it is). Alphanumeric items become JSON strings, with trailing spaces removed. A field of all spaces becomes a string of one space. Quotes and backslashes are escaped ( \\\" ). FILLER items are left out. The JSON text goes at the start of the output field, and is usually shorter than the field. Use COUNT IN to get that length, and refer to JSON-OUT (1:LEN) to use just the JSON. NAME OF and SUPPRESS NAME OF CUST-ID IS \"id\" uses a name of your choosing instead of the COBOL name, which matters when the receiving program expects lower-case names. NAME OF CUSTOMER IS OMITTED , for the group after FROM, leaves out the outer wrapper so the result is just {\"id\":42,...} . SUPPRESS CITY ACTIVE leaves those items out of the output. JSON-CODE and ON EXCEPTION After the statement, the special register JSON-CODE is zero if all went well. If the output field is too small, JSON-CODE is set to 1, the field holds as much of the text as fit, and the ON EXCEPTION statements run. NOT ON EXCEPTION runs when the statement succeeded. Example The first JSON GENERATE writes the whole CUSTOMER group: CUST-ID loses its leading zeros, the quotes inside the name are escaped, CITY (all spaces) becomes \" \" and the FILLER is skipped. The second renames two members, omits the outer CUSTOMER wrapper and suppresses CITY and ACTIVE. The third writes into a 20-character field, which is too small, so JSON-CODE is 1 and the field holds the first 20 characters. SP COBOL notes SP COBOL writes JSON itself, so no extra library has to be installed (GnuCOBOL uses the cJSON library for this). The text is the same as GnuCOBOL 3.2 produces, character for character. A table (an item with OCCURS ) is not written as a JSON array: only its first entry is generated, as a single object. The compiler warns that this is \"not implemented\". This is GnuCOBOL 3.2\u0027s behavior, kept so that results match. If you need an array, build that part of the text yourself with STRING . See also XML GENERATE , STRING , special registers (JSON-CODE), reference modification ."},{"t":"MERGE","u":"merge.html","s":"Combine files that are already in key order into one file in that order.","k":"MERGE SD ASCENDING DESCENDING KEY USING GIVING OUTPUT PROCEDURE RETURN combine files merge sorted files","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax MERGE sort-file-name { ON { ASCENDING | DESCENDING } KEY data-name-1 ... } ... [ COLLATING SEQUENCE IS alphabet-name ] USING file-name-1 file-name-2 ... { GIVING file-name-3 ... | OUTPUT PROCEDURE IS procedure-1 [ { THRU | THROUGH } procedure-2 ] } What it does MERGE takes two or more files whose records are each already in order by the same keys, and combines them into one stream in that order. Think of two sorted piles of cards dealt into one sorted pile. It works like SORT : you need a sort file described with SD , whose record holds the key fields, and the KEY phrases name them. The USING files are opened, read and closed by MERGE, and must not be open. There is no INPUT PROCEDURE: the input always comes from the USING files. The result goes either to a file ( GIVING ), or to an OUTPUT PROCEDURE that fetches the records one at a time with RETURN . When records in different files have equal keys, the one from the file named first in USING comes first. Each input file must already be in order by the merge keys; sort it first if it isn\u0027t. With COLLATING SEQUENCE IS alphabet-name the keys are compared in the order of that ALPHABET instead of ASCII order, as on SORT . Examples Monday\u0027s and Tuesday\u0027s files are each in account order. The merged file is too. Account A100 appears in both; Monday\u0027s record comes first because MONDAY-F is named first. The output procedure RETURNs the merged sales in date order and prints a total each time the date changes, plus one for the last date. Each shop\u0027s orders are in size order S, M, L, X, which isn\u0027t ASCII order, so the MERGE names the alphabet SIZES with COLLATING SEQUENCE . Both size-S orders come first, shop A\u0027s before shop B\u0027s because SHOP1-F is named first. See also SORT , RETURN , Files ."},{"t":"MOVE","u":"move.html","s":"Copy a value into one or more fields, converting it to fit each one.","k":"TO CORRESPONDING CORR copy assign assignment padding truncation justify edited de-editing figurative SPACES ZEROS","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax Format 1: copy a value MOVE { identifier-1 | literal-1 } TO identifier-2 ... Format 2: copy matching fields of two groups MOVE { CORRESPONDING | CORR } group-1 TO group-2 ... What it does MOVE copies the value on the left into every field after TO . The field it copies from (the sending field) doesn\u0027t change. Each receiving field gets the value in its own format: COBOL pads, cuts or converts the value so that it fits the receiving field\u0027s PICTURE. Most of what there is to learn about MOVE is how that fitting works. Moves into alphanumeric fields When the receiving field is alphanumeric ( PIC X ) the characters are copied from the left. If the value is shorter than the field, the rest is filled with spaces. If it is longer, the characters that don\u0027t fit on the right are lost. A field described with JUSTIFIED RIGHT works the other way round: the value is placed at the right end, spaces fill the left, and extra characters are cut from the left. Moves into numeric fields When the receiving field is numeric ( PIC 9 ) COBOL moves the value , not the characters. The decimal points are lined up first. Then: missing digits are filled with zeros, on both sides of the decimal point; digits that don\u0027t fit on the left (the high-order digits) are lost, with no warning: moving 1234 to PIC 9(3) leaves 234; extra decimal places are cut off, not rounded (use COMPUTE ... ROUNDED if you want rounding); if the receiving field has no S in its picture, the sign is dropped and the value is stored as positive. Group moves If either field is a group item, the move is a plain copy of bytes, treated like an alphanumeric move: no numbers are converted, no decimal points lined up. The bytes are copied from the left, and spaces fill a longer receiving field. This is quick and is often used to copy a whole record, but it puts the sending field\u0027s bytes into each item of the receiving group exactly as they are. Moves into edited fields A numeric-edited field (one whose PICTURE has editing symbols such as Z , , , . , $ , - or / ) is for showing a number to a person. Moving a number into it lines up the decimal point and then formats the digits: leading zeros become spaces or a floating $ , commas and the sign are inserted. See PICTURE for the symbols. The reverse move also works: moving a numeric-edited field to a numeric field reads the number back out of the formatted text (this is called de-editing ). The commas, the currency sign and the spaces are ignored, and the sign is kept. Figurative constants MOVE SPACES , MOVE ZEROS , MOVE HIGH-VALUES and the others fill the whole receiving field. MOVE ALL \"*-\" TO X repeats the text until the field is full. See figurative constants . MOVE CORRESPONDING MOVE CORRESPONDING A TO B looks for items in group A and group B that have the same name, and moves each one to its partner as a normal elementary move, with the usual conversion. The items don\u0027t need to be in the same order or the same format, which makes it a quick way to copy an input record into a print line. FILLER items and items whose names have no partner are left alone. What can move to what Sending field Receiving field What happens alphanumeric alphanumeric Characters copied from the left; padded with spaces or cut on the right. numeric numeric Value moved, decimal points lined up; high digits and extra decimals lost. numeric numeric-edited Value lined up and formatted for display. numeric-edited numeric De-editing: the number is read back out of the formatted text. numeric (integer) alphanumeric All the digits, leading zeros included, are copied as characters; the sign is dropped. alphanumeric numeric Allowed: the characters are taken as digits of a whole number. Only do this when the field holds digits. group anything Byte copy, no conversion. anything group Byte copy, no conversion. figurative constant anything The whole field is filled (ZERO into a numeric field gives the value 0). Moving a number with decimal places into an alphanumeric field is not allowed: the compiler stops with \"invalid MOVE statement\". Move it to a numeric-edited field first. Examples \"COBOL\" is padded with five spaces in a 10-character field and cut to \"PROG\" in a 4-character one; the JUSTIFIED RIGHT field puts the spaces in front. The numeric moves show zero filling (042), the lost high digit (1234 becomes 234), decimal alignment (7.5 becomes 007.50) and lost low digits (12.3456 becomes 012.34, cut, not rounded). Moving -8.25 to an unsigned whole-number field drops both the sign and the decimals. The last line shows figurative constants, including ALL \"*-\" repeated to fill six characters. -1234.5 is formatted with a floating dollar sign, a comma and a trailing minus, and the de-editing move reads the same value back. Moving a plain number into PIC 99/99/9999 inserts the slashes. MOVE CORRESPONDING moves ITEM, QTY and CUST-NO by name, each converted to its new picture, while the FILLER slashes stay. The two group moves copy bytes only: QTY\u0027s digits appear as they are stored, and moving X\u0027s into ORDER-IN even fills its numeric items with X\u0027s, which is why group moves need care. The signed number -42 moved into a text field arrives as the characters 0042 : all four digits, no sign. Going the other way, the text \"123\" becomes the number 123, and you can then do arithmetic with it. See also PICTURE , JUSTIFIED , INITIALIZE to clear a whole group by type, COMPUTE for rounding, SET for indexes and condition names, referring to data for qualified names ( ITEM OF ORDER-OUT )."},{"t":"MULTIPLY","u":"multiply.html","s":"Multiply two numbers and store the product.","k":"BY GIVING ROUNDED SIZE ERROR END-MULTIPLY times product multiplication","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax Format 1: multiply each receiving field in place MULTIPLY { identifier-1 | literal-1 } BY { identifier-2 [ ROUNDED ] } ... [ ON SIZE ERROR imperative-statement-1 ] [ NOT ON SIZE ERROR imperative-statement-2 ] [ END-MULTIPLY ] Format 2: store the product somewhere else MULTIPLY { identifier-1 | literal-1 } BY { identifier-2 | literal-2 } GIVING { identifier-3 [ ROUNDED ] } ... [ ON SIZE ERROR imperative-statement-1 ] [ NOT ON SIZE ERROR imperative-statement-2 ] [ END-MULTIPLY ] What it does Format 1 multiplies each field after BY by the first value and stores the answer back in that field. MULTIPLY 2 BY N means \"N = 2 × N\". Format 2 multiplies the two values and puts the product in every field after GIVING . The two values don\u0027t change. MULTIPLY PRICE BY QTY GIVING TOTAL means \"TOTAL = PRICE × QTY\". A GIVING field only receives the answer, so it may be a numeric-edited field such as PIC $$,$$9.99 . Decimals and rounding The product can have more decimal places than either value (0.25 × 0.5 = 0.125). If the receiving field has fewer decimal places, the extra digits are dropped, unless you write ROUNDED after that field. Each field gets its own choice: one may be rounded and the next truncated. SIZE ERROR If the product has more digits in front of the decimal point than the receiving field can hold, that is a size error. Without ON SIZE ERROR , the high-order digits are lost silently. With it, the receiving field keeps its old value and your statements run instead. NOT ON SIZE ERROR runs when the answer fit. Use END-MULTIPLY when the statement sits inside an IF or another statement. Example 12.99 × 3 is stored twice, once in a plain numeric field and once in an edited one. 38.97 × 0.0725 is 2.825325: TAX is rounded to 2.83 while TAX-CUT is cut to 2.82. 25 × 10 = 250 doesn\u0027t fit in PIC 99 , so TINY keeps 25 and the SIZE ERROR message shows. 25 × 7 = 175 without a SIZE ERROR phrase keeps only the last two digits, 75. The last two statements use Format 1 ( MULTIPLY 10 BY TINY ), so the answer goes back into TINY itself. See also COMPUTE for whole formulas, ADD , SUBTRACT , DIVIDE , PICTURE ."},{"t":"OPEN","u":"open.html","s":"Open one or more files for reading, writing, updating or adding to the end.","k":"INPUT OUTPUT I-O EXTEND OPTIONAL open file create file replace append 35 41 05","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax OPEN { { INPUT | OUTPUT | I-O | EXTEND } file-name-1 ... } ... What it does A file must be opened before any READ, WRITE, REWRITE, DELETE or START, and the open mode decides which of those you may use: Mode What it\u0027s for If the file doesn\u0027t exist If it exists INPUT Reading only. Reading starts at the first record. Status 35 (or 05 for an OPTIONAL file) Left as it is OUTPUT Writing a new file. Created Emptied : the old records are gone I-O Reading and changing records (REWRITE, DELETE, and WRITE for RELATIVE and INDEXED files). Status 35 Left as it is EXTEND Adding records after the last one. Status 35 New records go after the old ones One OPEN can open several files, and can mix modes: OPEN INPUT OLD-MASTER TRANSACTIONS OUTPUT NEW-MASTER . It does the same as separate OPENs, one after another. The FILE STATUS after OPEN is 00 when it worked. Opening a file that is already open gives 41, and leaves it open as it was. Opening a file that was closed WITH LOCK gives 38. OPTIONAL files A file declared SELECT OPTIONAL is allowed to be missing. OPEN INPUT of a missing optional file succeeds with status 05, and the first READ is at end (status 10). This suits files such as \"yesterday\u0027s errors\" that may or may not be there. Example The first OPEN opens two files for output at once. OPEN EXTEND then adds a second line to open-a.txt , while OPEN OUTPUT on open-b.txt throws its old line away, so only \"B NEW\" is left. The optional file open-c.txt was never created: OPEN gives 05 and the READ gives 10. OPEN OUTPUT on an existing file empties it without asking. When a program should add to a file, use EXTEND (or I-O); when it should create a file only if there is none, try OPEN INPUT first and look for status 35. See also CLOSE , Files for SELECT and ASSIGN, FILE STATUS codes , READ , WRITE ."},{"t":"PERFORM","u":"perform.html","s":"Run a paragraph, a section or a block of statements, once or in a loop.","k":"THRU THROUGH TIMES UNTIL VARYING FROM BY AFTER WITH TEST BEFORE END-PERFORM loop repeat subroutine call paragraph section inline nested loops","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax Format 1: run a paragraph or section once PERFORM procedure-1 [ { THRU | THROUGH } procedure-2 ] Format 2: a fixed number of times PERFORM procedure-1 [ THRU procedure-2 ] { identifier-1 | integer-1 } TIMES Format 3: until a condition is true PERFORM procedure-1 [ THRU procedure-2 ] [ WITH TEST { BEFORE | AFTER } ] UNTIL condition-1 Format 4: counting loops PERFORM procedure-1 [ THRU procedure-2 ] [ WITH TEST { BEFORE | AFTER } ] VARYING identifier-2 FROM { identifier-3 | literal-1 } BY { identifier-4 | literal-2 } UNTIL condition-1 [ AFTER identifier-5 FROM { identifier-6 | literal-3 } BY { identifier-7 | literal-4 } UNTIL condition-2 ] ... Inline: the same, with statements instead of a procedure name PERFORM [ times-or-until-or-varying-phrase ] statement ... END-PERFORM What it does PERFORM is how COBOL runs a piece of code from somewhere else and comes back, and it is also COBOL\u0027s loop statement. Out-of-line PERFORM PERFORM SAY-HELLO jumps to the paragraph named SAY-HELLO, runs it to its end (the next paragraph name), and then comes back to the statement after the PERFORM. A paragraph used this way works like a subroutine in other languages, but it has no parameters: it works on the program\u0027s data directly. PERFORM STEP-1 THRU STEP-3 runs every paragraph from STEP-1 up to and including STEP-3, in the order they are written in the program, then comes back. A section name can be performed too. PERFORM HELPERS , where HELPERS is a section, runs all the paragraphs of that section, from the section header to the next section header. TIMES PERFORM ADD-ONE 3 TIMES runs the paragraph three times. The count can be a numeric field; it is read once, before the first run. UNTIL, and WITH TEST PERFORM ADD-ONE UNTIL N \u003e= 10 runs the paragraph again and again until the condition is true. By default the condition is checked before each run ( WITH TEST BEFORE ), so if it is already true, the paragraph doesn\u0027t run at all. WITH TEST AFTER checks it after each run instead, so the paragraph always runs at least once. Make sure something in the loop changes the condition, or the loop never ends. VARYING VARYING I FROM 1 BY 1 UNTIL I \u003e 10 sets I to 1, then repeats: check the condition, run the code, add 1 to I. BY can be negative to count down. When the loop ends, the counter holds the value that made the condition true (11 here), not the last value used. Each AFTER phrase adds an inner loop. For every value of the first counter, the second counter runs through all its values; this is how you walk through the rows and columns of a two-dimensional table. The counter can be a numeric item or an index . If you count down to zero, give the counter an S in its picture or stop at zero, because an unsigned field can\u0027t go below zero and the loop would never end. Inline PERFORM Leave out the procedure name and put the statements to run between PERFORM and END-PERFORM . All the loop phrases ( TIMES , UNTIL , VARYING , WITH TEST ) work the same way. An inline PERFORM with no loop phrase just runs the statements once. Inline loops keep short loop bodies next to the code that uses them; out-of-line paragraphs suit longer or reused code. Where control goes After an out-of-line PERFORM finishes, control returns to the statement after it, even if the performed paragraph itself performed others. If the main code simply runs on into the paragraphs below it (you forgot a STOP RUN ), they run again as ordinary code, so end the main paragraph with STOP RUN or GOBACK . To leave a paragraph or loop early, see EXIT PARAGRAPH and EXIT PERFORM . Examples The first PERFORM runs one paragraph; THRU runs STEP-1, STEP-2 and STEP-3 in order. After three runs of ADD-ONE, N is 3, and UNTIL takes it to 10. The next PERFORM tests before running: N is already 10, which is \u003e= 5, so ADD-ONE doesn\u0027t run. With TEST AFTER the same condition runs ADD-ONE once before checking, so N becomes 11. VARYING with AFTER makes two nested loops: COL-NO goes from 1 to 3 for each value of ROW-NO, giving a small multiplication table. The inline loop counts down by 3 and stops when K reaches 0, which is the value left in K afterwards. PERFORM 2 TIMES ... END-PERFORM needs no paragraph at all. Performing the section HELPERS runs both of its paragraphs. See also EXIT (EXIT PARAGRAPH, EXIT PERFORM), GO TO , PROCEDURE DIVISION for paragraphs and sections, conditions , OCCURS and SEARCH for tables, CALL to run another program."},{"t":"READ","u":"read.html","s":"Get the next record of a file, or the record with a given key.","k":"READ NEXT PREVIOUS INTO AT END NOT AT END INVALID KEY NOT INVALID KEY KEY IS END-READ end of file 10 23 46 random read","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax Format 1: the next (or previous) record READ file-name [ NEXT | PREVIOUS ] [ RECORD ] [ INTO identifier-1 ] [ AT END imperative-statement-1 ] [ NOT AT END imperative-statement-2 ] [ END-READ ] Format 2: the record with a given key (RELATIVE and INDEXED, random or dynamic access) READ file-name [ RECORD ] [ INTO identifier-1 ] [ KEY IS data-name-1 ] [ INVALID KEY imperative-statement-3 ] [ NOT INVALID KEY imperative-statement-4 ] [ END-READ ] What it does READ copies a record from the file into the file\u0027s record area (the 01 level under its FD). The file must be open INPUT or I-O. Reading in order For SEQUENTIAL and LINE SEQUENTIAL files, and for RELATIVE and INDEXED files with ACCESS SEQUENTIAL , a plain READ file gets the next record. With ACCESS DYNAMIC you must write READ file NEXT to read in order; plain READ there means a read by key. When there are no more records, the READ is \"at end\": the status is 10 and the statements after AT END run. Otherwise the NOT AT END statements run. The usual loop reads until a flag set by AT END says to stop. A READ after the end gives status 46, an error, so don\u0027t read again once you\u0027ve hit the end. A RELATIVE file read in order skips empty slots, and puts the slot number it read in the RELATIVE KEY item. An INDEXED file is read in order of its key (the RECORD KEY, or the key the last START or keyed READ used). INTO READ file INTO WS-ITEM reads the record and then moves it to WS-ITEM , as a MOVE would. It\u0027s handy for keeping the record in WORKING-STORAGE, where it stays after the next READ or after CLOSE. Reading by key With random or dynamic access, put the key first and then READ: RELATIVE: move the slot number to the RELATIVE KEY item. INDEXED: move the value to the RECORD KEY field in the record. To read by an alternate key instead, move the value to that field and write KEY IS that field. If there is no such record, the status is 23 and the INVALID KEY statements run; otherwise NOT INVALID KEY runs. A keyed READ also sets the position, so a READ NEXT afterwards carries on from that record in key order. READ PREVIOUS READ file PREVIOUS (RELATIVE or INDEXED, dynamic access) goes one record backwards: to the record before the last one read, in key or slot order. A WRITE doesn\u0027t move the reading position. After OPEN, then a WRITE by key, a READ NEXT still starts at the first record of the file. Examples A read loop Each READ puts a line in the record area and copies it INTO SCORE-WS, where it is split into a name and points. After three records the fourth READ is at end, which sets NO-MORE and stops the loop. Reading a relative file by slot Slot 3 is empty, so the first READ takes the INVALID KEY branch with status 23. The read of slot 4 works. READ NEXT then carries on from slot 4 to slot 5 and sets SEAT-NO to 05. The last READ NEXT finds nothing after slot 5: AT END, status 10. Reading an indexed file by an alternate key The READ with KEY IS AUTHOR finds the first Austen book. Its status is 02: success, and the next record in AUTHOR order has the same author. READ NEXT then follows the AUTHOR key (PERSUASION, then HARD TIMES by Dickens), and READ PREVIOUS steps back to PERSUASION. The book 0009 doesn\u0027t exist: status 23. This example isn\u0027t compared with stock GnuCOBOL, whose reference build has no indexed file support. READ PREVIOUS on a relative file START and READ NEXT give seat 7. Each READ PREVIOUS then steps back one record: seat 5, then seat 2. After the file is opened again, the WRITE of seat 9 leaves the position at the start of the file, so READ NEXT gives seat 2 and then seat 5. GnuCOBOL 3.2 prints something else here (see the notes below). SP COBOL notes A READ after the end of the file is status 46 and counts as an error: if the file has a USE procedure, it runs even when the READ has an AT END phrase, as in GnuCOBOL. Differs from GnuCOBOL 3.2, on purpose. On a RELATIVE file SP COBOL follows the COBOL standard: READ PREVIOUS gives the record just before the last one read, and a WRITE doesn\u0027t move the reading position. GnuCOBOL 3.2 skips a record on READ PREVIOUS (seat 2 instead of seat 5 in the last example), and after a WRITE by key its READ NEXT is at end of file. See Differences from GnuCOBOL . A RELATIVE file read in order whose next slot number has more digits than the RELATIVE KEY item can hold gives status 14. The record is still read, and the key is set to 0, as in GnuCOBOL. See FILE STATUS codes for an example. See also START to choose where reading begins, WRITE , OPEN , Files , FILE STATUS codes , RETURN (the READ of a sort)."},{"t":"RELEASE","u":"release.html","s":"Hand a record to a SORT from inside its INPUT PROCEDURE.","k":"RELEASE FROM INPUT PROCEDURE sort record SD","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax RELEASE record-name [ FROM { identifier-1 | literal-1 } ] What it does RELEASE is the WRITE of a sort. It is used only in the INPUT PROCEDURE of a SORT , and gives the sort one record: the record of the sort file (the 01 under the SD). The sort keeps every record it is given, and sorts them all when the input procedure ends. RELEASE record FROM item moves item to the record first, the same as a MOVE followed by RELEASE. The input procedure decides what to release: it can read a file and skip records it doesn\u0027t want, change fields, or make records out of data that never was in a file. Example The input procedure splits a sentence into words with UNSTRING and releases each word as a record. The sort then hands them to the output procedure in alphabetical order. \"THE\" appears twice, so it comes out twice. See also SORT , RETURN , WRITE ."},{"t":"RETURN","u":"return.html","s":"Take the next sorted or merged record inside an OUTPUT PROCEDURE.","k":"RETURN INTO AT END NOT AT END END-RETURN OUTPUT PROCEDURE sort merge record SD","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax RETURN sort-file-name [ RECORD ] [ INTO identifier-1 ] AT END imperative-statement-1 [ NOT AT END imperative-statement-2 ] [ END-RETURN ] What it does RETURN is the READ of a sort. It is used only in the OUTPUT PROCEDURE of a SORT or MERGE . Each RETURN puts the next record, in sorted order, in the sort file\u0027s record (the 01 under the SD). Like READ, it takes the sort file name. When every record has been returned, the AT END statements run. Otherwise NOT AT END runs. A typical output procedure is a loop that RETURNs until AT END sets a flag. RETURN file INTO item also moves the record to item , like READ INTO. Don\u0027t confuse it with RETURN-CODE or GOBACK: RETURN has nothing to do with leaving a program. Example Four people are released in any order and sorted by age. The output procedure RETURNs them INTO a WORKING-STORAGE group and numbers them. After the fourth, RETURN reaches the end and the AT END statements stop the loop. See also SORT , MERGE , RELEASE , READ ."},{"t":"REWRITE","u":"rewrite.html","s":"Replace a record that is already in a file with a changed version.","k":"REWRITE FROM INVALID KEY NOT INVALID KEY END-REWRITE update record in place I-O 43 49 23","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax REWRITE record-name [ FROM { identifier-1 | literal-1 } ] [ INVALID KEY imperative-statement-1 ] [ NOT INVALID KEY imperative-statement-2 ] [ END-REWRITE ] What it does REWRITE puts a changed record back in the place of an existing one. The file must be open I-O (status 49 otherwise). FROM item moves item to the record first, as with WRITE . Which record is replaced depends on the access mode: Sequential access (all SEQUENTIAL and LINE SEQUENTIAL files): the record the last READ returned. The pattern is READ, change fields, REWRITE. Without a successful READ just before, the status is 43. In a SEQUENTIAL file the record must keep its length. Random or dynamic access : the record with the key you set, the slot number in the RELATIVE KEY or the value in the RECORD KEY. No READ is needed first. If there is no such record, the status is 23 and INVALID KEY runs. For an INDEXED file the RECORD KEY in the record says which record is replaced, so to change a record\u0027s key, DELETE it and WRITE a new one. Alternate key fields may be changed by REWRITE. Examples The file is opened I-O. Each READ is followed by a COMPUTE and a REWRITE, which puts the higher price back where the record was. Reading the file again shows the new prices. Member 1 exists and is replaced. Slot 2 lies between two used slots but is empty, so the second REWRITE finds no record: status 23 and the INVALID KEY branch. SP COBOL notes REWRITE of an empty RELATIVE slot gives 23 (record not found) in SP COBOL. GnuCOBOL 3.2 reports 00 and even writes the record into the empty slot, which is a bug in its file handler. That\u0027s why the second example\u0027s output differs from GnuCOBOL\u0027s. When a LINE SEQUENTIAL record is rewritten with a shorter one, SP COBOL keeps the line end intact; GnuCOBOL 3.2 overwrites the CR in that case. See also READ , WRITE , DELETE , OPEN (I-O), Files , FILE STATUS codes ."},{"t":"SEARCH","u":"search.html","s":"Look through a table for an entry that meets a condition.","k":"SEARCH ALL AT END WHEN VARYING END-SEARCH INDEXED BY ASCENDING KEY DESCENDING binary search lookup table find","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax Format 1: serial search, entry by entry SEARCH identifier-1 [ VARYING { identifier-2 | index-name-1 } ] [ AT END imperative-statement-1 ] { WHEN condition-1 { imperative-statement-2 | NEXT SENTENCE } } ... [ END-SEARCH ] Format 2: binary search of a sorted table SEARCH ALL identifier-1 [ AT END imperative-statement-1 ] WHEN key-condition [ AND key-condition ] ... { imperative-statement-2 | NEXT SENTENCE } [ END-SEARCH ] where key-condition is: data-name-1 [ ( index-name ) ] { IS EQUAL TO | IS = } { identifier-3 | literal-1 } | condition-name-1 What it does SEARCH looks through a table, an item defined with OCCURS , for an entry that matches a condition. identifier-1 is the item that has the OCCURS clause, and it must also have an INDEXED BY clause: SEARCH moves that index from entry to entry. An index is a special counter for a table; you set it and change it with SET , and use it as a subscript, as in COLOR-NAME (CX) . Serial SEARCH The plain SEARCH starts at the entry the index points to now , so you normally write SET CX TO 1 just before it. For each entry it checks the WHEN conditions in order. When one is true, its statements run and the SEARCH ends, with the index left pointing at the entry that matched. If the index runs past the last entry without a match, the AT END statements run instead (or, without AT END, the program just carries on after the SEARCH). You may write several WHEN phrases, and any kind of condition; the first one that is true wins. VARYING names a second item that SEARCH moves along with the index. If it is a numeric item, it ends up holding the same entry number as the index. SEARCH ALL SEARCH ALL finds an entry much faster in a large table by doing a binary search : it looks at the middle entry, decides which half the wanted entry must be in, and repeats. For that to work: the table must have an ASCENDING KEY (or DESCENDING KEY ) clause naming the field it is sorted on, and the entries must really be in that order; the WHEN condition may only test key fields for equality ( = ), joined with AND , and the key must be subscripted with the table\u0027s first index; there is only one WHEN. SEARCH ALL sets the index itself, so you don\u0027t SET it first. If the table is not in key order, SEARCH ALL can miss entries that are there; it doesn\u0027t check the order for you. Examples LOOK-UP sets the index to 1 and searches. BLUE is the third entry, so the WHEN runs and SET POSITION-NO TO CX copies the entry number out of the index. PINK isn\u0027t in the table, so AT END runs. The last SEARCH starts at entry 2 instead of 1 and finds YELLOW at entry 4; STEP-COUNT, the VARYING item, holds that entry number too. The table of states is in order of the two-letter code, which the ASCENDING KEY clause declares. SEARCH ALL finds NV and shows its name. TX isn\u0027t there, so AT END runs. See also OCCURS (INDEXED BY, ASCENDING KEY), SET , PERFORM VARYING for your own table loops, SORT (it can sort a table into key order), conditions ."},{"t":"SET","u":"set.html","s":"Set or move a table index, turn on a condition name, set a pointer or switch, or point a LINKAGE item at data.","k":"TO UP BY DOWN BY TRUE FALSE index condition-name 88 ADDRESS OF pointer switch ON OFF ENTRY NULL USAGE POINTER COB_SWITCH SWITCH-1 STATUS","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax Format 1: give an index (or a number) a value SET { index-name-1 | identifier-1 } ... TO { index-name-2 | identifier-2 | integer-1 } Format 2: move an index forward or back SET index-name-1 ... { UP | DOWN } BY { identifier-3 | integer-2 } Format 3: make a condition name true or false SET condition-name-1 ... TO { TRUE | FALSE } Format 4: point a LINKAGE SECTION item at some data SET ADDRESS OF identifier-4 TO { ADDRESS OF identifier-5 | pointer-2 } Format 5: give a pointer a value SET pointer-1 TO { ADDRESS OF identifier-6 | pointer-2 | NULL } Format 6: turn a switch on or off SET switch-name-1 ... TO { ON | OFF } What it does Indexes An index is the counter named in a table\u0027s INDEXED BY clause (see OCCURS ). It holds an entry number, but internally it is stored in a form the computer can use quickly, so you can\u0027t MOVE to it or ADD to it like an ordinary field. SET is how you change it: SET DX TO 1 points the index at entry 1. The value can be a number, a numeric field, or another index. SET DX UP BY 4 moves it four entries forward; DOWN BY moves it back. SET SLOT TO DX goes the other way: it copies the entry number out of the index into an ordinary numeric field, where you can display it or do arithmetic. Condition names A condition name (a level-88 item) stands for one or more values of its parent field. SET ORDER-SHIPPED TO TRUE moves the condition\u0027s value (its first value, if it has several) into the parent field, so that the condition becomes true. This reads better than MOVE \"S\" TO ORDER-STATUS and keeps the actual code letter in one place. SET ... TO FALSE works only when the 88 item has a FALSE phrase, as in 88 ALL-DONE VALUE \"Y\" FALSE \"N\" ; it moves the FALSE value into the field. ADDRESS OF An item in the LINKAGE SECTION has no storage of its own; normally it describes data passed in by a CALL . SET ADDRESS OF VIEW TO ADDRESS OF GREETING makes VIEW describe the bytes of GREETING, so reading or changing VIEW reads or changes GREETING. This is an advanced feature; most programs never need it. Pointers A field with USAGE POINTER holds an address. SET PTR TO ADDRESS OF GREETING stores GREETING\u0027s address in it, and SET ADDRESS OF VIEW TO PTR then points a LINKAGE item at that address. SET PTR TO NULL (or VALUE NULL in the data description) means \"points at nothing\"; test for it with IF PTR = NULL . A pointer lets you keep an address in a field, pass it around and use it later. Switches A switch is declared in the SPECIAL-NAMES paragraph (see ENVIRONMENT DIVISION ), for example SWITCH-1 IS TRACE-SWITCH ON STATUS IS TRACE-ON OFF STATUS IS TRACE-OFF . TRACE-ON and TRACE-OFF are conditions you can test with IF. A switch is normally set from outside the program, before it runs: setting the environment variable COB_SWITCH_1=ON turns switch 1 on. Inside the program, SET TRACE-SWITCH TO ON or TO OFF changes it. Examples The index starts at entry 1 (MON), moves up four to FRI and down two to WED. Setting it from SLOT, which holds 2, points it at TUE, and SET SLOT TO DX copies the 2 back out. SET ORDER-SHIPPED TO TRUE puts \"S\" in ORDER-STATUS, so the IF finds the condition true. ALL-DONE has a FALSE value, so it can be set either way. VIEW is a LINKAGE SECTION item. After the SET it describes GREETING\u0027s five bytes, so displaying VIEW shows HELLO, and moving WORLD into VIEW changes GREETING. A pointer field PTR starts out NULL. It is then set to GREETING\u0027s address, and VIEW is pointed at whatever PTR holds, so VIEW shows HELLO. Pointing PTR at FAREWELL and repeating the SET moves VIEW to the other field. Finally PTR is set back to NULL. A switch Nothing set COB_SWITCH_1 before this run, so the switch starts off. The two SETs turn it on and off again, and the ON STATUS and OFF STATUS conditions follow it. Run with COB_SWITCH_1=ON in the environment, the first line would say the switch starts on. SP COBOL notes SET program-pointer TO ENTRY \"name\" is not supported (see CALL ). See also OCCURS , SEARCH , condition names , PERFORM VARYING (which can vary an index), CALL , USAGE ."},{"t":"SORT","u":"sort.html","s":"Put the records of a file, or the entries of a table, in order by one or more keys.","k":"SORT SD ASCENDING DESCENDING KEY USING GIVING INPUT PROCEDURE OUTPUT PROCEDURE WITH DUPLICATES IN ORDER COLLATING SEQUENCE SORT-RETURN table sort stable order sort file","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax Format 1: sort records SORT sort-file-name { ON { ASCENDING | DESCENDING } KEY data-name-1 ... } ... [ WITH DUPLICATES IN ORDER ] [ COLLATING SEQUENCE IS alphabet-name ] { USING file-name-1 ... | INPUT PROCEDURE IS procedure-1 [ { THRU | THROUGH } procedure-2 ] } { GIVING file-name-2 ... | OUTPUT PROCEDURE IS procedure-3 [ { THRU | THROUGH } procedure-4 ] } Format 2: sort a table in memory SORT table-name { ON { ASCENDING | DESCENDING } KEY data-name-2 ... } ... What it does Sorting records Format 1 sorts records through a sort file . You declare it like an ordinary file, with a SELECT, but describe it with SD (sort description) instead of FD. Its record lists the fields the records are sorted by. You never OPEN, READ or WRITE the sort file yourself; SORT does that. The KEY phrases give the sort keys, most important first. ON ASCENDING KEY W-TEAM ON DESCENDING KEY W-GOALS means \"by team A to Z, and within a team by goals, highest first\". Numeric keys are compared by value (so -4 comes before 19); other keys character by character. The records come in one of two ways: USING file : SORT opens the file, reads every record and closes it. The file must not be open. INPUT PROCEDURE IS para : SORT performs your paragraph (or section), which hands each record over with RELEASE . Use it to pick or change records on the way in, or to sort data that isn\u0027t in a file. And go out in one of two ways: GIVING file : SORT writes the sorted records to a new file (it is opened OUTPUT, so an existing one is replaced). OUTPUT PROCEDURE IS para : SORT performs your paragraph, which takes the records one at a time in order with RETURN , for counting, totals, or a report. Records with equal keys come out in the order they went in. WITH DUPLICATES IN ORDER asks for exactly that. Keys that aren\u0027t numeric are compared in the normal character (ASCII) order. COLLATING SEQUENCE IS alphabet-name sorts them in another order: one you list with an ALPHABET clause in SPECIAL-NAMES (see ENVIRONMENT DIVISION ), or ALPHABET name IS EBCDIC , the character order of IBM mainframes. After the SORT, the special register SORT-RETURN is 0 if it worked and 16 if it failed. Sorting a table Format 2 sorts the entries of a table (an item with OCCURS ) in place. Name the OCCURS item, and give keys that are fields inside each entry. No SD or file is involved. Examples USING and GIVING The players are sorted by team, A to Z, and within a team by goals, highest first. SORT reads raw.txt and writes sorted.txt ; the program only reads the result. INPUT and OUTPUT PROCEDURE The input procedure releases four records taken from a table; the output procedure returns them warmest first. W-TEMP is a signed number, so -04 sorts below +19. Equal keys Three records have the key A1 and two have B2. Within each key they come out in the order they were released (2, 4, 6 and 1, 5). Sorting a table The same table is sorted twice in place, by number of moons and then by name. Your own order: COLLATING SEQUENCE The same five shirts are sorted twice by size. The first SORT uses the normal character order, so L comes before M and S. The second names the alphabet SIZES, defined in SPECIAL-NAMES as S, M, L, X, and the shirts come out smallest first. Mainframe order: EBCDIC In EBCDIC, lower-case letters come before capitals, and letters before digits: the opposite of the ASCII order a plain SORT uses, which would put 300 first and the lower-case codes last. SP COBOL notes SP COBOL sorts in memory, without work files, so the file named in the sort file\u0027s ASSIGN is never created. That suits the record counts of learning and business programs; a file too big for memory can\u0027t be sorted. Table SORT keeps equal entries in order; GnuCOBOL\u0027s may not. GnuCOBOL sorts tables with the C library\u0027s qsort , which may shuffle entries that have equal keys, and the result can vary between systems. SP COBOL\u0027s table sort is stable : entries with equal keys keep their order. The two agree whenever the keys are all different, as in the example above. If the order of equal entries matters to you, add a second key that tells them apart. See also RELEASE , RETURN , MERGE , Files , SEARCH (SEARCH ALL needs a sorted table), special registers ."},{"t":"START","u":"start.html","s":"Position a relative or indexed file so the next READ NEXT begins at a chosen key.","k":"START KEY IS EQUAL GREATER LESS NOT LESS THAN OR EQUAL TO INVALID KEY END-START position browse alternate key 23","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax START file-name [ KEY IS { EQUAL TO | = | GREATER THAN | \u003e | NOT LESS THAN | NOT \u003c | GREATER THAN OR EQUAL TO | \u003e= | LESS THAN | \u003c | NOT GREATER THAN | NOT \u003e | LESS THAN OR EQUAL TO | \u003c= } data-name-1 ] [ INVALID KEY imperative-statement-1 ] [ NOT INVALID KEY imperative-statement-2 ] [ END-START ] What it does START doesn\u0027t read anything. It finds the first record that meets a condition on its key and makes it the current position , so the next READ ... NEXT returns that record and later ones follow in key order. Use it to browse a RELATIVE or INDEXED file from a point in the middle, such as \"all customers from M on\". The file must be open INPUT or I-O, with sequential or dynamic access. Set the key first, then START: RELATIVE: the slot number in the RELATIVE KEY item; KEY IS names that item. INDEXED: the value in the RECORD KEY field, or in an alternate key field. KEY IS names the field, and that key also becomes the order for the READ NEXTs that follow. Without a KEY phrase the condition is \"equal to the RECORD KEY (or RELATIVE KEY)\". \u003e= and NOT \u003c mean the same thing. With \u003c or \u003c= the position is the last record below (or at) the value. If no record meets the condition, the status is 23 and INVALID KEY runs. Examples Relative file There is no day 5, so KEY IS GREATER THAN OR EQUAL TO positions at day 8. KEY \u003e DAY-NO with 8 positions at day 9. Day 3 exists, so the KEY = START succeeds. Nothing comes after day 9: status 23 and INVALID KEY. After a successful START and READ NEXT the RELATIVE KEY holds the slot number actually read. Indexed file, alternate key The START uses the alternate key DEPT, so the READ NEXTs that follow go in department order, from SALES on. The 02 status on Ava\u0027s record means the next record has the same department. This example isn\u0027t compared with stock GnuCOBOL, whose reference build has no indexed file support. See also READ , Files for RELATIVE KEY, RECORD KEY and ALTERNATE RECORD KEY, FILE STATUS codes ."},{"t":"STOP","u":"stop.html","s":"End the program, optionally with an exit code for whatever started it.","k":"STOP RUN RETURNING GIVING WITH ERROR STATUS NORMAL STATUS RETURN-CODE exit code end program terminate","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax STOP RUN [ { RETURNING | GIVING } { identifier-1 | integer-1 } ] STOP RUN WITH { ERROR | NORMAL } STATUS [ { identifier-1 | integer-1 } ] Obsolete: STOP literal-1 What it does STOP RUN ends the whole program at once. Files that are still open are closed, and control goes back to whatever started the program: the command prompt, a batch file, or Visual Studio. Statements after STOP RUN never run. It doesn\u0027t matter whether STOP RUN is in the main program or in a program reached by CALL : the whole run ends. To end only a called program and return to its caller, use GOBACK . The exit code Every program hands a number back to the operating system when it ends, its exit code . Zero means \"all went well\"; other values usually mean some kind of problem. A batch file can test it with IF ERRORLEVEL , and PowerShell shows it in $LASTEXITCODE . In COBOL the exit code comes from the special register RETURN-CODE , which starts at zero. Move a number into it before STOP RUN and that number becomes the exit code. ( RETURN-CODE is also how a called program passes a status back to its caller; see special registers .) STOP RUN RETURNING 4 (or GIVING 4 ) sets the exit code in the same statement. STOP RUN WITH ERROR STATUS 2 does the same thing in the wording of the 2014 standard, and WITH NORMAL STATUS ends with zero. STOP literal Old programs sometimes contain STOP \"MOUNT TAPE 2\" . It shows the message to the operator, waits for Enter, and then carries on with the next statement: the program does not end. It is obsolete; use DISPLAY followed by ACCEPT to pause for the user instead, which also lets you read what was typed. Examples The program finds three errors, puts 8 into RETURN-CODE and stops. Its exit code is 8; run from a command prompt, echo %ERRORLEVEL% afterwards would show 8. The last DISPLAY never runs. RETURNING sets the exit code to 4 without touching RETURN-CODE yourself. STOP literal The message is shown and the program waits. Once Enter is pressed, it goes on to the next DISPLAY; only STOP RUN ends it. See also GOBACK , EXIT PROGRAM , CALL , special registers (RETURN-CODE), PROCEDURE DIVISION ."},{"t":"STRING","u":"string.html","s":"Join pieces of text together into one field.","k":"DELIMITED BY SIZE INTO WITH POINTER ON OVERFLOW NOT ON OVERFLOW END-STRING concatenate join text combine","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax STRING { { identifier-1 | literal-1 } ... DELIMITED BY { identifier-2 | literal-2 | SIZE } } ... INTO identifier-3 [ WITH POINTER identifier-4 ] [ ON OVERFLOW imperative-statement-1 ] [ NOT ON OVERFLOW imperative-statement-2 ] [ END-STRING ] What it does STRING copies several pieces of text, one after another, into the field after INTO . It is how you build a full name from a first and a last name, or a line of output from several fields, without the extra spaces that each fixed-length field carries. DELIMITED BY Each piece (or group of pieces) is followed by DELIMITED BY , which says how much of it to copy: DELIMITED BY SIZE copies the whole piece, trailing spaces included. DELIMITED BY SPACE copies up to, but not including, the first space. This is the usual way to drop the padding at the end of a name, though it also stops at a space in the middle (\"SAN DIEGO\" gives just \"SAN\"). DELIMITED BY \",\" or by a field copies up to the first appearance of that text. If the text isn\u0027t there, the whole piece is copied. One DELIMITED BY phrase applies to all the pieces written in front of it, back to the previous DELIMITED BY. The receiving field STRING only writes the characters it copies. It doesn\u0027t pad the rest of the receiving field with spaces, so whatever was there before stays there. That is why the examples MOVE SPACES to the field first. WITH POINTER The pointer is a numeric field holding the position in the receiving field where the next character will go. Set it before the STRING (to 1 to start at the beginning); STRING starts there and, when it finishes, leaves the pointer just past the last character written. That lets you add to a field in several steps. Without a pointer, STRING starts at position 1. OVERFLOW If the receiving field fills up before all the text is copied, the rest is dropped and the ON OVERFLOW statements run. The characters that did fit stay in the field. Overflow also happens if the pointer starts out less than 1 or past the end of the field; then nothing is copied. NOT ON OVERFLOW runs when everything fit. Example The full name is built from the first name up to its first space, one space, and the last name up to its first space. In the second STRING, the comma delimiter applies to both \"CITY=\" and CITY, so the city is copied only up to its comma. With the pointer set to 4, ABCDEF goes into positions 4 to 9 of a field of periods and the pointer ends at 10. \"ADA\" plus seven spaces plus \"LOVELACE\" is too long for 8 characters, so only the first eight are kept and ON OVERFLOW runs; the next STRING fits. In the last one the pointer starts at 0, outside the field, so ON OVERFLOW runs and nothing is copied: SHORT-OUT still holds ADA. See also UNSTRING to split text apart, INSPECT , intrinsic functions such as CONCATENATE and TRIM, reference modification , MOVE ."},{"t":"SUBTRACT","u":"subtract.html","s":"Subtract one or more numbers from a field and store the difference.","k":"FROM GIVING ROUNDED SIZE ERROR END-SUBTRACT CORRESPONDING CORR minus subtraction difference","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax Format 1: subtract from each receiving field SUBTRACT { identifier-1 | literal-1 } ... FROM { identifier-2 [ ROUNDED ] } ... [ ON SIZE ERROR imperative-statement-1 ] [ NOT ON SIZE ERROR imperative-statement-2 ] [ END-SUBTRACT ] Format 2: store the difference somewhere else SUBTRACT { identifier-1 | literal-1 } ... FROM { identifier-2 | literal-2 } GIVING { identifier-3 [ ROUNDED ] } ... [ ON SIZE ERROR imperative-statement-1 ] [ NOT ON SIZE ERROR imperative-statement-2 ] [ END-SUBTRACT ] Format 3: subtract matching fields of two groups SUBTRACT { CORRESPONDING | CORR } group-1 FROM group-2 [ ROUNDED ] [ ON SIZE ERROR imperative-statement-1 ] [ NOT ON SIZE ERROR imperative-statement-2 ] [ END-SUBTRACT ] What it does Format 1 adds up every value before FROM and subtracts that total from each field after FROM . SUBTRACT A B FROM C means \"C = C - (A + B)\". Format 2 does the same subtraction but puts the answer in the GIVING field, leaving the field after FROM unchanged. SUBTRACT A FROM B GIVING C means \"C = B - A\". A GIVING field may be numeric-edited, such as PIC -(4)9.99 . Format 3 subtracts each numeric item of the first group from the item with the same name in the second group. Items with no partner are skipped. Negative answers Subtraction often gives a negative answer, so the receiving field usually needs an S in its picture (or a - if it is edited). If the field is unsigned, the sign is simply dropped: 5 - 8 stored in PIC 9(3) becomes 3, not -3, and this is not a size error. Rounding and SIZE ERROR Extra decimal places are cut off unless you write ROUNDED after the receiving field. If the answer has too many digits in front of the decimal point to fit, that is a size error. With ON SIZE ERROR the field keeps its old value and your statements run; without it, the high-order digits are lost. NOT ON SIZE ERROR runs when the answer fit. These work exactly as in ADD . Example The fee comes off BALANCE, leaving 97.50. The GIVING form subtracts the fee and the refund (12.50 together) from BALANCE without changing it, so LEFT-OVER is 85.00. 85.00 - 0.50 is 84.50, which ROUNDED makes 85 in a whole-number field. 97.50 - 200 is shown with a minus sign in an edited field. STOCK has no sign, so 5 - 8 leaves 3. SMALL is signed but only two digits long: -95 - 10 = -105 doesn\u0027t fit, so SMALL keeps -95. SUBTRACT CORRESPONDING takes SALES from SALES and RETURNS from RETURNS: 900 - 150 and 40 - 15. See also ADD , MULTIPLY , DIVIDE , COMPUTE , SIGN and PICTURE for signed fields."},{"t":"SUPPRESS","u":"suppress.html","s":"Stop one Report Writer group from printing, from inside a USE BEFORE REPORTING declarative.","k":"SUPPRESS PRINTING USE BEFORE REPORTING declaratives report writer skip footing","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax SUPPRESS [ PRINTING ] What it does SUPPRESS PRINTING tells the Report Writer not to print the report group that is about to print, this one time. It is only allowed in a USE BEFORE REPORTING section of the DECLARATIVES : that section runs just before its group prints, decides, and may suppress it. Suppressing a group does not stop anything else. SUM counters are still reset after a suppressed control footing, and the next GENERATE carries on as usual. Example The BEFORE-TOTAL section runs before every TEAM-TOTAL footing. While a footing prints, the control field still holds the team that is ending, so WS-TEAM is \"SOLO\" when SOLO\u0027s footing is due, and that total is suppressed. The REDS and BLUES totals print normally. SP COBOL notes SUPPRESS and USE BEFORE REPORTING work as in GnuCOBOL 3.2, except for declaratives on DETAIL groups, where SP COBOL fixes a GnuCOBOL bug that doubles the totals (see Report Writer ). See also Report Writer , USE , GENERATE ."},{"t":"TERMINATE","u":"terminate.html","s":"Finish a Report Writer report, printing its last footings and final totals.","k":"report writer end report finish FINAL grand total REPORT FOOTING","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax TERMINATE report-name-1 ... What it does TERMINATE ends a Report Writer report. It prints everything that is still owed: the control footings for the last values of the control fields, the CONTROL FOOTING FINAL group (grand totals), the page footing of the last page, and the report footing. Then LINE-COUNTER goes back to 0. TERMINATE does not close the file. CLOSE it afterwards, as usual. If you forget TERMINATE, the last subtotals and the grand total are never printed. After a TERMINATE you can INITIATE the report again. Example The two GENERATEs print MON and TUE. TERMINATE prints the WEEK total (the FINAL footing) and the report footing, and LINE-COUNTER is 0 afterwards. In SP COBOL, as in GnuCOBOL, the report footing goes on a page of its own: page 1 is padded to its 9 lines first, and LINE PLUS 2 puts \"END OF REPORT\" on line 2 of the new page. See also Report Writer , INITIATE , GENERATE , CLOSE ."},{"t":"UNSTRING","u":"unstring.html","s":"Split text into separate fields at the delimiters you name.","k":"DELIMITED BY ALL OR INTO DELIMITER IN COUNT IN WITH POINTER TALLYING IN ON OVERFLOW END-UNSTRING split parse CSV comma separated","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax UNSTRING identifier-1 [ DELIMITED BY [ ALL ] { identifier-2 | literal-1 } [ OR [ ALL ] { identifier-3 | literal-2 } ] ... ] INTO { identifier-4 [ DELIMITER IN identifier-5 ] [ COUNT IN identifier-6 ] } ... [ WITH POINTER identifier-7 ] [ TALLYING IN identifier-8 ] [ ON OVERFLOW imperative-statement-1 ] [ NOT ON OVERFLOW imperative-statement-2 ] [ END-UNSTRING ] What it does UNSTRING is the opposite of STRING . It reads identifier-1 from left to right and cuts it into pieces wherever it finds a delimiter , such as a comma. The first piece goes into the first field after INTO , the second into the second, and so on. Each piece is moved as an alphanumeric MOVE would move it: padded with spaces or cut to fit. The delimiters themselves are not copied. DELIMITED BY You can name several delimiters joined with OR ; whichever comes first in the text ends the current piece. Two delimiters next to each other mean an empty piece, so \"A,,B\" split on commas gives A, an empty field (spaces), and B. Writing ALL in front of a delimiter treats a run of them as one, which is what you want for words separated by several spaces. Without DELIMITED BY, the text is simply cut into lengths: each receiving field takes as many characters as it holds. DELIMITER IN and COUNT IN For each receiving field you may ask for two extra results. DELIMITER IN receives the delimiter that ended this piece, which tells you which of several delimiters was found (it gets spaces if the piece ended at the end of the text). COUNT IN receives the number of characters in the piece, before any padding or cutting, so you can tell how long a name really was. WITH POINTER The pointer is a numeric field holding the position in identifier-1 where UNSTRING starts reading. Set it (to 1, normally) before the statement; afterwards it holds the position just past the last character read. You can then carry on with another UNSTRING from where the first one stopped. Without a pointer, UNSTRING starts at position 1. TALLYING IN TALLYING IN adds the number of receiving fields that were filled to a counter. Like INSPECT\u0027s counters, it is added to, not replaced, so set it to zero first. OVERFLOW ON OVERFLOW runs if all the receiving fields are used up while text is still left, or if the pointer starts outside the text. Otherwise NOT ON OVERFLOW runs. Fields after the last piece found are left unchanged, not cleared. Example The CSV line is split at commas into four fields. The two commas in a row give an empty third field. COUNT IN shows that SMITH was 5 characters long. The last piece runs to the end of the 30-character line, so its count is 18 (SAN DIEGO and its trailing spaces), and it is cut to 8 characters in F4. TALLYING IN counts four fields filled, the empty one included. The second UNSTRING splits words at runs of spaces or at a semicolon. It has only two fields to fill, so after ONE and TWO text is left over: ON OVERFLOW runs, and the pointer shows that reading stopped at position 11. DELIMITER IN shows that ONE ended at a space. The third UNSTRING starts from that pointer and picks up the rest. SP COBOL notes UNSTRING into a JUSTIFIED RIGHT field fills it from the left, as an ordinary field, while MOVE into the same field does right-justify. This is how GnuCOBOL 3.2 behaves, and SP COBOL keeps it so that results match. If you need the piece right-justified, UNSTRING into an ordinary field and then MOVE it. SP COBOL\u0027s UNSTRING follows GnuCOBOL\u0027s own algorithm, so COUNT IN, DELIMITER IN, POINTER and TALLYING results are the same as in GnuCOBOL 3.2. See also STRING , INSPECT , JUSTIFIED , MOVE , intrinsic functions (TRIM, NUMVAL)."},{"t":"USE","u":"use.html","s":"Name a section in DECLARATIVES that runs automatically when a file operation fails.","k":"USE AFTER STANDARD ERROR EXCEPTION PROCEDURE ON INPUT OUTPUT I-O EXTEND GLOBAL DECLARATIVES END DECLARATIVES error handler file error nested program","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax PROCEDURE DIVISION. DECLARATIVES. section-name SECTION. USE [ GLOBAL ] AFTER STANDARD { EXCEPTION | ERROR } PROCEDURE ON { file-name-1 ... | INPUT | OUTPUT | I-O | EXTEND } . paragraph-name. statements ... [ another section with its own USE ] ... END DECLARATIVES. section-name-2 SECTION. the rest of the program ... What it does A USE statement turns a section into an error procedure for files. You don\u0027t PERFORM it yourself: the runtime runs it right after a file statement fails, and then the program carries on with the statement after the one that failed. Inside the procedure the file\u0027s FILE STATUS item already holds the code, so the procedure can report it, count it or set a flag. All USE sections go at the very start of the PROCEDURE DIVISION, between DECLARATIVES. and END DECLARATIVES. Each one is a section whose first sentence is the USE statement. After END DECLARATIVES the program proper must start with a section header. ERROR and EXCEPTION mean the same; STANDARD is optional. Which files it covers ON file-name : errors on those files. ON INPUT , ON OUTPUT , ON I-O , ON EXTEND : errors on any file open in that mode (that has no procedure of its own). A procedure for a particular file is used for that file in place of an open-mode procedure. When it runs For errors (status 30 and up, and 46 / 47 / 48 / 49 and the like). For end of file (10) and key problems (2x), but only when the statement has no AT END or INVALID KEY phrase. With the phrase, the phrase runs instead. A READ after end of file (46) is an error, so the procedure runs even when that READ has an AT END phrase, and the AT END statements don\u0027t. A file with a USE procedure doesn\u0027t stop the program on errors, even with no FILE STATUS clause. Use that with care: if the procedure only prints a message, the program goes on as if nothing happened. USE GLOBAL In a program that contains nested programs , USE GLOBAL makes the procedure cover errors in the nested programs too: on a GLOBAL file they use, or on their own files by open mode. The procedure runs in the outer program and control then goes back to the nested program. Two other kinds of USE exist: USE BEFORE REPORTING , part of the Report Writer , and USE FOR DEBUGGING , described with debugging . Its procedures run only when the environment variable COB_SET_DEBUG=Y is set, as in GnuCOBOL. Examples OPEN INPUT of the missing config.dat runs CONFIG-PROBLEM, which has the status 35, and the program goes on. CONFIG was open for input too, but only its own procedure runs. The second READ of ORDERS hits the end and has no AT END phrase, so INPUT-PROBLEM runs with status 10. The third READ is past the end: status 46, an error, so INPUT-PROBLEM runs again and the AT END phrase doesn\u0027t. The nested program USE2LOAD opens the GLOBAL file HISTORY, which doesn\u0027t exist. The outer program\u0027s USE GLOBAL procedure handles it, then USE2LOAD continues and sees the 35 itself. See also FILE STATUS codes , Files , PROCEDURE DIVISION , GLOBAL , Report Writer ."},{"t":"WRITE","u":"write.html","s":"Add a record to a file, with line spacing for printed reports.","k":"WRITE FROM ADVANCING BEFORE AFTER LINES LINE PAGE AT END-OF-PAGE EOP INVALID KEY END-WRITE LINAGE LINAGE-COUNTER form feed report print","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax Format 1: sequential and line sequential files WRITE record-name [ FROM { identifier-1 | literal-1 } ] [ { BEFORE | AFTER } ADVANCING { { integer-1 | identifier-2 } [ LINE | LINES ] | PAGE } ] [ AT { END-OF-PAGE | EOP } imperative-statement-1 ] [ NOT AT { END-OF-PAGE | EOP } imperative-statement-2 ] [ END-WRITE ] Format 2: relative and indexed files WRITE record-name [ FROM { identifier-1 | literal-1 } ] [ INVALID KEY imperative-statement-3 ] [ NOT INVALID KEY imperative-statement-4 ] [ END-WRITE ] What it does WRITE adds the record in the record area to the file. Notice that you write a record name (the 01 under the FD), while READ takes a file name. WRITE record FROM item first moves item into the record, then writes it. It saves a separate MOVE , and lets you build lines in WORKING-STORAGE. The file must be open OUTPUT or EXTEND (sequential access), or OUTPUT or I-O (RELATIVE and INDEXED files with random or dynamic access). Otherwise the status is 48. Where the record goes SEQUENTIAL and LINE SEQUENTIAL: after the last record written. RELATIVE: with sequential access, in the next slot (1, 2, 3, ...), and the slot number is put in the RELATIVE KEY item. If the next slot number has more digits than the RELATIVE KEY can hold, the status is 24 and nothing is written. With random or dynamic access, in the slot whose number you put in the RELATIVE KEY first. If that slot is in use, the status is 22 and INVALID KEY runs. INDEXED: by the value of its RECORD KEY. A key that already exists gives 22 and INVALID KEY . With sequential access, keys must come in ascending order (21 otherwise). Line spacing: ADVANCING ADVANCING is for report files (LINE SEQUENTIAL). AFTER ADVANCING 2 LINES moves the paper up two lines and then prints the record, leaving one blank line before it. BEFORE ADVANCING 1 LINE prints first, then moves up. PAGE starts a new page: a form feed character in the file, or blank lines to the end of the page when the file has LINAGE. A plain WRITE (no ADVANCING) prints the record and then ends the line. A WRITE ... AFTER ADVANCING moves first and does not end its own line. So a plain WRITE straight after an AFTER ADVANCING one lands on the same line, right after the previous record. In a report, use AFTER ADVANCING on every WRITE (or BEFORE on every WRITE); don\u0027t mix them with plain WRITEs. Pages: LINAGE and END-OF-PAGE An FD with LINAGE IS 4 LINES WITH FOOTING AT 3 LINES AT TOP 1 LINES AT BOTTOM 1 describes a page with a 1-line top margin, a body of 4 lines, and a 1-line bottom margin. The footing area is lines 3 and 4 of the body. The special register LINAGE-COUNTER holds the line of the body the next WRITE will use. When a WRITE reaches the footing area, or the page is full and the runtime moves to the next page, the AT END-OF-PAGE statements run; that is where a program prints page totals or a new heading. Examples WRITE FROM and ADVANCING Every WRITE uses AFTER ADVANCING, so each record moves down first: the file starts with an empty line, and the 2-line advances leave a blank line before TEA and before END. The program reads the file back to show the lines. LINAGE and AT END-OF-PAGE After line 2, LINAGE-COUNTER is 3, the start of the footing, so AT END-OF-PAGE runs; again after line 3. Line 4 fills the page, the runtime moves to the next page and LINAGE-COUNTER starts again at 1. On the second page line 6 reaches the footing. AFTER ADVANCING PAGE pads the rest of the page with blank lines and prints at the top of a new one. INVALID KEY on a relative file Locker 12 is free the first time, so the NOT INVALID KEY branch runs. The second WRITE to slot 12 finds it in use: status 22, INVALID KEY. A RELATIVE KEY that is too small BIN-NO has one digit, so it can number slots 1 to 9. The first nine WRITEs fill those slots. The tenth would go in slot 10, which BIN-NO can\u0027t hold: status 24, and the record is not written, so the file has nine records. Give the RELATIVE KEY enough digits for every slot the file will have. GnuCOBOL 3.2 prints something else here (see the notes below). SP COBOL notes As in GnuCOBOL 3.2, LINAGE files get blank lines rather than form feeds between pages, and lines end with CR LF. Differs from GnuCOBOL 3.2, on purpose. When a sequential WRITE reaches a slot number that doesn\u0027t fit the RELATIVE KEY, SP COBOL returns 24, the COBOL standard\u0027s code for it, and writes nothing. GnuCOBOL 3.2 returns 00, writes the record in the next slot anyway and sets the key to 0. See Differences from GnuCOBOL . See also READ , REWRITE , OPEN , Files , FILE STATUS codes , Report Writer for reports with headings and totals, RELEASE (the WRITE of a sort)."},{"t":"XML GENERATE","u":"xml-generate.html","s":"Turn a group item and its fields into XML text.","k":"XML GENERATE FROM COUNT IN XML-DECLARATION ATTRIBUTES NAME OF TYPE OF ATTRIBUTE ELEMENT CONTENT SUPPRESS WHEN NAMESPACE NAMESPACE-PREFIX ON EXCEPTION XML-CODE END-XML markup","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax XML GENERATE identifier-1 FROM identifier-2 [ COUNT IN identifier-3 ] [ WITH XML-DECLARATION ] [ WITH ATTRIBUTES ] [ NAMESPACE IS { identifier-4 | literal-1 } [ NAMESPACE-PREFIX IS { identifier-5 | literal-2 } ] ] [ NAME OF { identifier-6 IS literal-3 } ... ] [ TYPE OF { identifier-7 IS { ATTRIBUTE | ELEMENT | CONTENT } } ... ] [ SUPPRESS { identifier-8 ... | WHEN { SPACES | ZEROS | LOW-VALUES | HIGH-VALUES } } ] [ ON EXCEPTION imperative-statement-1 ] [ NOT ON EXCEPTION imperative-statement-2 ] [ END-XML ] What it does XML is a text format that marks data with named tags, like \u003cPRICE\u003e4.50\u003c/PRICE\u003e . XML GENERATE takes the data item after FROM , usually a group, and writes it as XML into the alphanumeric field identifier-1 . How the data becomes XML Each item becomes an element : a start tag with the item\u0027s COBOL name, the value, and an end tag. A group\u0027s element contains the elements of its items. Numeric values are written as numbers: leading zeros are dropped, and the sign and the decimal point are shown ( 0017 becomes 17 ). Text values lose their trailing spaces; a field of all spaces becomes one space. The characters that have a meaning in XML are escaped : \u0026 becomes \u0026amp; , \u003c becomes \u0026lt; , \u003e becomes \u0026gt; and a double quote becomes \u0026quot; . FILLER items are left out. The XML text goes at the start of the output field. COUNT IN gives its length, so XML-OUT (1:LEN) is exactly the XML. The optional phrases WITH XML-DECLARATION starts the text with \u003c?xml version=\"1.0\"?\u003e and a line break. WITH ATTRIBUTES writes elementary items as attributes of their group\u0027s start tag ( \u003cITEM ITEM-NO=\"0017\"\u003e ) instead of as separate elements. Groups stay elements. TYPE OF item IS ATTRIBUTE or IS ELEMENT chooses for one item, overriding WITH ATTRIBUTES. IS CONTENT writes the value as the bare text of the group\u0027s element, without a tag of its own. NAME OF item IS \"name\" uses your own tag name instead of the COBOL name. SUPPRESS item ... leaves the named items out. SUPPRESS WHEN SPACES (or ZEROS, LOW-VALUES, HIGH-VALUES) leaves out every element whose value is all spaces (or zero, and so on). NAMESPACE IS adds an XML namespace (a URI that identifies the vocabulary) to the outer element. With NAMESPACE-PREFIX IS \"m\" every tag gets the prefix m: . XML-CODE and ON EXCEPTION The special register XML-CODE is zero after a successful XML GENERATE. If the output field is too small, XML-CODE is 400, the field holds what fit, and the ON EXCEPTION statements run. Other nonzero codes report bad data: for example, a text field containing control characters is written as hexadecimal in an element called hex.NAME and XML-CODE is 417, and a namespace that isn\u0027t a valid URI gives 416. NOT ON EXCEPTION runs when there was no exception. Examples The first XML GENERATE uses no options: every item is an element, ITEM-NO loses its leading zeros, and the ampersand in the description is escaped. The second adds the XML declaration, renames ITEM and ITEM-NO, makes ITEM-NO an attribute and drops NOTE-TEXT because it is all spaces. The third writes all the elementary items as attributes, except the SIZES group. Note that attribute values are the field\u0027s raw characters: 0017 and 00450 , with no decimal point. The last one doesn\u0027t fit in 20 characters, so XML-CODE is 400. In the first statement WITH ATTRIBUTES makes X-POS, Y-POS and QTY attributes, while TYPE OF keeps LABEL-TEXT an element. QTY is zero but still appears: SUPPRESS WHEN only removes items written as elements. The second statement writes elements with a namespace and the prefix m: , and this time the zero QTY element is suppressed. SP COBOL notes SP COBOL writes the XML itself, so no extra library has to be installed (GnuCOBOL uses libxml2). The text, the XML-CODE values and the COUNT IN results are the same as in GnuCOBOL 3.2, quirks included: A table ( OCCURS ) is not repeated: only its first entry is generated, and the compiler warns that this is \"not implemented\". Attribute values are the raw characters of the field, so a negative number shows its sign as an overpunched last character ( -123.45 in PIC S9(5)V99 appears as 001234u ). When the output field is too small, COUNT IN is one more than the length of the complete XML text (it counts a final line break). A COMP-1 or COMP-2 item in the data stops the program with a runtime error, as GnuCOBOL\u0027s runtime does. Move such values to a display field first. See also JSON GENERATE , XML PARSE (not supported), special registers (XML-CODE), STRING ."},{"t":"XML PARSE","u":"xml-parse.html","s":"Read XML text event by event. Not supported: the compiler refuses it.","k":"XML PARSE PROCESSING PROCEDURE XML-EVENT XML-TEXT XML-CODE parse read XML not implemented","c":"Statements","x":"Syntax XML PARSE identifier-1 PROCESSING PROCEDURE procedure-1 [ { THRU | THROUGH } procedure-2 ] [ ON EXCEPTION imperative-statement-1 ] [ NOT ON EXCEPTION imperative-statement-2 ] [ END-XML ] XML PARSE is not supported. SP COBOL stops with a compile error when a program uses it. What it does (in other compilers) In compilers that implement it, XML PARSE reads an XML document from a field and calls the processing procedure once for every part it finds: the start of the document, each start tag, each piece of text, each end tag, and so on. Inside the procedure, special registers such as XML-EVENT (what was found) and XML-TEXT (the text of it) tell your code what to do with each part. Example The compiler accepts the syntax, then rejects the statement with an error. The warning line is GnuCOBOL\u0027s own; the error line comes from SP COBOL. SP COBOL notes GnuCOBOL 3.2 doesn\u0027t implement XML PARSE either. It compiles the statement, but when the program runs, the runtime delivers the first event and then stops the program with the message \"XML PARSE is not implemented\". Rather than build a program that half runs, SP COBOL reports the problem when you compile. What to do instead If the XML always has the same simple, known layout (one record per line, the same tags in the same order), you can pick it apart yourself: UNSTRING with the tags as delimiters, or INSPECT to find and count them, with reference modification to take out the values. This only works for XML you control; it is not a general XML reader. For real-world XML, let a program in another language (C#, for example, which has a full XML reader built in) convert the document into a plain fixed-layout or line sequential file, and read that file from COBOL with READ . To write XML, use XML GENERATE , which is supported. See also XML GENERATE , JSON GENERATE , UNSTRING , files , not supported ."},{"t":"COPY","u":"copy.html","s":"Bring the text of a copybook into the program as it is compiled.","k":"copybook cpy include REPLACING pseudo-text == COB_COPY_DIR COBCPY shared record layout library","c":"Compiler directives","x":"Syntax COPY { text-name | literal-1 } [ REPLACING { { ==pseudo-text-1== | word-1 } BY { ==pseudo-text-2== | word-2 } } ... ] . What it does COPY is not a statement that runs; it is an instruction to the compiler. Before the program is compiled, the COPY is replaced by the whole text of another file, called a copybook . It works anywhere in the program: in the DATA DIVISION to share a record layout between programs, in the PROCEDURE DIVISION to share code. Change the copybook, rebuild, and every program that copies it picks up the change. The COPY ends with a period. That period belongs to the COPY, not to the copied text, so COPY can sit in the middle of a paragraph without ending a sentence. Naming the copybook Write the name with or without quotes: COPY CUSTREC. or COPY \"CUSTREC.cpy\". Without an extension, the compiler tries the name with the usual extensions, including .cpy , which is what SP COBOL projects use. A copybook is written in the same source format as the program. REPLACING REPLACING changes text on its way in. Each pair says what to find and what to put in its place. Text between == marks is pseudo-text : a run of one or more COBOL words matched word by word, so spacing doesn\u0027t matter. This lets one copybook serve for several records: write a placeholder in it, such as :TAG: inside each name, and replace the placeholder differently in each COPY. A placeholder in colons can be part of a longer word ( :TAG:-NAME becomes OLD-NAME ). Where copybooks are found The compiler looks for a copybook in the current folder, in each folder given to it with -I , in the folders listed in the COBCPY environment variable, and in its own copybook folder ( COB_COPY_DIR , which holds GnuCOBOL\u0027s standard copybooks such as screenio.cpy ). In an SP COBOL project in Visual Studio you don\u0027t set any of this up. Put the .cpy files in the project folder (or a subfolder) and add them to the project. The build passes the project folder, the folder of every .cbl file, and the folder of every .cpy file in the project to the compiler with -I , so COPY CUSTREC. finds CUSTREC.cpy wherever it sits in the project. A program is also rebuilt when one of the project\u0027s copybooks changes. Example: sharing a record and some code The copybook COPY1REC.cpy holds the record layout: 000010*\u003e COPY1REC: the customer record, shared by every program 000020 01 CUSTOMER-REC. 000030 05 CUST-NAME PIC X(15). 000040 05 CUST-BALANCE PIC 9(4)V99. and COPY1SHOW.cpy, named here in quotes with its extension, holds two statements: 000010 DISPLAY \"NAME: \" CUST-NAME 000020 DISPLAY \"BALANCE: \" CUST-BALANCE The first COPY puts the record into WORKING-STORAGE; the second puts the two DISPLAYs into the PROCEDURE DIVISION, so the program runs as if they had been typed in place. Example: REPLACING COPY2REC.cpy is a record with a placeholder in every name: 000010 01 :TAG:-RECORD. 000020 05 :TAG:-NAME PIC X(8). 000030 05 :TAG:-AMOUNT PIC 9(4). The first COPY makes OLD-RECORD, OLD-NAME and OLD-AMOUNT. The second makes the NEW- names and also replaces 9(4) by 9(6) , so NEW-AMOUNT has room for 120000. SP COBOL notes COPY is handled by GnuCOBOL\u0027s own front end, so it behaves exactly as in GnuCOBOL 3.2. See also REPLACE for replacing text in the program itself, DATA DIVISION , source format ."},{"t":"REPLACE","u":"replace.html","s":"Replace pieces of text throughout the source, until REPLACE OFF.","k":"REPLACE OFF pseudo-text == compiler directive substitute macro constant text replacement","c":"Compiler directives","x":"Syntax Format 1: start replacing REPLACE { ==pseudo-text-1== BY ==pseudo-text-2== } ... . Format 2: stop replacing REPLACE OFF . What it does Like COPY , REPLACE is an instruction to the compiler, not a statement that runs. From the REPLACE onward, every place where the source text matches pseudo-text-1 is compiled as if you had written pseudo-text-2 there instead. It stays in force until REPLACE OFF , until another REPLACE (which takes over from the first), or to the end of the program. Pseudo-text is the text between == marks. It is matched as COBOL words, so extra spaces or a line break between words don\u0027t matter. The replacement can be several words, or a whole statement. Quoted literals and comments are not searched: a word inside quotes is never replaced. REPLACE can go anywhere a word can, including before the DATA DIVISION. Its period belongs to it, so a REPLACE in the middle of a paragraph does not end a sentence. Uses: a size used in several places (one change updates every OCCURS and loop limit), or a short name for a statement you type often. For a value with a name, a constant (level 78 or 01 ... CONSTANT AS ) is usually clearer. Example The first REPLACE turns every TABLE-SIZE into 3 , both in the OCCURS clause and in the PERFORM\u0027s UNTIL, and every SHOW-RULE into a DISPLAY of a dashed line. Inside the literal \"TABLE-SIZE IS \" nothing changes. The second REPLACE takes over: from there on, MESSAGE-TEXT means the literal \"REPLACED TEXT\" . After REPLACE OFF , MESSAGE-TEXT is the data item again. SP COBOL notes REPLACE is handled by GnuCOBOL\u0027s own front end, so it behaves exactly as in GnuCOBOL 3.2. See also COPY (whose REPLACING phrase does the same for one copybook), constants ."},{"t":"SCREEN SECTION","u":"screen-section.html","s":"Lay out a full-screen form with labels, input fields and colors, then DISPLAY and ACCEPT it.","k":"SCREEN SECTION screen form LINE COL COLUMN PLUS VALUE USING FROM TO BLANK SCREEN BLANK LINE ERASE EOL EOS FOREGROUND-COLOR BACKGROUND-COLOR HIGHLIGHT LOWLIGHT REVERSE-VIDEO UNDERLINE BLINK SECURE REQUIRED AUTO FULL BELL CRT STATUS CURSOR IS cursor position function keys F1 F3 Esc COB-CRT-STATUS console window COB_SCREEN DISPLAY AT ACCEPT AT","c":"Topics","x":"A SCREEN SECTION describes a whole screen at once: where each label goes, which fields the user can type into, and their colors. DISPLAY screen-name draws it and ACCEPT screen-name lets the user fill it in, moving between fields with Tab, until they press Enter or a function key. In SP COBOL the same program runs as a DOS-style text screen in a console window, or as a real Windows window. Syntax Format 1: describe a screen (last section of the DATA DIVISION) SCREEN SECTION. 01 screen-name [ attributes ] . nn [ item-name ] [ LINE NUMBER IS [ PLUS ] integer-1 ] [ { COLUMN | COL } NUMBER IS [ PLUS ] integer-2 ] { VALUE IS literal-1 | { PICTURE | PIC } IS picture-string { USING identifier-1 | FROM { identifier-2 | literal-2 } | TO identifier-3 } ... } [ attributes ] . attributes: BLANK SCREEN | BLANK LINE | ERASE { EOL | EOS } FOREGROUND-COLOR integer-3 | BACKGROUND-COLOR integer-4 HIGHLIGHT | LOWLIGHT | REVERSE-VIDEO | UNDERLINE | BLINK SECURE | REQUIRED | AUTO | FULL | BELL Format 2: show and fill in a screen DISPLAY screen-name ACCEPT screen-name Format 3: one field at a position DISPLAY { identifier-4 | literal-3 } LINE integer-5 { COLUMN | COL } integer-6 [ WITH { BLANK SCREEN | BLANK LINE | ERASE { EOL | EOS } | HIGHLIGHT | REVERSE-VIDEO | UNDERLINE | FOREGROUND-COLOR integer-3 | BACKGROUND-COLOR integer-4 } ... ] ACCEPT identifier-5 LINE integer-5 { COLUMN | COL } integer-6 [ WITH { AUTO | SECURE | REQUIRED | UPPER | LOWER | UPDATE | HIGHLIGHT ... } ... ] Format 4: which key ended the ACCEPT, and where the cursor starts (ENVIRONMENT DIVISION) SPECIAL-NAMES. [ CRT STATUS IS identifier-6 ] [ CURSOR IS identifier-7 ] . What it does Screen items Screen items are numbered like data items: an 01 for the whole screen, and 05 (or any higher level) for the parts. An item is one of three kinds: a literal , VALUE \"Name:\" : text that is shown and can\u0027t be changed; a field , PIC X(20) USING CUST-NAME : a place on the screen tied to a WORKING-STORAGE item; a group with no PIC or VALUE, which holds other items and passes its attributes (such as colors) down to them. A field\u0027s PIC sets how wide it is and how its value looks, so an edited picture such as ZZ,ZZ9.99 works on screens as it does in MOVE . Three words say which way the data goes: Clause DISPLAY shows ACCEPT stores what was typed into FROM item the item\u0027s value nothing: the field is output only TO item an empty field the item USING item the item\u0027s value the same item Positions Lines are numbered from 1 at the top and columns from 1 at the left, on an 80 by 25 screen. LINE 3 COL 10 is an absolute position. LINE PLUS 2 is two lines below the previous item, and COL PLUS 2 starts two columns after the end of the previous item (so one blank column between them). An item with no LINE stays on the previous item\u0027s line, and one with no COL follows straight on after the previous item. Clearing BLANK SCREEN clears the whole screen before the screen is drawn, in the colors given with it. BLANK LINE clears the item\u0027s line first; ERASE EOL clears from the item to the end of its line; ERASE EOS from the item to the end of the screen. Colors and looks FOREGROUND-COLOR is the color of the characters and BACKGROUND-COLOR the color behind them. The numbers are the eight colors of the PC text screen: Number Color Number Color 0 black 4 red 1 blue 5 magenta 2 green 6 brown (yellow when HIGHLIGHT) 3 cyan 7 white (light gray) HIGHLIGHT uses the bright version of the foreground color, REVERSE-VIDEO swaps the foreground and background colors, and UNDERLINE underlines. LOWLIGHT , BLINK and BELL are accepted but have no visible effect in SP COBOL. Input fields SECURE : what is typed shows as * , and the field is drawn empty afterwards. Use it for passwords. REQUIRED : Enter is refused while this field is empty; the cursor goes to it with the message \"Please fill in this field.\" AUTO : when the field is full, the cursor moves on to the next field by itself (on the last field, the ACCEPT ends as if Enter was pressed). FULL is accepted but not enforced by SP COBOL. While a screen is accepted the keys work like this: typing replaces or overwrites characters (Insert switches to inserting), Left/Right, Home/End, Backspace and Delete edit, Tab or Down goes to the next field, Shift+Tab or Up to the previous one. Fields are visited top to bottom, left to right. Enter, Esc, PgUp, PgDn and the function keys end the ACCEPT. Input fields show an underscore in each empty position. CRT STATUS: which key was pressed Name a 4-digit field in CRT STATUS IS (in SPECIAL-NAMES, see ENVIRONMENT DIVISION ) and every ACCEPT puts there the key that ended it: Key Code Enter 0000 F1 to F24 1001 to 1024 Page Up / Page Down 2001 / 2002 Esc 2005 ACCEPT of a screen that has no input fields 8000 Without a CRT STATUS clause the code goes to the special register COB-CRT-STATUS (see special registers ). Up and Down move between fields rather than ending the ACCEPT, so they don\u0027t return a code. CURSOR IS: where the ACCEPT starts Normally an ACCEPT of a screen starts in its first input field. Name a 4-digit field (line and column, LLCC ) in CURSOR IS in SPECIAL-NAMES, and the ACCEPT starts in the input field at that position instead: with 0410 in it, the field at line 4, column 10. When the ACCEPT ends, the field holds the position of the input field the cursor was in. Single fields with LINE and COL Without a SCREEN SECTION you can still place things: DISPLAY \"Age:\" LINE 3 COL 1 shows a value at a position, and ACCEPT WS-AGE LINE 3 COL 8 reads one field there. Such an ACCEPT starts with an empty field; add WITH UPDATE to start from the item\u0027s current value. WITH UPPER or LOWER changes the case of letters as they are typed. Once the screen is in use, a plain DISPLAY (without LINE/COL) writes on the next line of the screen. See also ACCEPT and DISPLAY . One program, two kinds of screen SP COBOL draws the same SCREEN SECTION in two ways. The console version is a DOS-style text screen in a console window. The Windows version is a window with a grid of character cells, where the input fields are real text boxes. The program\u0027s logic is the same in both: ACCEPT waits until Enter or a function key, then returns with the fields filled in. A \"COBOL Screen App\" project has a ScreenMode property: Both (the default) builds NAME.exe (console) and NAME-Win.exe (window) from the one source; Console or Forms builds only one. When running, the environment variable COB_SCREEN=console or COB_SCREEN=forms picks the kind of screen. Examples These examples are run with keystrokes from a script; the boxes show the screen as it looked at each ACCEPT and when the program ended, and the lines under a box say which key ended that ACCEPT ( [key 0] is Enter) and any message or beep. Colors are not shown. A data-entry screen The keys were Tab, Enter, \"LONDON\", Tab, \"1234\", Enter. The name field starts with \"ADA LOVELACE\" because of USING; Tab leaves it unchanged. The first Enter is refused, since City is REQUIRED and empty, so there is a beep, a message, and a second round of ACCEPT. The PIN shows as stars while typed and is blanked afterwards, but the program received 1234. \"Customers so far:\" comes from FROM: shown, never typed into. The last lines are single-field DISPLAYs with LINE and COL. Function keys and CRT STATUS The keys were F1, \"12\" Enter, F5, F3. Each ACCEPT puts the key code in WS-KEY (1001 for F1, 0 for Enter, 1005, 1003 for F3) and the EVALUATE picks a message, which the next DISPLAY shows. ERASE EOL clears the rest of line 5 each time. Single fields, and number checks The keys were \"a42\" Enter, \"1.5\" Enter, \"-5\" Enter, \"7\" Enter, \"abc\". The letter \"a\" is refused in a numeric field. \"1.5\" is refused because WS-QTY is PIC 9(3), whole numbers only, and \"-5\" because it has no sign; each time the cursor stays in the field. The 7 is shown as 007, the way PIC 9(3) shows it. The Code field is AUTO and UPPER, so \"abc\" becomes ABC and the ACCEPT ends when the third letter fills it. Starting in a chosen field, and an assumed decimal point The keys were \"12.5\" Enter. WS-CURSOR holds 0410, so the ACCEPT starts in the Price field on line 4, column 10, not in Item, and Item keeps PENCIL. The Price field has the same picture as WS-PRICE, 9(3)V99 : it shows 1.25 as 00125, with no point, and the 12.5 typed is stored as 12.50. After the ACCEPT, WS-CURSOR holds 0410, the start of the field the cursor was in. SP COBOL notes Differs from GnuCOBOL 3.2 , on purpose, to suit beginners (plain DISPLAY and ACCEPT without screens are not affected): An item with no color given takes the colors of the last BLANK SCREEN, not the terminal\u0027s default colors. The first key typed in a field replaces its old contents, like a Windows text box. GnuCOBOL overwrites in place. To edit the old value instead, press an arrow key, Home or End first. Numeric fields are checked when you leave them, with plain-English messages (\"This field holds whole numbers only.\"), and are shown again through their PICTURE. After an ACCEPT, the CURSOR IS field holds the start position of the input field the cursor was in. GnuCOBOL gets the exact column from its curses library, so there it can be a column inside the field, after what was typed. A numeric input field with an assumed decimal point ( PIC 9(3)V99 ) shows no point, which is hard to read. An edited picture with a real point, such as PIC ZZ9.99 USING WS-PRICE , is friendlier. On ACCEPT ... LINE ... COL , the phrases for TIMEOUT, SCROLL, SIZE, CONTROL and CURSOR are accepted but ignored. JUSTIFIED and BLANK WHEN ZERO on screen items are accepted; JUSTIFIED RIGHT had no visible effect in a test. Screen examples can\u0027t be compared with stock GnuCOBOL, because the reference build has no screen support, so the screens above were checked by eye. See also ACCEPT , DISPLAY , ENVIRONMENT DIVISION (CRT STATUS), PICTURE , Differences from GnuCOBOL ."},{"t":"Files","u":"files.html","s":"How a COBOL program names, describes, opens, reads, writes and closes data files.","k":"SELECT ASSIGN ORGANIZATION SEQUENTIAL LINE SEQUENTIAL RELATIVE INDEXED ACCESS MODE RANDOM DYNAMIC RECORD KEY ALTERNATE RECORD KEY DUPLICATES RELATIVE KEY FILE STATUS FD RECORD CONTAINS VARYING DEPENDING BLOCK CONTAINS LINAGE OPTIONAL FILE-CONTROL FILE SECTION COB_FILE_PATH DD_ COB_NO_HINTS hint data file ISAM SPCOBIX1","c":"Topics","x":"Syntax Format 1: name the file (ENVIRONMENT DIVISION, INPUT-OUTPUT SECTION, FILE-CONTROL) SELECT [ OPTIONAL ] file-name ASSIGN TO { literal-1 | identifier-1 | DISPLAY | KEYBOARD } [ ORGANIZATION IS ] { SEQUENTIAL | LINE SEQUENTIAL | RELATIVE | INDEXED } [ ACCESS MODE IS { SEQUENTIAL | RANDOM | DYNAMIC } ] [ RELATIVE KEY IS identifier-2 ] [ RECORD KEY IS identifier-3 ] [ ALTERNATE RECORD KEY IS identifier-4 [ WITH DUPLICATES ] ] ... [ FILE STATUS IS identifier-5 ] . Format 2: describe its records (DATA DIVISION, FILE SECTION) FD file-name [ IS GLOBAL ] [ BLOCK CONTAINS integer-1 [ TO integer-2 ] { RECORDS | CHARACTERS } ] [ RECORD CONTAINS integer-3 CHARACTERS ] [ RECORD IS VARYING IN SIZE FROM integer-4 TO integer-5 CHARACTERS DEPENDING ON identifier-6 ] [ LINAGE IS integer-7 LINES [ WITH FOOTING AT integer-8 ] [ LINES AT TOP integer-9 ] [ LINES AT BOTTOM integer-10 ] ] . 01 record-name ... [ 01 record-name-2 ... ] ... What it does A COBOL program works with a file in three places. In the ENVIRONMENT DIVISION, a SELECT entry gives the file a name inside the program and says where it is on disk and how it is organized. In the DATA DIVISION, an FD (file description) describes the records. In the PROCEDURE DIVISION, statements open the file, move records in and out, and close it. A record is one unit of data in the file, such as one customer or one line of a report. The 01 level under the FD is the record area : READ puts the next record there, and WRITE takes the record from there. The program can only see one record of a file at a time. ASSIGN: which file on disk ASSIGN TO \"pay.dat\" names the file on disk. A name with no folder in it means a file in the current folder , the folder the program is running in (in Visual Studio, normally the output folder beside the .exe). You can also give a full path such as \"C:\\data\\pay.dat\" . ASSIGN TO a data item ( ASSIGN TO LOG-NAME ) uses whatever name is in that item when OPEN runs, so one SELECT can open different files at different times. The trailing spaces of the item are ignored. Before the name is used, the runtime looks for an environment variable that replaces it, the way GnuCOBOL does: For a name with no folder in it, such as pay.dat , it tries the variables DD_pay_dat , dd_pay_dat and pay_dat (dots become underscores). The first one that is set gives the real file name. So set DD_pay_dat=C:\\test\\pay2.dat redirects the file without changing the program. A part of a path that starts with $ , as in \"$DATA\\pay.dat\" , is replaced by that variable (here DATA , also tried as DD_DATA and dd_DATA ). If COB_FILE_PATH is set, a name that isn\u0027t a full path is looked for in that folder instead of the current one. ASSIGN TO DISPLAY makes a LINE SEQUENTIAL file that writes to the screen (standard output), and ASSIGN TO KEYBOARD one that reads from standard input. OPTIONAL means the file may be missing: opening it for input then succeeds with status 05 and the first READ is at end (see OPEN ). ORGANIZATION: how records are stored Organization On disk Use it for SEQUENTIAL (the default) Records one after another, each exactly as long as the record area, with nothing between them. Variable-length records ( RECORD VARYING ) get a 4-byte length header each. Data that is always read from start to end. LINE SEQUENTIAL A text file: one record per line, trailing spaces removed, each line ended by CR LF. You can open it in Notepad. Text files, reports, data shared with other programs. RELATIVE Numbered slots 1, 2, 3, ... of equal size. A slot can be empty. The RELATIVE KEY item holds the slot number. Data looked up by a small number, such as a room or seat number. INDEXED Records found by a key field inside the record ( RECORD KEY ), and optionally by more keys ( ALTERNATE RECORD KEY ). Data looked up by a code or name, read in key order. ACCESS MODE: how the program gets at the records SEQUENTIAL (the default): records are read and written in order, one after the next. RANDOM : each READ or WRITE goes straight to the record whose key you set first: the slot number in the RELATIVE KEY, or the value in the RECORD KEY field. DYNAMIC : both. Use READ ... KEY or a plain READ for direct access, and READ ... NEXT (often after START ) to carry on in order from there. LINE SEQUENTIAL and SEQUENTIAL files are always accessed sequentially. Keys RELATIVE KEY names an unsigned numeric item in WORKING-STORAGE (not in the record). With random or dynamic access you put the slot number there before READ, WRITE, REWRITE, DELETE or START; with sequential access the runtime puts the number of each slot it reads or writes there. RECORD KEY names a field inside the record. Each record must have a different value in it; writing a second record with the same key fails with status 22. An ALTERNATE RECORD KEY is another field you can read by. Add WITH DUPLICATES if several records may share a value (several customers in one city, for example). FILE STATUS FILE STATUS IS FS names a two-character item ( PIC XX ) that the runtime sets after every statement on the file: \"00\" for success, \"10\" for end of file, \"35\" for \"file not found\", and so on. Always give your files one, and check it. FILE STATUS codes lists them all. Several files may share one status item. The FD Each SELECT needs a matching FD with at least one 01 record. Some useful clauses: RECORD CONTAINS 14 CHARACTERS states the record size. It must agree with the 01 level; it adds nothing else. RECORD IS VARYING ... DEPENDING ON LEN makes the records variable in length: before a WRITE you put the length in LEN, and after a READ LEN holds the length of the record just read. BLOCK CONTAINS comes from tape and old mainframe files. It is accepted and has no effect: the file is the same with or without it. LINAGE divides a LINE SEQUENTIAL report file into pages, with margins at the top and bottom and a footing area. See WRITE for an example. Several 01 levels under one FD are different views of the same record area, like an automatic REDEFINES . A file of heading lines and detail lines can describe both shapes and use whichever fits the record in hand. A READ fills the area; you decide which view to look at. The life cycle of a file Every file goes through the same steps: OPEN it, saying what you will do: INPUT (read), OUTPUT (write a new file), I-O (read and change) or EXTEND (add to the end). READ , WRITE , REWRITE , DELETE and START as needed. CLOSE it. A file left open when the program ends is closed for you, with a warning. Which statements work where Statement SEQUENTIAL LINE SEQUENTIAL RELATIVE INDEXED OPEN INPUT, OUTPUT, I-O yes yes yes yes OPEN EXTEND yes yes sequential access sequential access READ (next record) yes yes sequential or dynamic access sequential or dynamic access READ by key no no random or dynamic access random or dynamic access READ PREVIOUS no no see READ dynamic access WRITE OUTPUT, EXTEND OUTPUT, EXTEND OUTPUT, I-O (EXTEND) OUTPUT, I-O (EXTEND) REWRITE I-O, after a READ I-O, after a READ I-O I-O DELETE no no I-O I-O START no no sequential or dynamic access sequential or dynamic access With sequential access, REWRITE and DELETE work on the record the last READ returned, so a READ must come first (status 43 otherwise). With random or dynamic access they work on the record whose key you set. Examples A sequential file: write, then read back The program writes three 14-character records, closes the file, opens it again for input, and reads until AT END . The status after the last READ is 10, end of file. The BLOCK and RECORD CONTAINS clauses change nothing: pay.dat is 42 bytes, three records end to end. A line sequential file with two record layouts notes.txt is an ordinary text file. TITLE-LINE and ITEM-LINE share one record area; the program writes a title through one and items through the other, and reads them back the same way. This loop tests the FILE STATUS instead of using AT END . A relative file Only slots 2 and 7 are written. Reading slot by slot shows the others as empty: the READ takes its INVALID KEY branch with status 23, record not found. An indexed file Records are written in any order and come back in key order. The third WRITE gets status 02: it worked, but another record already has bin A01 (allowed, because of WITH DUPLICATES ). This example isn\u0027t compared with stock GnuCOBOL, whose reference build has no indexed file support. Choosing the file at run time LOG-NAME holds the file name, so the same SELECT writes day1.log and day2.log , then reads the second one back. SP COBOL notes SEQUENTIAL, LINE SEQUENTIAL and RELATIVE files are written byte for byte the way GnuCOBOL 3.2 writes them (CR LF line ends, the 4-byte header of variable-length records, the 8-byte header of each relative slot), so programs built with either can share data files. The FILE STATUS values and the AT END / INVALID KEY / END-OF-PAGE conditions are the same too. Where GnuCOBOL 3.2 has a plain bug in its file handling, SP COBOL does what the code was clearly meant to do, or what the COBOL standard says (see also Differences from GnuCOBOL ): Situation GnuCOBOL 3.2 SP COBOL A TAB, form feed or ESC character inside a LINE SEQUENTIAL record WRITE refuses it with status 71 written and read back as is A file name in quotes inside the literal, such as \"\u0027a.dat\u0027\" the first quote stays in the name; OPEN fails with 30 the quotes are removed $VAR in the middle of a path the \\ after it is lost the separators are kept Reading a LINE SEQUENTIAL file read in Windows text mode: a Ctrl-Z byte ends the file, and a file with LF-only line ends can lose text when a long line is split CR LF and LF both end a line; Ctrl-Z is ordinary data (status 09, like other control characters) REWRITE of a shorter line in a LINE SEQUENTIAL file overwrites the CR of the line end the line end is kept REWRITE or DELETE of an empty RELATIVE slot status 00 (REWRITE even fills the slot) status 23, record not found READ PREVIOUS on a RELATIVE file ( READ ) skips a record the record before the last one read, as the standard says READ NEXT on a RELATIVE file after a WRITE by key at end of file the WRITE doesn\u0027t move the position, as the standard says A sequential WRITE to a RELATIVE file whose slot number doesn\u0027t fit the RELATIVE KEY ( WRITE ) status 00, the record is written and the key set to 0 status 24, nothing written The error message for a missing file names the last environment variable it tried names the ASSIGN value INDEXED files use SP COBOL\u0027s own format. GnuCOBOL keeps indexed files through an outside library (Berkeley DB or VBISAM); SP COBOL uses a simple one-file format of its own, pure .NET, with no extra DLLs. The file starts with the header SPCOBIX1 and the key layout, followed by the records in the order they were written. OPEN reads the whole file and builds the key indexes in memory, which is fine for files of thousands of records but not millions. Deleted records leave unused space that isn\u0027t reused. The FILE STATUS values are the ones GnuCOBOL reports (02, 21, 22, 23, 39, ...). Indexed files written by SP COBOL and by GnuCOBOL can\u0027t be read by each other. When a file error stops the program (no FILE STATUS and no USE procedure), SP COBOL prints GnuCOBOL\u0027s message and then one hint: line for learners, for example: libcob: error: file does not exist (status = 35) for file NOFILE (\u0027no-such-file.dat\u0027) hint: Check the name in ASSIGN. The program looked in C:\\work\\bin. Use OPEN OUTPUT (or SELECT OPTIONAL) to create the file. Set the environment variable COB_NO_HINTS=1 to leave the hint out. The program ends with exit code 1, as in GnuCOBOL. See also OPEN , CLOSE , READ , WRITE , REWRITE , DELETE , START , FILE STATUS codes , USE for error procedures, SORT and MERGE , Report Writer , ENVIRONMENT DIVISION , DATA DIVISION ."},{"t":"FILE STATUS codes","u":"file-status.html","s":"The two-character code set after every file operation, what each value means, and how to test it.","k":"FILE STATUS status code 00 02 04 05 06 07 09 10 14 21 22 23 24 30 31 35 37 38 39 41 42 43 44 46 47 48 49 57 61 71 91 error end of file not found duplicate key 88 condition","c":"Topics","x":"Syntax SELECT file-name ASSIGN TO ... FILE STATUS IS identifier-1 . WORKING-STORAGE SECTION. 01 identifier-1 PIC XX. [ 88 condition-name VALUE \"nn\" [ \"nn\" ] ... . ] ... What it does After every OPEN, CLOSE, READ, WRITE, REWRITE, DELETE and START on a file, the runtime stores a two-character code in the file\u0027s FILE STATUS item. \"00\" means the statement worked. Anything else tells you what happened, so you can react instead of letting the program stop. The first character is the class of the result: 0 : success. The second digit may add a remark (a duplicate alternate key, a missing OPTIONAL file, a shortened record). 1 : AT END. There is no next record. 2 : INVALID KEY. A key was wrong for this operation: not found, already there, or out of order. 3 : a permanent error, usually with the file itself: missing, not allowed, or not the shape the program expects. 4 : a logic error: the statement didn\u0027t make sense at that moment, such as reading a file that isn\u0027t open. 9 : other errors from the runtime. When a statement fails (class 3, 4 or 9, or 1 and 2 with no AT END / INVALID KEY phrase) and the file has neither a FILE STATUS item nor a USE procedure, the program stops with an error message. With a FILE STATUS item, the program carries on and it\u0027s up to you to look at the code. Declaring and testing it Declare the item as PIC XX in WORKING-STORAGE, and name it in the SELECT. Testing it with 88-level condition names makes the code read well: IF FS-NOT-FOUND rather than IF FS = \"35\" . Check it right after the statement; the next file statement replaces it. To act on a whole class, test the first character: IF FS(1:1) = \"3\" . The codes Code Meaning What to check 00 Success. 02 Success, and another record has the same alternate key value (INDEXED, WITH DUPLICATES ). On READ it means the next record has the same key. Nothing, unless the key was meant to be unique. 04 Success, but the record read was not the length the FD allows (too short or too long for a variable-length record). The RECORD VARYING sizes against the file. 05 OPEN of an OPTIONAL file that doesn\u0027t exist. The open worked; reading gives end of file at once. Nothing; this is how OPTIONAL files work. 06 READ of a LINE SEQUENTIAL line longer than the record: you got the first part, the next READ returns the rest. Make the record longer. 07 CLOSE REEL / UNIT or NO REWIND on a disk file: done, but the phrase has no meaning on disk. Use a plain CLOSE. 09 READ of a LINE SEQUENTIAL line containing a control character such as Ctrl-Z. The file may not be text. 10 End of file: no next record (sequential READ, READ NEXT, RETURN). Normal at the end of a loop. 14 Sequential READ of a RELATIVE file reached a slot number too big for the RELATIVE KEY item. The record is still read, and the key is set to 0. Give the RELATIVE KEY more digits. 21 Key out of order: an INDEXED file with sequential access must be written in ascending key order (after OPEN EXTEND too: above the highest key already there). Sort the data first, or use random access. 22 Duplicate key: WRITE to a RELATIVE slot that is in use, or WRITE of an INDEXED record whose RECORD KEY is already in the file. Read first, or REWRITE instead. 23 Record not found: READ, START, REWRITE or DELETE of a key or slot that isn\u0027t there. The key value; normal when looking things up. 24 Key outside the file\u0027s limits, such as RELATIVE KEY 0, or a sequential WRITE of a RELATIVE file whose next slot number is too big for the RELATIVE KEY item (nothing is written). Slot numbers start at 1. Give the RELATIVE KEY more digits. 30 Permanent error reported by Windows while reading or writing. The disk, the path, the file. 31 The file name is empty or badly quoted. The ASSIGN data item has a name in it when OPEN runs. 35 OPEN INPUT, I-O or EXTEND of a file that doesn\u0027t exist. The name, and the current folder. Use OPEN OUTPUT or SELECT OPTIONAL to create it. 37 Permission denied: the file is read-only, or the name is a folder. The file\u0027s properties. 38 OPEN of a file closed earlier WITH LOCK . Don\u0027t lock it if you need it again. 39 The file doesn\u0027t match the program: wrong record size, or (INDEXED) a different key layout. The FD against the program that wrote the file. 41 OPEN of a file that is already open. CLOSE it first. 42 CLOSE of a file that isn\u0027t open. 43 REWRITE or DELETE with sequential access, with no successful READ just before. READ the record first. 44 Record size wrong: WRITE or REWRITE of a record shorter or longer than the FD allows. The DEPENDING ON item before WRITE. 46 READ after the previous READ already hit end of file or failed. Stop the loop at status 10. 47 READ or START on a file that isn\u0027t open for INPUT or I-O (or is closed). The OPEN mode. 48 WRITE on a file that isn\u0027t open for OUTPUT, EXTEND (or I-O for RELATIVE and INDEXED). The OPEN mode. 49 REWRITE or DELETE on a file not open I-O. Open it I-O. 57 The LINAGE values make no sense (footing beyond the page, for example). The LINAGE clause. 61 The file is already open elsewhere, in this program or another one. Close the other user, such as Excel. 91 The operation isn\u0027t available for this kind of file. GnuCOBOL also defines 34 (disk full or a boundary passed) and 51 (record locked); SP COBOL doesn\u0027t produce them. Examples Each line shows the status after one statement on a sequential file: 35 because the file doesn\u0027t exist yet, 41 for opening twice, 47 for reading a file opened for output, 42 for closing twice, 48 for writing a closed file, 10 at the end, 46 for reading past the end, 49 for REWRITE when the file is open INPUT, and 43 for REWRITE with no READ before it. The key and record codes: 22 for a slot already in use, 24 for slot 0, 23 for an empty slot, 44 for a 1-character record when the FD says at least 2. Reading a 10-character line into a 6-character record gives 06 and the first six characters; the next READ returns the rest with 00. CLOSE ... REEL on a disk file gives 07 and leaves the file open, so a plain CLOSE follows. Slots 8 to 11 are written through ROOM-NO, which has two digits. The file is then read in order through a second SELECT whose RELATIVE KEY, SHORT-NO, has only one digit. Slots 8 and 9 read normally. Slot 10 doesn\u0027t fit: status 14, SHORT-NO is set to 0, and the record ROOM10 is still read. SP COBOL notes The codes are the ones GnuCOBOL 3.2 sets in each situation. The exceptions are listed on the Files page, for example REWRITE or DELETE of an empty RELATIVE slot, which gives 23 here and 00 in GnuCOBOL, and a sequential WRITE whose slot number doesn\u0027t fit the RELATIVE KEY, which gives 24 here and 00 in GnuCOBOL (see WRITE ). When a file error stops the program, SP COBOL adds a hint: line under GnuCOBOL\u0027s message saying what to check. COB_NO_HINTS=1 turns it off. See also Files , USE for error procedures, OPEN , READ , WRITE , condition names ."},{"t":"Report Writer","u":"report-writer.html","s":"Describe a printed report\u0027s layout once, then let COBOL handle headings, page breaks and totals.","k":"REPORT SECTION RD REPORT IS CONTROLS FINAL PAGE LIMIT HEADING FIRST DETAIL LAST DETAIL FOOTING TYPE DETAIL CONTROL HEADING CONTROL FOOTING PAGE HEADING PAGE FOOTING REPORT HEADING REPORT FOOTING LINE NUMBER PLUS NEXT GROUP COLUMN CENTER RIGHT LEFT SOURCE SUM RESET UPON GROUP INDICATE PRESENT WHEN PAGE-COUNTER LINE-COUNTER CODE USE BEFORE REPORTING control break subtotal printed report","c":"Topics","x":"Most business programs end by printing something: a list with a heading on every page, subtotals when a department or region changes, and a grand total at the end. Writing that by hand means counting lines, remembering the previous value of a field and adding up totals yourself. The Report Writer does all of that for you. You describe what each kind of line looks like in the REPORT SECTION, and the PROCEDURE DIVISION needs only four statements: INITIATE , GENERATE , TERMINATE and, in a declarative, SUPPRESS . Syntax Format 1: name the report in the file\u0027s FD FD file-name { REPORT IS | REPORTS ARE } report-name ... Format 2: describe the report (DATA DIVISION, after WORKING-STORAGE) REPORT SECTION. RD report-name [ { CONTROL IS | CONTROLS ARE } [ FINAL ] identifier-1 ... ] [ PAGE [ LIMIT IS ] integer-1 [ LINES ] [ HEADING integer-2 ] [ FIRST DETAIL integer-3 ] [ LAST DETAIL integer-4 ] [ FOOTING integer-5 ] ] . Format 3: a report group (level 01) and its lines (02-49) 01 [ group-name ] TYPE IS { REPORT HEADING | RH | PAGE HEADING | PH | CONTROL HEADING | CH { identifier-1 | FINAL } | DETAIL | DE | CONTROL FOOTING | CF { identifier-1 | FINAL } | PAGE FOOTING | PF | REPORT FOOTING | RF } [ NEXT GROUP IS { integer-6 | PLUS integer-7 | NEXT PAGE } ] . nn LINE NUMBER IS { integer-8 | PLUS integer-9 } [ PRESENT WHEN condition-1 ] . nn [ field-name ] COLUMN NUMBER IS [ LEFT | CENTER | RIGHT ] integer-10 PICTURE IS picture-string { SOURCE IS identifier-2 | VALUE IS literal-1 | SUM identifier-3 ... [ RESET ON { identifier-4 | FINAL } ] } [ GROUP INDICATE ] . Format 4: a procedure that runs just before a group prints DECLARATIVES. section-name SECTION. USE BEFORE REPORTING group-name. paragraph-name. statements ... END DECLARATIVES. What it does The pieces A report is written to a file, so you need a SELECT and an FD as for any output file (see Files ). The FD has no record description. Instead it says REPORT IS report-name , and the layout goes in an RD (report description) entry in the REPORT SECTION. A LINE SEQUENTIAL file is the usual choice: each printed line becomes one line of a text file. In the PROCEDURE DIVISION you OPEN OUTPUT the file, INITIATE the report, GENERATE a detail line for each record, TERMINATE the report and CLOSE the file. Headings, footings, page breaks and totals happen automatically during GENERATE and TERMINATE. The RD entry CONTROLS ARE lists the control fields , from the most important to the least. When the value of a control field changes between one GENERATE and the next, that\u0027s a control break : the footing for that field prints (with its subtotals), then the heading for the new value. A break in one field also counts as a break in every field listed after it. FINAL is a pretend control field that \"breaks\" only once, at TERMINATE, which is where grand totals go. Your data must already be in order by the control fields (see SORT ). PAGE LIMIT is the number of lines on a page. HEADING is the first line anything may print on, FIRST DETAIL the first line for detail and control groups, LAST DETAIL the last line for detail lines, and FOOTING the last line for control footings. When the next group won\u0027t fit, the page footing prints and a new page starts with the page heading. Always give a PAGE clause. Without one there are no pages, and line positions are not what you\u0027d expect: in a test, LINE PLUS 2 left no blank line, and an unwanted blank line appeared inside the heading. Report groups Each 01 level in the RD is a report group , one or more lines that print together. Its TYPE says when it prints: TYPE Prints REPORT HEADING once, at the start of the report PAGE HEADING at the top of every page CONTROL HEADING field before the first detail of each new value of the field DETAIL each time you GENERATE it CONTROL FOOTING field after the last detail of each value of the field (subtotals) CONTROL FOOTING FINAL once, at TERMINATE (grand totals) PAGE FOOTING at the bottom of every page REPORT FOOTING once, at TERMINATE, after everything else. In SP COBOL and GnuCOBOL it starts a new page. A group needs a name only if the program refers to it: a DETAIL you GENERATE, or a group named in USE BEFORE REPORTING. Lines and columns LINE NUMBER IS 3 prints on line 3 of the page (absolute). LINE NUMBER IS PLUS 1 prints one line below the previous line (relative); PLUS 2 leaves one blank line. NEXT GROUP on a group moves the position after the group prints: PLUS n skips n lines, NEXT PAGE forces a page break, and an integer moves to that line. COLUMN 9 starts the field in column 9. COLUMN CENTER 20 centers it on column 20, and COLUMN RIGHT 40 makes it end in column 40. COLUMN LEFT is the same as plain COLUMN . Every printed field has a PICTURE , usually an edited one such as Z,ZZ9.99 (see PICTURE ). Where a field\u0027s value comes from VALUE \"text\" : fixed text, such as a column title. SOURCE identifier : the field\u0027s current value, moved in when the group prints. PAGE-COUNTER and LINE-COUNTER are allowed here. SUM identifier : a running total. Every GENERATE adds the identifier into the counter; when the footing prints, the counter is printed and reset to zero. Give the sum field a name and a higher footing can SUM that name: a sum of sums , as the grand total in the first example does. RESET ON FINAL (or RESET ON a higher control field) keeps the counter running across smaller breaks, which gives a cumulative total. SUM ... UPON detail-name is accepted, but in GnuCOBOL 3.2 and SP COBOL the counter still grows on every GENERATE of every detail, so don\u0027t rely on it. Printing only some of the time GROUP INDICATE on a detail field prints it only on the first detail after a control break or a new page, so a region name isn\u0027t repeated on every line. PRESENT WHEN condition on a LINE prints that line only when the condition is true. The second example uses it for a warning line. PRESENT WHEN on a single field (rather than a LINE) compiles, but GnuCOBOL 3.2 never prints such a field, whether the condition is true or not. SP COBOL keeps that behavior. Put the field on a line of its own and use PRESENT WHEN on the LINE, or move the text into a SOURCE field yourself. PAGE-COUNTER and LINE-COUNTER Each report has two counters, which you can DISPLAY or use as a SOURCE. PAGE-COUNTER is the page number (1 after INITIATE). LINE-COUNTER is the line last printed on the current page. With two reports, say which one: PAGE-COUNTER OF SALES-REPORT . See special registers . USE BEFORE REPORTING A section in the DECLARATIVES with USE BEFORE REPORTING group-name runs just before that group prints. It can change the fields the group\u0027s SOURCE clauses read, and it can stop the group from printing with SUPPRESS PRINTING . During a control footing, the control field still holds the old value (the one being totaled), so the declarative sees the group that is ending. Examples Subtotals and a grand total The report goes to rw1.txt ; after closing it the program reads the file back and displays each line, so you can see it. The page heading takes lines 1 and 2, and details start on line 4 (FIRST DETAIL 4). When WS-REGION changes from EAST to WEST the EAST footing prints with its SUM. REGION-TOTAL is a named SUM field, so the FINAL footing can add up the region totals. The empty lines at the end pad the page out to its 16 lines. Pages, GROUP INDICATE and PRESENT WHEN The page is 12 lines long and details may go no lower than line 10, so DAVE\u0027s line is the last that fits on page 1; the page footing prints on line 12 and page 2 starts with the heading again. The department name is GROUP INDICATE, so it prints only on the first line of each department, and once more at the top of page 2. The extra line under BOB and ERIN is a LINE with PRESENT WHEN WS-HOURS \u003e 50. NEXT GROUP PLUS 1 on the footing leaves a blank line after each total. \"STAFF HOURS\" is centered on column 15 and \"HOURS\" ends in column 20. The DISPLAY lines show PAGE-COUNTER and LINE-COUNTER after each GENERATE. A USE BEFORE REPORTING declarative on a detail line The declarative runs before each ORDER-LINE prints and fills in WS-FLAG, which the line prints through its SOURCE clause. The total is 12.50 + 240.00 + 35.25 = 287.75. SP COBOL notes SP COBOL\u0027s Report Writer is a step-by-step port of GnuCOBOL\u0027s own (libcob\u0027s reportio.c ), so reports come out the same, line for line, as with GnuCOBOL 3.2. One exception is a bug fix: Differs from GnuCOBOL 3.2: when a DETAIL group has a USE BEFORE REPORTING declarative, stock GnuCOBOL 3.2 adds each detail into the SUM counters twice, so every total is doubled (the third example prints 563.00 there); it can also run control-heading declaratives again and print a page heading twice. SP COBOL adds each detail once and prints 287.75. Declaratives on headings and footings behave the same in both. Don\u0027t use CODE IS in the RD. The program builds, but GnuCOBOL 3.2 writes an empty report file when it is given, and SP COBOL does the same. A report file assigned with ASSIGN TO DISPLAY prints on the screen, including the blank lines that pad the last page, as in GnuCOBOL. Not checked against GnuCOBOL yet, so use with care: PAGE LIMIT and the other PAGE numbers given by data items instead of integers, OCCURS in report lines, PRESENT AFTER / ABSENT AFTER. See also INITIATE , GENERATE , TERMINATE , SUPPRESS , USE , Files , WRITE for printing without the Report Writer, SORT to put records in control-field order."},{"t":"Intrinsic functions","u":"intrinsic-functions.html","s":"Built-in functions for text, arithmetic, dates, statistics and money, used as FUNCTION name (arguments).","k":"FUNCTION UPPER-CASE LOWER-CASE TRIM LENGTH REVERSE NUMVAL NUMVAL-C CONCATENATE SUBSTITUTE SQRT ABS MOD REM INTEGER RANDOM MAX MIN SUM MEAN MEDIAN ANNUITY PRESENT-VALUE CURRENT-DATE INTEGER-OF-DATE DATE-OF-INTEGER FORMATTED-DATE REPOSITORY FUNCTION ALL INTRINSIC built-in function","c":"Topics","x":"Syntax Format 1: call a function FUNCTION function-name [ ( argument-1 ... ) ] [ ( start : [ length ] ) ] Format 2: allow function names without the word FUNCTION ENVIRONMENT DIVISION. CONFIGURATION SECTION. REPOSITORY. FUNCTION ALL INTRINSIC. What it does An intrinsic function is a calculation built into COBOL. You write FUNCTION , the function\u0027s name and, in parentheses, its arguments : the values it works on, separated by spaces (commas are allowed too). The function returns a value, which you use where you would use a data item of that kind: in DISPLAY , MOVE , COMPUTE , IF and so on. MOVE FUNCTION UPPER-CASE(WS-NAME) TO WS-NAME COMPUTE WS-ROOT = FUNCTION SQRT(WS-AREA) IF FUNCTION MOD(WS-YEAR 4) = 0 ... Functions return either a number (numeric functions, used in arithmetic such as COMPUTE) or text (alphanumeric functions, used where text goes). A text result can be cut with reference modification, as in FUNCTION UPPER-CASE(WS-TEXT)(3:5) , and functions can be nested: FUNCTION LENGTH(FUNCTION TRIM(WS-TEXT)) . Functions that take \"a list of numbers\" (MAX, SUM, MEAN, ...) take any number of arguments. GnuCOBOL 3.2 doesn\u0027t accept a whole table as ITEM(ALL) , so list the items, or add them up in a PERFORM loop. Without the word FUNCTION FUNCTION ALL INTRINSIC in the REPOSITORY paragraph lets you leave out the word FUNCTION everywhere in the program: DISPLAY UPPER-CASE(WS-NAME) . The function names then become reserved words in that program, so you can\u0027t use them as data names (no item called SUM or LENGTH ). The functions Every function below works in SP COBOL and gives the same results as GnuCOBOL 3.2. Text Function Returns UPPER-CASE(text) the text with letters changed to capitals LOWER-CASE(text) the text with letters changed to small letters TRIM(text [LEADING | TRAILING]) the text without spaces at both ends (or only the front, or only the end) REVERSE(text) the characters in reverse order LENGTH(item) the number of characters (or bytes) in the item BYTE-LENGTH(item) the number of bytes the item takes in storage LENGTH-AN(item) the item\u0027s length in bytes, as for alphanumeric data STORED-CHAR-LENGTH(text) the length without trailing spaces CONCATENATE(text ...) / CONCAT all the arguments joined into one string SUBSTITUTE(text from to ...) the text with every \"from\" string replaced by its \"to\" string (pairs may repeat) SUBSTITUTE-CASE(text from to ...) the same, ignoring upper and lower case when finding \"from\" CHAR(n) the character at position n of the character set (counting from 1, so CHAR(66) is \"A\") ORD(char) the position of a character in the character set (ORD(\"A\") is 66) HEX-OF(item) the item\u0027s bytes written as hexadecimal digits HEX-TO-CHAR(text) the bytes that a string of hex digits stands for BIT-OF(item) the item\u0027s bytes written as 0s and 1s BIT-TO-CHAR(text) the bytes that a string of 0s and 1s stands for NUMVAL(text) the number written in the text, such as \" -12.5 \" or \"12.5CR\" NUMVAL-C(text [currency]) the same, also allowing a currency sign and thousands commas (\"$1,234.50\") NUMVAL-F(text) the number in a floating-point string such as \"1.5E+3\" TEST-NUMVAL(text), TEST-NUMVAL-C, TEST-NUMVAL-F 0 if the matching NUMVAL function would accept the text, otherwise the position of the first bad character LOCALE-COMPARE(text-1 text-2 [locale]) \"=\", \"\u003c\" or \"\u003e\" comparing the two texts by the rules of a language Numbers and mathematics Function Returns ABS(n) n without its sign SIGN(n) -1, 0 or 1 for a negative, zero or positive n INTEGER(n) the largest whole number not greater than n (INTEGER(-3.5) is -4) INTEGER-PART(n) n with its decimals dropped (INTEGER-PART(-3.5) is -3) FRACTION-PART(n) just the decimals of n, with n\u0027s sign MOD(a b) the remainder of a divided by b, with b\u0027s sign (MOD(-17 5) is 3) REM(a b) the remainder of a divided by b, with a\u0027s sign (REM(-17 5) is -2) SQRT(n) the square root FACTORIAL(n) 1 x 2 x ... x n EXP(n), EXP10(n) e to the power n, 10 to the power n LOG(n), LOG10(n) the natural logarithm, the base-10 logarithm SIN(n), COS(n), TAN(n) sine, cosine, tangent of an angle in radians ASIN(n), ACOS(n), ATAN(n) the angle (in radians) for a sine, cosine, tangent PI, E the constants π and e (no arguments) RANDOM [(seed)] a pseudo-random number from 0 up to (not including) 1; give a seed once to start a repeatable sequence HIGHEST-ALGEBRAIC(item), LOWEST-ALGEBRAIC(item) the largest and smallest value the numeric item can hold Statistics and money Function Returns MAX(n ...), MIN(n ...) the largest, the smallest argument (they work on text too) ORD-MAX(n ...), ORD-MIN(n ...) which argument (1, 2, ...) is the largest, the smallest SUM(n ...) the total MEAN(n ...) the average MEDIAN(n ...) the middle value once sorted (the average of the two middle ones for an even count) MIDRANGE(n ...) halfway between the smallest and largest RANGE(n ...) largest minus smallest VARIANCE(n ...), STANDARD-DEVIATION(n ...) how spread out the values are ANNUITY(rate periods) the payment per period that pays off a loan of 1 at that interest rate per period PRESENT-VALUE(rate amount ...) what a series of future amounts, one per period, is worth today Dates and times Many date functions use an integer date : a day number counted from 1 January 1601 (day 1). Turn two dates into day numbers and you can subtract them, or add days and turn the number back into a date. Function Returns CURRENT-DATE 21 characters: YYYYMMDDHHMMSSss, then the offset from UTC (+hhmm) INTEGER-OF-DATE(yyyymmdd) the day number of a date DATE-OF-INTEGER(n) the YYYYMMDD date of a day number INTEGER-OF-DAY(yyyyddd) the day number of a year-and-day-of-year date DAY-OF-INTEGER(n) the YYYYDDD date of a day number TEST-DATE-YYYYMMDD(n), TEST-DAY-YYYYDDD(n) 0 if the date is valid; 1, 2 or 3 if the year, month or day part is wrong DATE-TO-YYYYMMDD(yymmdd [window year]) a date with a 2-digit year expanded to 4 digits DAY-TO-YYYYDDD(yyddd [window year]) the same for YYDDD dates YEAR-TO-YYYY(yy [window year]) a 2-digit year expanded to 4 digits FORMATTED-DATE(format n) a day number as text in a format such as \"YYYY-MM-DD\" or \"YYYYDDD\" FORMATTED-TIME(format seconds [offset]) seconds past midnight as text in a format such as \"hh:mm:ss\" FORMATTED-DATETIME(format n seconds [offset]) a date and time together, in a format such as \"YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss\" FORMATTED-CURRENT-DATE(format) today\u0027s date and time in that format INTEGER-OF-FORMATTED-DATE(format text) the day number of a formatted date SECONDS-FROM-FORMATTED-TIME(format text) the seconds past midnight of a formatted time TEST-FORMATTED-DATETIME(format text) 0 if the text matches the format and is a valid date/time, otherwise the position of the problem SECONDS-PAST-MIDNIGHT the time now, in seconds since midnight COMBINED-DATETIME(n seconds) a day number and seconds as one number, n.sssss LOCALE-DATE(yyyymmdd [locale]), LOCALE-TIME(hhmmss [locale]), LOCALE-TIME-FROM-SECONDS(seconds [locale]) a date or time written the way the computer\u0027s language settings write it WHEN-COMPILED the date and time the program was compiled Other Function Returns CURRENCY-SYMBOL the currency sign of the computer\u0027s settings NUMERIC-DECIMAL-POINT, NUMERIC-THOUSANDS-SEPARATOR the decimal point and thousands separator of the computer\u0027s settings MONETARY-DECIMAL-POINT, MONETARY-THOUSANDS-SEPARATOR the same, for money amounts EXCEPTION-STATUS the name of the last exception condition (such as EC-SIZE-ZERO-DIVIDE), or spaces EXCEPTION-FILE the file status and file name of the last file error EXCEPTION-LOCATION where the last exception happened (program, paragraph, line) EXCEPTION-STATEMENT the statement that raised the last exception MODULE-ID, MODULE-CALLER-ID this program\u0027s name, and the name of the program that called it MODULE-SOURCE, MODULE-PATH the source file name, and the path of the running program MODULE-DATE, MODULE-TIME, MODULE-FORMATTED-DATE when this program was compiled CONTENT-LENGTH(pointer), CONTENT-OF(pointer [length]) the length of, and the text at, the data a pointer points to Examples Text functions UPPER-CASE and LOWER-CASE keep the field\u0027s 20 characters, spaces included; TRIM removes them. LENGTH of the field is 20, of the trimmed text 12. ORD(\"A\") is 66 because COBOL counts character positions from 1. The (3:5) after UPPER-CASE takes five characters starting at the third. NUMVAL-C reads \"1,234.50\" as a number that can be stored in a numeric field. Numeric functions Results go through COMPUTE into an edited field so they print readably. Compare MOD and REM, and INTEGER and INTEGER-PART, on negative numbers. Values displayed straight from a function (MAX, SUM, ...) are shown in a size the function chose. Date functions 25 November 2026 is day 155557 counted from 1 January 1601. Adding 30 and converting back gives 25 December. Because day 1 (1 January 1601) was a Monday, the day number MOD 7 gives the weekday with 0 for Sunday: 3 is Wednesday. TEST-DATE-YYYYMMDD returns 3 for 30 February: the day part is wrong. The example uses fixed dates so its output never changes; CURRENT-DATE gives today\u0027s. FUNCTION ALL INTRINSIC, and money With FUNCTION ALL INTRINSIC in the REPOSITORY, UPPER-CASE and LENGTH need no FUNCTION in front. ANNUITY gives the monthly payment on a loan of 1 at 0.5% a month for 12 months; times 10,000 that is 860.66. PRESENT-VALUE says three yearly payments of 100 are worth 272.32 today at 5% interest. SP COBOL notes Not implemented, in GnuCOBOL 3.2 or SP COBOL: BASECONVERT, BOOLEAN-OF-INTEGER, CHAR-NATIONAL, CONVERT, DISPLAY-OF, EXCEPTION-FILE-N, EXCEPTION-LOCATION-N, FIND-STRING, INTEGER-OF-BOOLEAN, MODULE-NAME, NATIONAL-OF and STANDARD-COMPARE. The compiler stops with \"FUNCTION \u0027name\u0027 is not implemented\". For FIND-STRING use INSPECT TALLYING, and for DISPLAY-OF / NATIONAL-OF a MOVE between PIC X and PIC N items. Known limits: EXCEPTION-STATUS reports the current exception rather than keeping the last one separately as GnuCOBOL does; EXCEPTION-STATEMENT always returns spaces; CONTENT-OF treats every pointer as NULL. Differs from GnuCOBOL 3.2: stock GnuCOBOL\u0027s SECONDS-FROM-FORMATTED-TIME can return a wrong number of decimals when the time has a fraction of a second, picking up a scale left over from another function. SP COBOL doesn\u0027t. Some odd stock behaviors are kept, so results match: TEST-NUMVAL-C rejects a currency string of more than one character, and SECONDS-PAST-MIDNIGHT ignores the COB_CURRENT_DATE setting. See also COMPUTE , INSPECT , STRING , ACCEPT (FROM DATE, TIME), special registers , ENVIRONMENT DIVISION (REPOSITORY)."},{"t":"Special registers","u":"special-registers.html","s":"Data items COBOL defines for you, such as RETURN-CODE, TALLY, LINAGE-COUNTER and LENGTH OF.","k":"RETURN-CODE SORT-RETURN TALLY LINAGE-COUNTER PAGE-COUNTER LINE-COUNTER XML-CODE JSON-CODE JSON-STATUS NUMBER-OF-CALL-PARAMETERS WHEN-COMPILED LENGTH OF ADDRESS OF DEBUG-ITEM COB-CRT-STATUS exit code","c":"Topics","x":"Syntax register-name LENGTH OF identifier-1 ADDRESS OF identifier-2 { PAGE-COUNTER | LINE-COUNTER } [ { OF | IN } report-name ] LINAGE-COUNTER [ { OF | IN } file-name ] What it does A special register is a data item that exists in every program without you declaring it. The compiler or the runtime fills it in, and you read it (and sometimes set it) like any other item. Don\u0027t define an item with one of these names. Register What it holds Defined as RETURN-CODE A number a program hands back. A called program sets it before GOBACK and the caller reads it after the CALL; when the main program ends, it becomes the program\u0027s exit code (what Windows sees as %ERRORLEVEL% ). binary, 9 digits, signed, starts at 0 SORT-RETURN 0 after a successful SORT or MERGE, 16 after a failed one. binary, signed TALLY A general counter, handy as the TALLYING item of INSPECT. PIC 9(5) binary, starts at 0 LINAGE-COUNTER For a file with a LINAGE clause: the line of the page the next WRITE goes to. numeric, one per such file PAGE-COUNTER , LINE-COUNTER For a Report Writer report: the page number, and the last line printed on the page. numeric, one per report NUMBER-OF-CALL-PARAMETERS In a called program: how many arguments the caller passed. binary, signed XML-CODE 0 after a successful XML GENERATE, or an error number. PIC S9(9) binary JSON-CODE 0 after a successful JSON GENERATE, or an error number (1: the receiving item was too small). PIC S9(9) binary COB-CRT-STATUS The key that ended the last screen ACCEPT, when the program has no CRT STATUS clause. PIC 9(4) WHEN-COMPILED The date and time the program was compiled, as text. PIC X(16) LENGTH OF item The number of bytes in the item, as a constant. binary ADDRESS OF item Where the item is in memory, as a pointer (see below). USAGE POINTER RETURN-CODE and the exit code Because RETURN-CODE becomes the exit code, a program that used it for something else should MOVE 0 TO RETURN-CODE before STOP RUN , or a batch file or build tool will think the program failed. STOP RUN RETURNING n sets the exit code on the way out; see STOP . ADDRESS OF ADDRESS OF is for passing data by its location: SET ptr TO ADDRESS OF item stores where an item is in a USAGE POINTER field, and SET ADDRESS OF linkage-item TO ptr makes a LINKAGE SECTION item refer to that place. Beginners rarely need it; CALL ... BY REFERENCE does the same job more safely. WHEN-COMPILED Its value changes every time you compile, so it isn\u0027t shown in an example here. DISPLAY WHEN-COMPILED prints something like 25/11/2604.59.02 (day/month/year, then the time, with no space between). Examples RETURN-CODE, TALLY, LENGTH OF, NUMBER-OF-CALL-PARAMETERS The called program SPREGS1 (in its own source file): PROCEDURE DIVISION USING LS-A LS-B. DISPLAY \" called with \" NUMBER-OF-CALL-PARAMETERS \" argument(s)\" IF NUMBER-OF-CALL-PARAMETERS = 2 COMPUTE RETURN-CODE = LS-A + LS-B ELSE MOVE LS-A TO RETURN-CODE END-IF GOBACK. TALLY counts the three A\u0027s in \"BANANA SPLIT\". SPREGS1 is called first with one argument, then with two; NUMBER-OF-CALL-PARAMETERS tells it which, and it hands back 7 and then 12 in RETURN-CODE. The main program sets RETURN-CODE to 0 at the end so it exits with code 0. LINAGE-COUNTER and JSON-CODE After each WRITE, LINAGE-COUNTER is the line the next WRITE will use. After the JSON GENERATE, JSON-CODE is 0: the text fitted in WS-JSON. With WS-JSON only 40 characters long, JSON-CODE would be 1. COB-CRT-STATUS A screen example (see SCREEN SECTION ): the keys were \"y\" and Esc. There is no CRT STATUS clause, so the Esc key\u0027s code, 2005, is in COB-CRT-STATUS. SP COBOL notes All of these behave as in GnuCOBOL 3.2. DEBUG-ITEM is filled in while a USE FOR DEBUGGING declarative runs, which happens only when the program runs with the environment variable COB_SET_DEBUG=Y , as in GnuCOBOL (see Debugging , which has an example). The registers used by XML PARSE (XML-EVENT, XML-TEXT, ...) are defined, but XML PARSE itself is not supported (see XML PARSE ). Special registers are not shown in the Visual Studio Watch window. See also CALL , STOP , INSPECT , Report Writer , JSON GENERATE , XML GENERATE , intrinsic functions ."},{"t":"Debugging","u":"debugging.html","s":"Find mistakes in SP COBOL programs with Visual Studio\u0027s debugger, plain-English messages, debugging lines and DISPLAY tracing.","k":"debug debugger Visual Studio F5 breakpoint step Watch window call stack WS_PAY COBOL[\"name\"] error message hint COB_NO_HINTS WITH DEBUGGING MODE D column 7 debugging line trace DISPLAY USE FOR DEBUGGING DEBUG-ITEM DEBUG-LINE DEBUG-NAME DEBUG-CONTENTS COB_SET_DEBUG declaratives libcob error stack trace","c":"Topics","x":"Syntax Format 1: debugging lines (compiled only in debugging mode) SOURCE-COMPUTER. computer-name WITH DEBUGGING MODE. D statement ... (D in column 7) Format 2: Watch window expressions (Visual Studio) DATA_NAME a data item, with _ for each - DATA_NAME[n] occurrence n of a table COBOL every 01 and 77 item COBOL[\"data-name\"] any item by its COBOL name Format 3: debugging declaratives (run only when COB_SET_DEBUG=Y) DECLARATIVES. section-name SECTION. USE FOR DEBUGGING ON { procedure-name | data-name | ALL PROCEDURES } ... . statement ... END DECLARATIVES. What it does A bug is a mistake that makes a program do the wrong thing. There are three kinds of help for finding one: the compiler\u0027s and runtime\u0027s error messages, Visual Studio\u0027s debugger, which lets you stop the program and look inside it, and extra DISPLAY statements that print what the program is doing as it runs. Error messages When a program doesn\u0027t compile, Visual Studio\u0027s Error List shows each message with its COBOL file and line; double-click one to jump there. For the mistakes beginners make most often, the message ends with a plain-English hint after -- , for example a syntax error suggests checking for a missing period, a misspelled word or code that starts before column 8. When a running program stops with a fatal error (a file that isn\u0027t there, a CALL of a program that doesn\u0027t exist), the runtime prints GnuCOBOL\u0027s message and then a line starting hint: that says what to check. Set the environment variable COB_NO_HINTS=1 to turn the hint lines off. Every runtime error that the program doesn\u0027t handle itself ends this way, with a libcob: error: line, a hint and exit code 1, not a .NET stack trace. That includes a subscript or reference modification that runs past the end of its data (see OCCURS ). Run the program under the debugger (F5) and Visual Studio stops on the COBOL statement that failed, before the message is printed. The Visual Studio debugger Press F5 to run the program under the debugger. Click in the margin next to a COBOL line to set a breakpoint : the program stops just before that line runs. Then step through the program one COBOL line at a time with Visual Studio\u0027s usual Step Over (F10) and Step Into (F11) commands. The Call Stack window shows the names of the paragraphs and sections being performed, because each paragraph becomes a method in the program SP COBOL builds. The Watch window While the program is stopped, type a data item\u0027s name into the Watch window to see its value. Write it in the debugger\u0027s style: - becomes _ , so WS-PAY is WS_PAY . The value is shown the way DISPLAY would show it, and the Type column shows the item\u0027s PICTURE and USAGE. For the example program below: Watch Shows WS_PAY 0712.50 , type PIC 9(4)V99 WS_TOTAL its value, type PIC S9(7)V99 COMP-3 CUSTOMER the whole group; expand it to see CUST-NAME and CUST-CITY MONTH_ENTRY[2] occurrence 2 of the table (expand MONTH_ENTRY to see them all) COBOL every 01 and 77 item of the program COBOL[\"ws-pay\"] any item by its COBOL name, in upper or lower case Some things to know: An item inside a table, such as MONTH_SALES , shows its first occurrence. Use the table\u0027s [n] for the others. If two items have the same name (in different groups, the ones you\u0027d qualify with OF ), only the first gets a Watch name. Reach the others by expanding their group, or through COBOL . LINKAGE SECTION items show \"(no data ...)\" until the program has been CALLed. Not shown: elementary FILLER items, level 66 (RENAMES), 88 (condition names) and 78 (constants), special registers such as RETURN-CODE, COB-CRT-STATUS, EXTERNAL items, LOCAL-STORAGE items and ANY LENGTH items. To see a condition name, watch the item it belongs to. Debugging lines A line with D in column 7 is a debugging line . It\u0027s compiled only when the SOURCE-COMPUTER paragraph says WITH DEBUGGING MODE ; otherwise it\u0027s treated as a comment. That lets you leave tracing DISPLAYs in a program and switch them all on or off by changing one line. USE FOR DEBUGGING A USE FOR DEBUGGING ON declarative names paragraphs, sections or data items to watch. Its code runs each time one of the paragraphs is PERFORMed or entered, and after each statement that changes one of the data items. While it runs, the special register DEBUG-ITEM says what happened: DEBUG-LINE is the source line, DEBUG-NAME the name, and DEBUG-CONTENTS the item\u0027s new value (for a paragraph, how it was reached, such as PERFORM LOOP ). A field of DEBUG-ITEM that an event doesn\u0027t set keeps its last value. The declarative needs WITH DEBUGGING MODE , and even then it stays silent unless the environment variable COB_SET_DEBUG is Y when the program runs, as in GnuCOBOL. So you can leave it in the program and switch it on only for a run where you need the trace. Type set COB_SET_DEBUG=Y in a Command Prompt window, then run the program from that same window. Tracing with DISPLAY The oldest way to debug still works everywhere: add DISPLAY statements that show where the program is and what its key fields hold, such as DISPLAY \"IN CALC-PAY: HOURS=\" WS-HOURS . Put square brackets around text fields ( DISPLAY \"[\" WS-NAME \"]\" ) to see trailing spaces. Examples A program to try the Watch window on Open it in a COBOL project, set a breakpoint on the DISPLAY line, press F5, and try the Watch expressions from the table above. By then the MOVEs have filled in the table, so MONTH_ENTRY[2] shows FEB and 200. Run without the debugger, it just prints DONE. Debugging lines With WITH DEBUGGING MODE the D line runs and traces each pass of the loop. Remove those three words and the program prints only the TOTAL line. A runtime error with a hint The file doesn\u0027t exist, so OPEN INPUT fails with status 35 and the program stops with exit code 1. The first message is GnuCOBOL\u0027s; the hint: line after it is SP COBOL\u0027s, and names the folder the program looked in (here, the folder the example was run in). USE FOR DEBUGGING The output above is from a normal run: COB_SET_DEBUG isn\u0027t set, so the declarative stays silent and only the TOTAL line appears. With COB_SET_DEBUG=Y the same program prints a line for every MOVE or ADD that changes TOTAL and every PERFORM of ADD-ONE: DEBUG: LINE 19 TOTAL [040 ] DEBUG: LINE 20 ADD-ONE [PERFORM LOOP] DEBUG: LINE 24 TOTAL [041 ] DEBUG: LINE 24 ADD-ONE [041 ] DEBUG: LINE 24 TOTAL [042 ] TOTAL 042 Line 19 is the MOVE and line 20 the PERFORM. On the second pass through ADD-ONE only DEBUG-NAME changes, so the line number and contents are the ones left by the ADD on line 24. GnuCOBOL 3.2 prints the same. SP COBOL notes Differs from GnuCOBOL 3.2: stock GnuCOBOL prints only the libcob: error line, without the hint. Its message also names the file as \u0027customers_dat\u0027 =\u003e customers.dat (the last environment variable it looked up); SP COBOL names it as written in ASSIGN. See also DISPLAY , source format (column 7), ENVIRONMENT DIVISION (SOURCE-COMPUTER), file status , Differences from GnuCOBOL ."},{"t":"Differences from GnuCOBOL","u":"differences.html","s":"Every place SP COBOL deliberately behaves differently from GnuCOBOL 3.2, and the GnuCOBOL quirks it keeps.","k":"GnuCOBOL 3.2 stock compatibility difference bug fix quirk deliberate cobc libcob","c":"Topics","x":"SP COBOL uses GnuCOBOL 3.2\u0027s compiler front end, and its runtime is written to give the same results as GnuCOBOL\u0027s: a test suite runs every sample program under both and compares the output and the data files byte for byte. So a program that works with GnuCOBOL 3.2 should work the same way with SP COBOL, and the other way round. The exceptions are listed here. Each one is deliberate: either GnuCOBOL has a plain bug, or its behavior would only confuse someone learning COBOL, or the COBOL standard says otherwise. Features neither of them supports are on Not supported . Deliberate differences Area GnuCOBOL 3.2 SP COBOL Page Screen colors An item with no color uses the terminal\u0027s default colors. It uses the colors of the last BLANK SCREEN. SCREEN SECTION Typing in a screen field Typing overwrites the old value in place. The first key typed replaces the old value, like a Windows text box. SCREEN SECTION Numeric screen fields No check when leaving the field. Checked when you leave the field, with plain-English messages, then shown again through the PICTURE. SCREEN SECTION DISPLAY of PIC N (national) data Writes the raw UTF-16 bytes, so N\"ABC\" prints with a NUL byte before each letter. Prints the characters (anything the console can\u0027t show as ? ). Storage, MOVE and comparisons are unchanged. National data TAB, form feed or ESC in a LINE SEQUENTIAL record Rejected with status 71. Written, as intended. Files A quoted name inside ASSIGN ( \"\u0027a.dat\u0027\" ) Keeps the first quote, fails with status 30. Quotes removed. Files $VAR in the middle of an ASSIGN path Swallows the next \\ . Separators kept. Files Reading LINE SEQUENTIAL files Windows text mode: Ctrl-Z ends the file, and LF-only files can lose text when a long line is split. CR LF and LF both end a line; Ctrl-Z is data (status 09 like other control bytes). Files LINE SEQUENTIAL REWRITE of a shorter record Overwrites the CR of the line end. The line end is kept. REWRITE REWRITE or DELETE of an empty RELATIVE slot Status 00 (REWRITE even stores the record). Status 23, record not found. File status READ PREVIOUS on a RELATIVE file, and READ NEXT after a random WRITE READ PREVIOUS can skip a record, and a READ NEXT after a random WRITE can hit end of file. As the standard says: WRITE doesn\u0027t move the file position, and PREVIOUS is the record just before. READ Sequential WRITE to a RELATIVE file whose record number doesn\u0027t fit the RELATIVE KEY Status 00: the record is written and the key set to 0. Status 24, and nothing is written. File status File error messages Name the last environment variable looked up ( \u0027missing_dat\u0027 ). Name the ASSIGN value ( \u0027missing.dat\u0027 ). File status Fatal runtime errors One libcob: error line. The same line, then a hint: line saying what to check ( COB_NO_HINTS=1 turns it off). Debugging A subscript, index or reference modification outside its data item Without -debug , quietly reads or overwrites the bytes after the item. Stops with libcob: error: a subscript, index or reference modification is outside its data item and a hint, as GnuCOBOL does when compiled with -debug . OCCURS , Referring to data ORGANIZATION INDEXED files Need an external ISAM library, with its own file format. Built in, in SP COBOL\u0027s own simple one-file format. Same file statuses, but the files can\u0027t be shared with GnuCOBOL programs. Files USE BEFORE REPORTING on a DETAIL group Each detail is added into the SUM counters twice, so totals are doubled. Added once. Report Writer Table SORT ( SORT table ON ... KEY ) Elements with equal keys may come out in any order (C\u0027s qsort). Equal elements keep their order (a stable sort). SORT SORT and MERGE files Records past a memory limit spill to temporary work files. All records are kept in memory. Same results. SORT FUNCTION SECONDS-FROM-FORMATTED-TIME with fractions of a second Can pick up the wrong number of decimals from another function\u0027s work area. Correct. Intrinsic functions NUMERIC class test on COMP-1 / COMP-2 True only for \"not a number\" or infinity (inverted). True for any ordinary floating-point value. Conditions XML PARSE Compiles; at run time sends one event and stops with \"XML PARSE is not implemented\". A compile error with the same message. XML PARSE The \"unfinished\" warning cobc warns that BY VALUE and PIC N handling are unfinished. SP COBOL projects compile with -Wno-unfinished , since its runtime handles both. CALL , National data Kept on purpose, although surprising These GnuCOBOL 3.2 behaviors look odd, but SP COBOL keeps them so that programs give the same results in both: Report Writer: PRESENT WHEN on a single field never prints the field ( Report Writer ). On a LINE it works. UNSTRING into a JUSTIFIED RIGHT item fills it from the left ( UNSTRING ). MOVE does justify. XML and JSON GENERATE output only the first occurrence of an OCCURS item, put the raw bytes of a number into an XML attribute ( -123.45 becomes 001234u ), and stop the program on COMP-1 / COMP-2 items ( XML GENERATE , JSON GENERATE ). A READ after the end of a file gives status 46, and goes to a USE AFTER ERROR procedure even when the READ has an AT END phrase ( USE ). Files: lines end with CR LF on Windows; CLOSE ... REEL gives status 07; a LINAGE file pads pages with blank lines, not form feeds; an \"implicit CLOSE\" warning appears when STOP RUN finds a file still open; the program ends with exit code 1 after a fatal file error ( Files ). DISPLAY of a COMP-5 item shows as many digits as its size can hold (5 for 2 bytes, 10 for 4, 20 for 8), whatever its PICTURE says ( USAGE ). Intrinsic functions: TEST-NUMVAL-C rejects a currency string longer than one character; EXCEPTION-FILE treats any exception in the 05xx group as a file error; short negative UTC offsets print as +00mm ; COMBINED-DATETIME overflows for very large day numbers; SECONDS-PAST-MIDNIGHT ignores COB_CURRENT_DATE ( Intrinsic functions ). Not implemented in either: PROCEDURE DIVISION RETURNING and the intrinsic functions listed on Not supported . See also Not supported , Debugging , Reading this reference ."},{"t":"Not supported","u":"not-supported.html","s":"COBOL features SP COBOL does not support, and what to write instead.","k":"not supported not implemented PROCEDURE DIVISION RETURNING XML PARSE CALL RETURNING ADDRESS SET ENTRY program pointer CALL STATIC CODE IS limitation workaround","c":"Topics","x":"SP COBOL compiles the same language as GnuCOBOL 3.2. This page lists what it doesn\u0027t support, mostly because GnuCOBOL 3.2 doesn\u0027t either, and says what to do instead. Where SP COBOL works but deliberately gives a different result, see Differences from GnuCOBOL . Calling programs PROCEDURE DIVISION RETURNING : a called program can\u0027t return a value this way. The compiler warns \"program RETURNING is not implemented\", as GnuCOBOL 3.2 does. Instead, pass the result back in an argument passed BY REFERENCE , or a status number in RETURN-CODE ; the caller can copy RETURN-CODE into a field with CALL ... RETURNING field . See the example below. CALL ... RETURNING ADDRESS OF , program pointers ( SET proc-pointer TO ENTRY \"name\" and calling through one), and CALL STATIC are not done. Call programs by name with CALL \"NAME\" or CALL identifier , where the identifier holds the name; see CALL . XML and JSON XML PARSE is a compile error: \"XML PARSE is not implemented (GnuCOBOL 3.2 doesn\u0027t implement it either)\". Read XML text with UNSTRING and INSPECT , or in a C# part of the project. See XML PARSE . Tables in XML GENERATE and JSON GENERATE : only the first occurrence of an OCCURS item is written (the compiler warns \"not implemented\"). Generate one occurrence at a time from a group that holds a single copy, or build the text with STRING . COMP-1 and COMP-2 items in XML GENERATE and JSON GENERATE stop the program. MOVE the value to a DISPLAY item (such as PIC -9(7).9(4) ) and generate from that instead. Intrinsic functions BASECONVERT, BOOLEAN-OF-INTEGER, CHAR-NATIONAL, CONVERT, DISPLAY-OF, EXCEPTION-FILE-N, EXCEPTION-LOCATION-N, FIND-STRING, INTEGER-OF-BOOLEAN, MODULE-NAME, NATIONAL-OF and STANDARD-COMPARE are not implemented: the compiler stops with \"FUNCTION \u0027name\u0027 is not implemented\". Use EXCEPTION-FILE and EXCEPTION-LOCATION in place of the -N forms, INSPECT in place of FIND-STRING, and a MOVE between PIC X and PIC N items in place of DISPLAY-OF and NATIONAL-OF. Among the functions that do work, EXCEPTION-STATEMENT always returns spaces and CONTENT-OF treats every pointer as NULL. See Intrinsic functions . Reports CODE IS in a Report Writer RD compiles, but the report comes out empty, in GnuCOBOL 3.2 and in SP COBOL alike. Leave it out; to tell several reports apart, give each its own file. See Report Writer . Screens The screen attributes LOWLIGHT , BLINK , BELL and FULL are accepted but do nothing, and the TIMEOUT , SCROLL , SIZE , CONTROL and CURSOR phrases of ACCEPT/DISPLAY at a position are ignored. Laying out a screen in Visual Studio\u0027s Windows Forms designer is planned, not available: write the SCREEN SECTION. See SCREEN SECTION . Files INDEXED files work, but in SP COBOL\u0027s own file format, so they can\u0027t be read by GnuCOBOL programs (or the other way round). Sequential, line sequential and relative files are the same on disk in both. See Files . Example: returning a result without RETURNING The called program, NOTSUPS1, adds 8% tax. The price goes in BY CONTENT, so NOTSUPS1 can\u0027t change it, and the answer comes back in WS-WITH-TAX, passed BY REFERENCE: 80.00 plus 8% is 86.40. RETURN-CODE carries a status: 0 the first time. The second call\u0027s result doesn\u0027t fit in PIC 9(4)V99, so NOTSUPS1 sets RETURN-CODE to 8, and RETURNING WS-STATUS copies it into WS-STATUS. See also Differences from GnuCOBOL , CALL , special registers ."}];
